const LESSONS = [{"id": "MA02", "slug": "recalling", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Return to base for recovery and purchases while weighing the cost of leaving.", "title": "Recalling", "file": "MA02-recalling.md", "meta": {"roles": "Top, Mid and Bot; beginner. Jungle and Support can use the recovery and destination checks without assuming responsibility for a lane wave.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Wave crashing, Resource management.", "action": "Before staying for another wave action, name the threat that could stop your recall."}, "aliases": ["choose-a-safe-recall-window"], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Recalling is the action that returns your champion to base. Recall timing is the decision about when and where to begin it; a reset includes recovery, purchases and returning to a useful job. The wave and nearby threats shape that decision.<\/p><p>Improve the wave before recalling when you can do it safely. If completing the push would expose you to likely death or prevent needed recovery, accept the imperfect wave and leave. A good recall can still cost minions.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Understand the window and make the decision<\/h2><p>A <strong>recall<\/strong> returns you to base; a <strong>reset<\/strong> is the wider job of recovering, buying and returning to useful play. A <strong>wave<\/strong> is a group of lane minions. To <strong>hold<\/strong> is to limit how quickly its position changes; to <strong>build<\/strong> is to let allied minions accumulate; to <strong>clear<\/strong> is to kill the opposing minions. A <strong>crash<\/strong> happens when your wave reaches the enemy turret. Clearing the current enemies does not by itself prove that a crash will finish before reinforcements arrive.<\/p><p>Pushing can support a recall, but feasibility matters. Mobalytics explicitly asks about clear speed, available time and wave size. Riot gives a champion-specific shove-to-recall example for Hwei and warns about exposed positioning. Neither establishes a universal safe recipe. <a href=\"https://mobalytics.gg/lol/guides/wave-management\">Mobalytics, “The Fast Push”<\/a>; <a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/event/hwei-abilities-rundown/\">Riot, “Laning Phase”<\/a>.<\/p><p>Use this decision sequence, an editorial synthesis of those conditions:<\/p><ol><li><strong>Name the reset&#x27;s purpose.<\/strong> Are you recovering health or mana, making a useful purchase, or preparing for the next job? Resources include health, mana or your champion&#x27;s equivalent, and abilities available to defend yourself. Count what would remain after clearing.<\/li><li><strong>Inspect what finishing requires.<\/strong> Look at the current wave and approaching minions. Can your champion finish without spending the tools needed to escape? If you cannot judge a safe crash, do not invent an exact minion recipe.<\/li><li><strong>Check the threat to staying.<\/strong> Where is the lane opponent? What could their return change? A jungler&#x27;s last sighting is evidence from that moment; missing enemies may still approach you.<\/li><li><strong>Choose and stop.<\/strong> Crash and leave, retreat now, or make one brief safe improvement and reassess. Extra turret attacks extend the commitment. Once recovered, choose your destination from the new map state.<\/li><\/ol><p>Staying may preserve farm, but it also delays recovery and your return. In solo queue, count cover only when an ally is actually providing it. In coordinated play, confirm who will protect the push. Enemy interference, return mobility and weak waveclear can remove the window.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>You are mid, your opponent has left, and the enemy jungler is currently visible far from your lane. You have enough health and resources to finish the wave and watch it reach the turret. You then retreat to recall instead of staying for turret damage.<\/p><p>Change the situation: you are nearly dead, clearing would spend your escape tool, and the jungler is missing. You leave immediately. The unfinished wave is a cost you accept; its appearance does not make staying safe. Even the first situation does not guarantee zero lost farm.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma02-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma02-desktop.svg\" alt=\"With enough resources, a jungler visible elsewhere and a feasible crash, finish the crash then leave. When nearly dead and clearing would spend the escape tool while the jungler is missing, leave with an imperfect wave.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>The same intended reset can require a different choice when resources and threats change.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><p>Figure files: <a href=\"assets/ma02-desktop.svg\">desktop SVG<\/a> · <a href=\"assets/ma02-mobile.svg\">mobile SVG<\/a> · <a href=\"assets/ma02-desktop.png\">desktop PNG<\/a> · <a href=\"assets/ma02-mobile.png\">mobile PNG<\/a>.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> “Push before every back.” You keep clearing because leaving an awkward wave feels wrong.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Ask whether you can finish safely with the information available. A wave improvement is useful only if its cost is acceptable. If the threat changes, abandon the original push.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Review three recalls from one game. Pause before each decision and write one sentence naming the wave state, a threat and the cost you accepted. Then reveal the outcome. Check whether all three decisions included a safety reason; record lost farm separately from the quality of the information you used.<\/p><p>This is a proposed teaching drill, not a validated improvement method or rank benchmark. An unlucky outcome does not prove the original decision was unreasonable.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/wave-crashing\">Wave crashing<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/resource-management\">Resource management<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/itemization\">Itemization<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p>Reviewed source passages above support a conditional recall opportunity. The safety concession and decision sequence are editorial synthesis. No fixed recall timing, guaranteed bounce, current quest reward or universal turret recipe is taught. No client or replay experiment validates this hypothetical example.<\/p><p>Last independently reviewed and sources accessed: <strong>3 November 2026 Pacific / 4 November 2026 UTC<\/strong>. Source dates below are historical publication/update dates, not live-patch certification.<\/p><ul><li>Mobalytics / displayed Mobalytics; updated 15 Dec 2025.<\/li><li>Riot Games / Myles “Riot Emizery” Salholm; publication date not displayed.<\/li><\/ul><p>Editorial revision: concept scope updated for LEA-24 on 3 November 2026 Pacific. Prior review dates above refer to the inherited research package; revised prose received an integration review, not a new independent expert review. Examples and drills remain illustrative.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Recalling\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA02\n- Slug: recalling\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: Top, Mid and Bot; beginner. Jungle and Support can use the recovery and destination checks without assuming responsibility for a lane wave.\n- Summary: Return to base for recovery and purchases while weighing the cost of leaving.\n- Prerequisites: Wave crashing, Resource management.\n- Observable action: Before staying for another wave action, name the threat that could stop your recall.\n\n## Short answer\n\nRecalling is the action that returns your champion to base. Recall timing is the decision about when and where to begin it; a reset includes recovery, purchases and returning to a useful job. The wave and nearby threats shape that decision.\n\nImprove the wave before recalling when you can do it safely. If completing the push would expose you to likely death or prevent needed recovery, accept the imperfect wave and leave. A good recall can still cost minions.\n\n## Understand the window and make the decision\n\nA **recall** returns you to base; a **reset** is the wider job of recovering, buying and returning to useful play. A **wave** is a group of lane minions. To **hold** is to limit how quickly its position changes; to **build** is to let allied minions accumulate; to **clear** is to kill the opposing minions. A **crash** happens when your wave reaches the enemy turret. Clearing the current enemies does not by itself prove that a crash will finish before reinforcements arrive.\n\nPushing can support a recall, but feasibility matters. Mobalytics explicitly asks about clear speed, available time and wave size. Riot gives a champion-specific shove-to-recall example for Hwei and warns about exposed positioning. Neither establishes a universal safe recipe. [Mobalytics, “The Fast Push”](https://mobalytics.gg/lol/guides/wave-management); [Riot, “Laning Phase”](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/event/hwei-abilities-rundown/).\n\nUse this decision sequence, an editorial synthesis of those conditions:\n\n1. **Name the reset's purpose.** Are you recovering health or mana, making a useful purchase, or preparing for the next job? Resources include health, mana or your champion's equivalent, and abilities available to defend yourself. Count what would remain after clearing.\n2. **Inspect what finishing requires.** Look at the current wave and approaching minions. Can your champion finish without spending the tools needed to escape? If you cannot judge a safe crash, do not invent an exact minion recipe.\n3. **Check the threat to staying.** Where is the lane opponent? What could their return change? A jungler's last sighting is evidence from that moment; missing enemies may still approach you.\n4. **Choose and stop.** Crash and leave, retreat now, or make one brief safe improvement and reassess. Extra turret attacks extend the commitment. Once recovered, choose your destination from the new map state.\n\nStaying may preserve farm, but it also delays recovery and your return. In solo queue, count cover only when an ally is actually providing it. In coordinated play, confirm who will protect the push. Enemy interference, return mobility and weak waveclear can remove the window.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYou are mid, your opponent has left, and the enemy jungler is currently visible far from your lane. You have enough health and resources to finish the wave and watch it reach the turret. You then retreat to recall instead of staying for turret damage.\n\nChange the situation: you are nearly dead, clearing would spend your escape tool, and the jungler is missing. You leave immediately. The unfinished wave is a cost you accept; its appearance does not make staying safe. Even the first situation does not guarantee zero lost farm.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA02; use one width variant at a time. -->\n![With enough resources, a jungler visible elsewhere and a feasible crash, finish the crash then leave. When nearly dead and clearing would spend the escape tool while the jungler is missing, leave with an imperfect wave.](../visuals/previews/ma02-mobile.png)\n\n*The same intended reset can require a different choice when resources and threats change.*\n\nFigure files: [desktop SVG](../visuals/assets/ma02-desktop.svg) · [mobile SVG](../visuals/assets/ma02-mobile.svg) · [desktop PNG](../visuals/previews/ma02-desktop.png) · [mobile PNG](../visuals/previews/ma02-mobile.png).\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** “Push before every back.” You keep clearing because leaving an awkward wave feels wrong.\n\n**Correction:** Ask whether you can finish safely with the information available. A wave improvement is useful only if its cost is acceptable. If the threat changes, abandon the original push.\n\n## Practice\n\nReview three recalls from one game. Pause before each decision and write one sentence naming the wave state, a threat and the cost you accepted. Then reveal the outcome. Check whether all three decisions included a safety reason; record lost farm separately from the quality of the information you used.\n\nThis is a proposed teaching drill, not a validated improvement method or rank benchmark. An unlucky outcome does not prove the original decision was unreasonable.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Wave crashing](../macro/MA16-wave-crashing.md) · [Resource management](../micro/MI16-resource-management.md) · [Itemization](../macro/MA21-itemization.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\nReviewed source passages above support a conditional recall opportunity. The safety concession and decision sequence are editorial synthesis. No fixed recall timing, guaranteed bounce, current quest reward or universal turret recipe is taught. No client or replay experiment validates this hypothetical example.\n\nLast independently reviewed and sources accessed: **3 November 2026 Pacific / 4 November 2026 UTC**. Source dates below are historical publication/update dates, not live-patch certification.\n\n- Mobalytics / displayed Mobalytics; updated 15 Dec 2025.\n- Riot Games / Myles “Riot Emizery” Salholm; publication date not displayed.\n\nEditorial revision: concept scope updated for LEA-24 on 3 November 2026 Pacific. Prior review dates above refer to the inherited research package; revised prose received an integration review, not a new independent expert review. Examples and drills remain illustrative.\n", "minutes": 4, "sha256": "30cfd46a000685aada70042408bee867df0c019cc6c61a74920885f5b231c4d4"}, {"id": "MA04", "slug": "lane-priority", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Understand who can move first from lane and what that opportunity can support.", "title": "Lane priority", "file": "MA04-lane-priority.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner, especially Jungle, Mid and Support.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Wave crashing, Recalling, Map awareness.", "action": "Before entering a contest, identify the first ally who can realistically help."}, "aliases": ["check-whether-lane-priority-is-usable"], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Lane priority is a relative opportunity to act or move before the opposing laner. Wave position can help create it, but resources, routes and actual movement decide whether it supports the next play.<\/p><p>A pushed lane can create an opportunity to move. Check the player&#x27;s wave duty, resources, route and actual movement before relying on that opportunity. Priority alone does not establish a favorable fight.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Understand priority and check availability<\/h2><p><strong>Lane priority<\/strong> describes a relative opportunity to act or move before the opposing laner. It is not a displayed score. A <strong>push<\/strong> moves allied minions toward the enemy side; a <strong>crash<\/strong> brings them to the enemy turret. <strong>Holding<\/strong> limits wave movement, <strong>building<\/strong> lets allied minions accumulate, and <strong>clearing<\/strong> removes opposing minions. These choices affect what a laner gives up by leaving.<\/p><p>Dignitas connects pushing with an earlier move. Riot&#x27;s objective design discussion describes lane control providing cover for junglers. These support the opportunity, while Tomo&#x27;s coaching emphasizes adapting to teammate intentions and game state. <a href=\"https://dignitas.gg/articles/the-art-of-roaming-in-league-of-legends\">Dignitas, “When Do You Roam?”<\/a>; <a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-gb/news/game-updates/patch-26-1-notes/\">Riot, “Epic Objective Tuning”<\/a>; <a href=\"https://dignitas.gg/articles/adc-mindset-guide-with-dig-tomo\">Dignitas, “What Can You Get Away With in 2v2?”<\/a>.<\/p><p>To decide whether that priority can support the play you are considering, check it in this order:<\/p><ol><li><strong>Identify the lane duty.<\/strong> Is your ally collecting a wave, pushing, defending a turret or already leaving? A wave under the enemy turret may reduce a reason to stay, but the enemy can still abandon farm to move.<\/li><li><strong>Check readiness.<\/strong> Look at health, mana or equivalent resources, and known available abilities. A nearby ally who cannot survive the approach may offer little practical help. Compare what the player can do after joining, not just whether they can walk there.<\/li><li><strong>Trace both arrival routes.<\/strong> Which ally and enemy can reach the play first? Consider obstructions, dangerous entrances and observed movement. Do not count an unseen enemy as absent.<\/li><li><strong>Use actual behavior.<\/strong> A ping requests help; it does not confirm it. If the ally recalls, stays to farm or takes another route, revise the play before crossing into danger.<\/li><\/ol><p>Movement spends lane income, health and map presence. Solo queue calls should adapt to the players visibly available. Coordinated teams can confirm who moves and who covers the abandoned duty. Even confirmed help does not settle combat strength, enemy reinforcements or escape safety.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>You are the jungler considering a river contest. Your mid laner has pushed a wave toward the enemy turret, so you expect help. Looking again, you see your ally retreating to recall while the enemy mid laner walks toward river.<\/p><p>Your ally&#x27;s wave position created an opportunity they chose to spend on recovery. You stop at a safe entrance instead of entering the contest as though mid were beside you. If your ally later returns ready to move, you assess the new situation; the earlier push is no longer enough information.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma04-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma04-desktop.svg\" alt=\"A wave is pushed near the enemy turret, but allied mid recalls while enemy mid moves toward river. The jungler pauses at a safe entrance because the opportunity is not available support.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>The pushed wave creates an opportunity; the ally’s recall means that opportunity is not river support.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><p>Figure files: <a href=\"assets/ma04-desktop.svg\">desktop SVG<\/a> · <a href=\"assets/ma04-mobile.svg\">mobile SVG<\/a> · <a href=\"assets/ma04-desktop.png\">desktop PNG<\/a> · <a href=\"assets/ma04-mobile.png\">mobile PNG<\/a>.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Counting two pushed lanes as two guaranteed teammates in a fight.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Name the actual first arrival and a reason each nearby player might stay. If support depends on someone changing their current action, treat it as unconfirmed and preserve an exit.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Before three river entries in a replay, pause in player view. Predict the first reinforcements and write why one nearby ally might remain in lane. Then reveal movement and record whether the available information should have changed your entry.<\/p><p>The observable check is three explicit arrival assessments, including any required adjustment. Prediction accuracy is descriptive; this teaching proposal has no validated target and does not promise contest wins.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/wave-crashing\">Wave crashing<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/recalling\">Recalling<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/map-awareness\">Map awareness<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/weak-side\">Weak side<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p>The cited passages support movement opportunity, lane cover and adaptation. The four checks and “usable priority” definition are editorial synthesis. Riot&#x27;s dated notes supply official design context, not a complete current reward table. Enemy choices, unseen movement and changing resources limit every prediction. No exact arrival timer or fight-win claim is supplied.<\/p><p>Last independently reviewed and sources accessed: <strong>3 November 2026 Pacific / 4 November 2026 UTC<\/strong>. Source dates below are historical publication/update dates, not live-patch certification.<\/p><ul><li>Dignitas / Log; 16 Jan 2020.<\/li><li>Riot Games / Riru, Sakaar; 7 Jan 2026.<\/li><li>Dignitas / Myodo interviewing Tomo; 7 Dec 2025.<\/li><\/ul><p>Editorial revision: concept scope updated for LEA-24 on 3 November 2026 Pacific. Prior review dates above refer to the inherited research package; revised prose received an integration review, not a new independent expert review. Examples and drills remain illustrative.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Lane priority\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA04\n- Slug: lane-priority\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner, especially Jungle, Mid and Support.\n- Summary: Understand who can move first from lane and what that opportunity can support.\n- Prerequisites: Wave crashing, Recalling, Map awareness.\n- Observable action: Before entering a contest, identify the first ally who can realistically help.\n\n## Short answer\n\nLane priority is a relative opportunity to act or move before the opposing laner. Wave position can help create it, but resources, routes and actual movement decide whether it supports the next play.\n\nA pushed lane can create an opportunity to move. Check the player's wave duty, resources, route and actual movement before relying on that opportunity. Priority alone does not establish a favorable fight.\n\n## Understand priority and check availability\n\n**Lane priority** describes a relative opportunity to act or move before the opposing laner. It is not a displayed score. A **push** moves allied minions toward the enemy side; a **crash** brings them to the enemy turret. **Holding** limits wave movement, **building** lets allied minions accumulate, and **clearing** removes opposing minions. These choices affect what a laner gives up by leaving.\n\nDignitas connects pushing with an earlier move. Riot's objective design discussion describes lane control providing cover for junglers. These support the opportunity, while Tomo's coaching emphasizes adapting to teammate intentions and game state. [Dignitas, “When Do You Roam?”](https://dignitas.gg/articles/the-art-of-roaming-in-league-of-legends); [Riot, “Epic Objective Tuning”](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-gb/news/game-updates/patch-26-1-notes/); [Dignitas, “What Can You Get Away With in 2v2?”](https://dignitas.gg/articles/adc-mindset-guide-with-dig-tomo).\n\nTo decide whether that priority can support the play you are considering, check it in this order:\n\n1. **Identify the lane duty.** Is your ally collecting a wave, pushing, defending a turret or already leaving? A wave under the enemy turret may reduce a reason to stay, but the enemy can still abandon farm to move.\n2. **Check readiness.** Look at health, mana or equivalent resources, and known available abilities. A nearby ally who cannot survive the approach may offer little practical help. Compare what the player can do after joining, not just whether they can walk there.\n3. **Trace both arrival routes.** Which ally and enemy can reach the play first? Consider obstructions, dangerous entrances and observed movement. Do not count an unseen enemy as absent.\n4. **Use actual behavior.** A ping requests help; it does not confirm it. If the ally recalls, stays to farm or takes another route, revise the play before crossing into danger.\n\nMovement spends lane income, health and map presence. Solo queue calls should adapt to the players visibly available. Coordinated teams can confirm who moves and who covers the abandoned duty. Even confirmed help does not settle combat strength, enemy reinforcements or escape safety.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYou are the jungler considering a river contest. Your mid laner has pushed a wave toward the enemy turret, so you expect help. Looking again, you see your ally retreating to recall while the enemy mid laner walks toward river.\n\nYour ally's wave position created an opportunity they chose to spend on recovery. You stop at a safe entrance instead of entering the contest as though mid were beside you. If your ally later returns ready to move, you assess the new situation; the earlier push is no longer enough information.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA04; use one width variant at a time. -->\n![A wave is pushed near the enemy turret, but allied mid recalls while enemy mid moves toward river. The jungler pauses at a safe entrance because the opportunity is not available support.](../visuals/previews/ma04-mobile.png)\n\n*The pushed wave creates an opportunity; the ally’s recall means that opportunity is not river support.*\n\nFigure files: [desktop SVG](../visuals/assets/ma04-desktop.svg) · [mobile SVG](../visuals/assets/ma04-mobile.svg) · [desktop PNG](../visuals/previews/ma04-desktop.png) · [mobile PNG](../visuals/previews/ma04-mobile.png).\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Counting two pushed lanes as two guaranteed teammates in a fight.\n\n**Correction:** Name the actual first arrival and a reason each nearby player might stay. If support depends on someone changing their current action, treat it as unconfirmed and preserve an exit.\n\n## Practice\n\nBefore three river entries in a replay, pause in player view. Predict the first reinforcements and write why one nearby ally might remain in lane. Then reveal movement and record whether the available information should have changed your entry.\n\nThe observable check is three explicit arrival assessments, including any required adjustment. Prediction accuracy is descriptive; this teaching proposal has no validated target and does not promise contest wins.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Wave crashing](../macro/MA16-wave-crashing.md) · [Recalling](../macro/MA02-recalling.md) · [Map awareness](../macro/MA39-map-awareness.md) · [Weak side](../macro/MA37-weak-side.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\nThe cited passages support movement opportunity, lane cover and adaptation. The four checks and “usable priority” definition are editorial synthesis. Riot's dated notes supply official design context, not a complete current reward table. Enemy choices, unseen movement and changing resources limit every prediction. No exact arrival timer or fight-win claim is supplied.\n\nLast independently reviewed and sources accessed: **3 November 2026 Pacific / 4 November 2026 UTC**. Source dates below are historical publication/update dates, not live-patch certification.\n\n- Dignitas / Log; 16 Jan 2020.\n- Riot Games / Riru, Sakaar; 7 Jan 2026.\n- Dignitas / Myodo interviewing Tomo; 7 Dec 2025.\n\nEditorial revision: concept scope updated for LEA-24 on 3 November 2026 Pacific. Prior review dates above refer to the inherited research package; revised prose received an integration review, not a new independent expert review. Examples and drills remain illustrative.\n", "minutes": 4, "sha256": "854759436da9d02c0988794b65b41c43466d78592a57ff48e90bcbbda097895d"}, {"id": "MA05", "slug": "warding", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Place vision where it can inform a decision, with safe access and a response.", "title": "Warding", "file": "MA05-warding.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner, with Support and Jungle emphasis.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Map awareness.", "action": "Before placing a ward, say what decision its information will change."}, "aliases": ["place-a-ward-to-answer-your-next-question"], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Warding is placing wards to gain information about areas of the map. Useful coverage depends on what you need to know, when you need it, and whether you can reach the spot safely. This introduction covers placement decisions, not every ward type or detection interaction.<\/p><p>Name your next question, choose safely reachable coverage that helps answer it, and decide how you will respond. One ward can inform a decision while leaving other approaches unknown.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Connect coverage to a decision<\/h2><p><strong>Fog of war<\/strong> is the area your team cannot currently see. A ward provides <strong>coverage<\/strong>: information about part of the map under the game&#x27;s vision rules. A <strong>route<\/strong> is an approach an enemy might use; a <strong>choke<\/strong> is a narrow passage. Seeing one route does not establish what is happening along every other route.<\/p><p>Biofrost&#x27;s warding advice makes depth conditional on pressure and assistance, and warns against risking life for late objective vision. Isles&#x27; coaching connects map movement with useful information and time. <a href=\"https://dignitas.gg/articles/pro-level-warding-guide-with-dignitas-biofrost\">Dignitas, “Warding Around the Mid” and “Warding Around Dragon”<\/a>; <a href=\"https://dignitas.gg/articles/five-important-support-tricks-and-advice-with-isles\">Dignitas, section 3, “Managing your time around the map wisely”<\/a>.<\/p><p>The question-first process below is editorial synthesis:<\/p><ol><li><strong>State the imminent decision.<\/strong> “Can I keep collecting this side wave?” is more actionable than “I need more vision.” Choose a question that matters before your next move.<\/li><li><strong>Pick a relevant approach.<\/strong> Ask where a warning would help you change that move. Consider the time and space your champion needs to retreat, without assuming a universal warning distance.<\/li><li><strong>Check access before depth.<\/strong> Can you reach the spot without crossing an unchecked entrance? Count visible allies who can help, not an escort you hope will appear. Use closer useful coverage, wait for help or reduce the depth of your intended play when access is unsafe.<\/li><li><strong>Attach a response.<\/strong> Decide what seeing an enemy would change. Also name an uncovered approach. Reassess when the ward is removed, coverage ends, or your next job changes.<\/li><\/ol><p>Placement spends a ward, travel time and presence elsewhere. An empty covered route can still be useful information, but only within its coverage and the period you observed. Enemy mobility, stealth and alternate paths limit what that absence tells you. This lesson supplies no universal ward location or detection rule for those abilities.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>You want to collect a side wave. A reachable ward can watch an approach toward your retreat path. Your question is, “Will someone approach along this route while I collect?” Your planned response is to leave when a threat appears.<\/p><p>A second route remains uncovered. You collect only the portion of the wave that leaves an acceptable exit instead of treating the ward as permission to advance indefinitely. If placing the ward itself requires walking through an unknown choke, you wait or choose a closer location. The ideal-looking spot is not worth assuming safe access.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma05-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma05-desktop.svg\" alt=\"An abstract watched approach and a second unknown approach converge toward a side wave. A ward answers only the first-route question; the player keeps an exit and plans to leave when a threat appears.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>A useful warning leaves other routes unknown.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><p>Figure files: <a href=\"assets/ma05-desktop.svg\">desktop SVG<\/a> · <a href=\"assets/ma05-mobile.svg\">mobile SVG<\/a> · <a href=\"assets/ma05-desktop.png\">desktop PNG<\/a> · <a href=\"assets/ma05-mobile.png\">mobile PNG<\/a>.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> “I warded, so this area is safe.” You continue forward without checking what the ward can reveal.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Complete the sentence: “This watches ___; ___ remains unknown; I will ___ if the information changes.” A useful ward answers a bounded question. It does not remove every risk.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>For five wards in a replay, record the question and planned response before revealing later events. Check whether the coverage mattered while the decision was still active. Review at least one response to new information or ended coverage; if none occurred, note that and use another sample.<\/p><p>This proposed drill measures whether you gave the ward a purpose and used the result. It has no vision-score quota, validated success rate or requirement that an enemy cross the ward.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/map-awareness\">Map awareness<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/vision-denial\">Vision denial<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p>Both coaching sources share the Dignitas publisher; they do not establish independent-publisher consensus. Exact current coverage, ward duration and historical placement diagrams are excluded. Any later precise placement illustration needs a current client check. The proposed question-and-response framework has not been tested for teaching efficacy.<\/p><p>Last independently reviewed and sources accessed: <strong>3 November 2026 Pacific / 4 November 2026 UTC<\/strong>. Source dates below are historical publication/update dates, not live-patch certification.<\/p><ul><li>Dignitas / Teatones interviewing Biofrost; 15 May 2022.<\/li><li>Dignitas / Myodo interviewing Isles; 14 Aug 2025.<\/li><\/ul><p>Editorial revision: concept scope updated for LEA-24 on 3 November 2026 Pacific. Prior review dates above refer to the inherited research package; revised prose received an integration review, not a new independent expert review. Examples and drills remain illustrative.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Warding\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA05\n- Slug: warding\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner, with Support and Jungle emphasis.\n- Summary: Place vision where it can inform a decision, with safe access and a response.\n- Prerequisites: Map awareness.\n- Observable action: Before placing a ward, say what decision its information will change.\n\n## Short answer\n\nWarding is placing wards to gain information about areas of the map. Useful coverage depends on what you need to know, when you need it, and whether you can reach the spot safely. This introduction covers placement decisions, not every ward type or detection interaction.\n\nName your next question, choose safely reachable coverage that helps answer it, and decide how you will respond. One ward can inform a decision while leaving other approaches unknown.\n\n## Connect coverage to a decision\n\n**Fog of war** is the area your team cannot currently see. A ward provides **coverage**: information about part of the map under the game's vision rules. A **route** is an approach an enemy might use; a **choke** is a narrow passage. Seeing one route does not establish what is happening along every other route.\n\nBiofrost's warding advice makes depth conditional on pressure and assistance, and warns against risking life for late objective vision. Isles' coaching connects map movement with useful information and time. [Dignitas, “Warding Around the Mid” and “Warding Around Dragon”](https://dignitas.gg/articles/pro-level-warding-guide-with-dignitas-biofrost); [Dignitas, section 3, “Managing your time around the map wisely”](https://dignitas.gg/articles/five-important-support-tricks-and-advice-with-isles).\n\nThe question-first process below is editorial synthesis:\n\n1. **State the imminent decision.** “Can I keep collecting this side wave?” is more actionable than “I need more vision.” Choose a question that matters before your next move.\n2. **Pick a relevant approach.** Ask where a warning would help you change that move. Consider the time and space your champion needs to retreat, without assuming a universal warning distance.\n3. **Check access before depth.** Can you reach the spot without crossing an unchecked entrance? Count visible allies who can help, not an escort you hope will appear. Use closer useful coverage, wait for help or reduce the depth of your intended play when access is unsafe.\n4. **Attach a response.** Decide what seeing an enemy would change. Also name an uncovered approach. Reassess when the ward is removed, coverage ends, or your next job changes.\n\nPlacement spends a ward, travel time and presence elsewhere. An empty covered route can still be useful information, but only within its coverage and the period you observed. Enemy mobility, stealth and alternate paths limit what that absence tells you. This lesson supplies no universal ward location or detection rule for those abilities.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYou want to collect a side wave. A reachable ward can watch an approach toward your retreat path. Your question is, “Will someone approach along this route while I collect?” Your planned response is to leave when a threat appears.\n\nA second route remains uncovered. You collect only the portion of the wave that leaves an acceptable exit instead of treating the ward as permission to advance indefinitely. If placing the ward itself requires walking through an unknown choke, you wait or choose a closer location. The ideal-looking spot is not worth assuming safe access.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA05; use one width variant at a time. -->\n![An abstract watched approach and a second unknown approach converge toward a side wave. A ward answers only the first-route question; the player keeps an exit and plans to leave when a threat appears.](../visuals/previews/ma05-mobile.png)\n\n*A useful warning leaves other routes unknown.*\n\nFigure files: [desktop SVG](../visuals/assets/ma05-desktop.svg) · [mobile SVG](../visuals/assets/ma05-mobile.svg) · [desktop PNG](../visuals/previews/ma05-desktop.png) · [mobile PNG](../visuals/previews/ma05-mobile.png).\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** “I warded, so this area is safe.” You continue forward without checking what the ward can reveal.\n\n**Correction:** Complete the sentence: “This watches ___; ___ remains unknown; I will ___ if the information changes.” A useful ward answers a bounded question. It does not remove every risk.\n\n## Practice\n\nFor five wards in a replay, record the question and planned response before revealing later events. Check whether the coverage mattered while the decision was still active. Review at least one response to new information or ended coverage; if none occurred, note that and use another sample.\n\nThis proposed drill measures whether you gave the ward a purpose and used the result. It has no vision-score quota, validated success rate or requirement that an enemy cross the ward.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Map awareness](../macro/MA39-map-awareness.md) · [Vision denial](../macro/MA19-vision-denial.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\nBoth coaching sources share the Dignitas publisher; they do not establish independent-publisher consensus. Exact current coverage, ward duration and historical placement diagrams are excluded. Any later precise placement illustration needs a current client check. The proposed question-and-response framework has not been tested for teaching efficacy.\n\nLast independently reviewed and sources accessed: **3 November 2026 Pacific / 4 November 2026 UTC**. Source dates below are historical publication/update dates, not live-patch certification.\n\n- Dignitas / Teatones interviewing Biofrost; 15 May 2022.\n- Dignitas / Myodo interviewing Isles; 14 Aug 2025.\n\nEditorial revision: concept scope updated for LEA-24 on 3 November 2026 Pacific. Prior review dates above refer to the inherited research package; revised prose received an integration review, not a new independent expert review. Examples and drills remain illustrative.\n", "minutes": 4, "sha256": "ffdaec5a0842d4961b658c60d8631890a96a5a1304dc3c710bdd943ae0e35750"}, {"id": "MA06", "slug": "jungle-tracking", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Infer likely jungle movement from sightings while keeping uncertainty explicit.", "title": "Jungle tracking", "file": "MA06-jungle-tracking.md", "meta": {"roles": "Jungle and all laners; intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Map awareness, Warding.", "action": "State a last sighting, a likely area, an alternative and the resulting decision as separate items."}, "aliases": ["track-the-jungler-without-pretending-to-know"], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Jungle tracking is using visible information to estimate a jungler’s location and possible next actions between sightings. It produces a prediction, not a view through fog of war.<\/p><p>Separate where the jungler was seen from where they might go next. Use the prediction to inform one decision, then revise it when new evidence arrives or the old sighting no longer supports it. “Unknown” is a useful answer.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Separate observation from inference<\/h2><p>An <strong>observation<\/strong> is something your team actually saw: the jungler appeared in a place at a particular game time. An <strong>inference<\/strong> is your explanation of what may follow. An <strong>unknown<\/strong> is a gap the evidence does not resolve. Fog hides choices between sightings; a plausible route is not an observed route.<\/p><p>Dignitas&#x27; tracking guide discusses sightings and likely camp sequencing. Tomo&#x27;s coaching connects jungle information with judging whether a lane exchange may receive extra enemies. Those ideas support using information, but not exact route certainty. <a href=\"https://dignitas.gg/articles/how-to-track-the-enemy-jungler\">Dignitas, “First Recall” and “Tracking Through Vision”<\/a>; <a href=\"https://dignitas.gg/articles/adc-mindset-guide-with-dig-tomo\">Dignitas, “What Can You Get Away With in 2v2?”<\/a>.<\/p><p>The following uncertainty discipline is editorial synthesis, not a calibrated prediction model:<\/p><ol><li><strong>Record what was seen.<\/strong> Name location and game time. If a start location came from a guess, label it a guess. A teammate&#x27;s old report retains its original age.<\/li><li><strong>Suggest a likely area and an alternative.<\/strong> Use visible direction and opportunities you actually know about. The jungler may skip camps, attack a lane, enter the other jungle or return to base. Do not fill every hidden step with the route you expected.<\/li><li><strong>Connect it to one action.<\/strong> You might continue briefly, hold a defensive ability, shorten a push, use another route or retreat. Name which risk the prediction changes and which risks remain.<\/li><li><strong>Update or return to unknown.<\/strong> A new sighting may overturn the story. Time passing permits more possibilities; there is no universal freshness timer. Movement tools and unusual paths can make the old position less useful sooner than you expect.<\/li><\/ol><p>In solo queue, communicate “last seen” and “may be” rather than issuing a certainty claim. Coordinated teams can pool sightings while retaining their times. Uncertainty does not require permanent retreat: excessive caution gives up pressure and income. Make the smallest commitment your current information supports and reassess.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>You see the enemy jungler top. Your observation is “seen top now.” Your inference is that some immediate bottom-side gank risk is reduced, subject to travel and movement tools. One alternative is that they leave top quickly rather than continue clearing there.<\/p><p>You take a brief bottom-lane action with an exit available. Later, without another sighting, you stop relying on the top appearance and shorten your next push. You never observed a full top-side clear, so “they must still be clearing top” would add information you do not have.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma06-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma06-desktop.svg\" alt=\"A solid observation says seen top now. A dashed inference suggests a likely area and an alternative. Without fresh evidence the location becomes unknown, shown with hatching. No hidden route is revealed.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Later choices must account for more possible locations.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><p>Figure files: <a href=\"assets/ma06-desktop.svg\">desktop SVG<\/a> · <a href=\"assets/ma06-mobile.svg\">mobile SVG<\/a> · <a href=\"assets/ma06-desktop.png\">desktop PNG<\/a> · <a href=\"assets/ma06-mobile.png\">mobile PNG<\/a>.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Turning one sighting into an exact camp sequence, then dismissing a surprising gank as impossible.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Keep an alternative and revisit the age of the observation. Review the prediction from player view before revealing the route. Spectator knowledge can explain the outcome afterward; it cannot justify a decision made without that knowledge.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Pause a replay at three decisions before revealing hidden enemy movement. Write four fields: <strong>seen / likely / alternative / my action<\/strong>. Reveal the path afterward and record what new evidence should trigger an update. If a field was only a guess, mark it as such.<\/p><p>Check that all three records distinguish observation from inference and that later information changes the account when needed. Describe correct and incorrect area predictions without a target accuracy. This is an unvalidated teaching proposal.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/map-awareness\">Map awareness<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/warding\">Warding<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/lane-priority\">Lane priority<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p>The coaching evidence shares the Dignitas publisher. No independently calibrated prediction accuracy is claimed. CS arithmetic, mandatory full clears, exact expiry windows, item prescriptions and rank-based path assumptions are excluded. Current camp geography is entry knowledge, not verified route instruction from this article.<\/p><p>Last independently reviewed and sources accessed: <strong>3 November 2026 Pacific / 4 November 2026 UTC<\/strong>. Source dates below are historical publication/update dates, not live-patch certification.<\/p><ul><li>Dignitas / Famous; 29 Nov 2025.<\/li><li>Dignitas / Myodo interviewing Tomo; 7 Dec 2025.<\/li><\/ul><p>Editorial revision: concept scope updated for LEA-24 on 3 November 2026 Pacific. Prior review dates above refer to the inherited research package; revised prose received an integration review, not a new independent expert review. Examples and drills remain illustrative.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Jungle tracking\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA06\n- Slug: jungle-tracking\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: Jungle and all laners; intermediate.\n- Summary: Infer likely jungle movement from sightings while keeping uncertainty explicit.\n- Prerequisites: Map awareness, Warding.\n- Observable action: State a last sighting, a likely area, an alternative and the resulting decision as separate items.\n\n## Short answer\n\nJungle tracking is using visible information to estimate a jungler’s location and possible next actions between sightings. It produces a prediction, not a view through fog of war.\n\nSeparate where the jungler was seen from where they might go next. Use the prediction to inform one decision, then revise it when new evidence arrives or the old sighting no longer supports it. “Unknown” is a useful answer.\n\n## Separate observation from inference\n\nAn **observation** is something your team actually saw: the jungler appeared in a place at a particular game time. An **inference** is your explanation of what may follow. An **unknown** is a gap the evidence does not resolve. Fog hides choices between sightings; a plausible route is not an observed route.\n\nDignitas' tracking guide discusses sightings and likely camp sequencing. Tomo's coaching connects jungle information with judging whether a lane exchange may receive extra enemies. Those ideas support using information, but not exact route certainty. [Dignitas, “First Recall” and “Tracking Through Vision”](https://dignitas.gg/articles/how-to-track-the-enemy-jungler); [Dignitas, “What Can You Get Away With in 2v2?”](https://dignitas.gg/articles/adc-mindset-guide-with-dig-tomo).\n\nThe following uncertainty discipline is editorial synthesis, not a calibrated prediction model:\n\n1. **Record what was seen.** Name location and game time. If a start location came from a guess, label it a guess. A teammate's old report retains its original age.\n2. **Suggest a likely area and an alternative.** Use visible direction and opportunities you actually know about. The jungler may skip camps, attack a lane, enter the other jungle or return to base. Do not fill every hidden step with the route you expected.\n3. **Connect it to one action.** You might continue briefly, hold a defensive ability, shorten a push, use another route or retreat. Name which risk the prediction changes and which risks remain.\n4. **Update or return to unknown.** A new sighting may overturn the story. Time passing permits more possibilities; there is no universal freshness timer. Movement tools and unusual paths can make the old position less useful sooner than you expect.\n\nIn solo queue, communicate “last seen” and “may be” rather than issuing a certainty claim. Coordinated teams can pool sightings while retaining their times. Uncertainty does not require permanent retreat: excessive caution gives up pressure and income. Make the smallest commitment your current information supports and reassess.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYou see the enemy jungler top. Your observation is “seen top now.” Your inference is that some immediate bottom-side gank risk is reduced, subject to travel and movement tools. One alternative is that they leave top quickly rather than continue clearing there.\n\nYou take a brief bottom-lane action with an exit available. Later, without another sighting, you stop relying on the top appearance and shorten your next push. You never observed a full top-side clear, so “they must still be clearing top” would add information you do not have.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA06; use one width variant at a time. -->\n![A solid observation says seen top now. A dashed inference suggests a likely area and an alternative. Without fresh evidence the location becomes unknown, shown with hatching. No hidden route is revealed.](../visuals/previews/ma06-mobile.png)\n\n*Later choices must account for more possible locations.*\n\nFigure files: [desktop SVG](../visuals/assets/ma06-desktop.svg) · [mobile SVG](../visuals/assets/ma06-mobile.svg) · [desktop PNG](../visuals/previews/ma06-desktop.png) · [mobile PNG](../visuals/previews/ma06-mobile.png).\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Turning one sighting into an exact camp sequence, then dismissing a surprising gank as impossible.\n\n**Correction:** Keep an alternative and revisit the age of the observation. Review the prediction from player view before revealing the route. Spectator knowledge can explain the outcome afterward; it cannot justify a decision made without that knowledge.\n\n## Practice\n\nPause a replay at three decisions before revealing hidden enemy movement. Write four fields: **seen / likely / alternative / my action**. Reveal the path afterward and record what new evidence should trigger an update. If a field was only a guess, mark it as such.\n\nCheck that all three records distinguish observation from inference and that later information changes the account when needed. Describe correct and incorrect area predictions without a target accuracy. This is an unvalidated teaching proposal.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Map awareness](../macro/MA39-map-awareness.md) · [Warding](../macro/MA05-warding.md) · [Lane priority](../macro/MA04-lane-priority.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\nThe coaching evidence shares the Dignitas publisher. No independently calibrated prediction accuracy is claimed. CS arithmetic, mandatory full clears, exact expiry windows, item prescriptions and rank-based path assumptions are excluded. Current camp geography is entry knowledge, not verified route instruction from this article.\n\nLast independently reviewed and sources accessed: **3 November 2026 Pacific / 4 November 2026 UTC**. Source dates below are historical publication/update dates, not live-patch certification.\n\n- Dignitas / Famous; 29 Nov 2025.\n- Dignitas / Myodo interviewing Tomo; 7 Dec 2025.\n\nEditorial revision: concept scope updated for LEA-24 on 3 November 2026 Pacific. Prior review dates above refer to the inherited research package; revised prose received an integration review, not a new independent expert review. Examples and drills remain illustrative.\n", "minutes": 4, "sha256": "a5510da3ea1a0bc6db4ef64fef9c8dae9753732f2bc5484fedc97d3162b56037"}, {"id": "MA10", "slug": "objective-control", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Use waves, resources, vision and access to influence neutral-objective contests.", "title": "Objective control", "file": "MA10-objective-control.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; intermediate, especially Jungle and Support.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Lane priority, Warding, Jungle tracking, Resets.", "action": "Before an objective commitment, state start, wait or leave with a reason tied to the available information."}, "aliases": ["prepare-an-objective-before-committing-to-it"], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Objective control is the ability to influence whether and how teams can take or contest an objective. For major neutral monsters, it involves lane pressure, prepared players, vision and access. Starting the monster is one possible use of control; waiting, denying access or conceding can also follow from the situation.<\/p><p>Before starting an epic objective, check who can actually help, whether they are ready, what nearby lanes need, and how enemies can approach. Choose start, wait or leave according to urgency and those conditions. Recheck when the situation changes.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Control before the start<\/h2><p>Control can exist before anyone attacks the monster. A team that can move from lane and safely watch entrances may have more options to start, threaten a start or turn toward approaching enemies. That advantage can disappear when players leave or resources run low. Vision informs those choices; it does not prevent an opponent from contesting.<\/p><p>This introduction focuses on preparation and the start decision. It does not cover every objective, exact rewards, pit execution or matchup-specific fights. Use the checks below to explain what control your team actually has.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Prepare for a commitment<\/h2><p>An <strong>epic objective<\/strong> here means a major neutral monster such as Dragon or Baron. A <strong>reset<\/strong> recovers resources and purchases; <strong>priority<\/strong> is an opportunity to act before an opposing laner. <strong>Access<\/strong> means the routes you can use and the information and protection available along them. A ward covering one approach leaves others uncertain. A last jungle sighting is a past observation, not a live location.<\/p><p>Riot&#x27;s objective design discussion links commitment risk with lane cover. Dignitas&#x27; older objective-trading guide asks about strength, vision and numbers, while Biofrost&#x27;s warding discussion warns against unsafe late access. <a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-gb/news/game-updates/patch-26-1-notes/\">Riot, “Epic Objective Tuning”<\/a>; <a href=\"https://dignitas.gg/articles/an-eye-for-an-eye-tactics-for-trading-objectives\">Dignitas, “Dragon”<\/a>; <a href=\"https://dignitas.gg/articles/pro-level-warding-guide-with-dignitas-biofrost\">Dignitas, “Warding Around Dragon”<\/a>.<\/p><p>Use these four checks as an editorial decision aid:<\/p><ol><li><strong>Urgency: what does waiting or conceding cost?<\/strong> Consider the base, the enemy&#x27;s route to winning and jobs available elsewhere. Routine caution may be inappropriate if conceding leaves no credible chance to continue the game. Availability alone is not a reason to start.<\/li><li><strong>Participants and resources: who can act now?<\/strong> Count allies actually able to arrive, their health, mana or equivalent resources, and known useful abilities. Consider recovery and purchases early enough to return, without imposing a fixed setup countdown. A player walking from base is not already beside you.<\/li><li><strong>Wave duties: who can leave?<\/strong> A wave is a group of lane minions; clearing removes opposing minions, while a crash brings your wave to the enemy turret. Check who is defending or collecting and what joining costs. Do not assume every lane can push or every pushed laner will join.<\/li><li><strong>Access: what can approach, and where can you leave?<\/strong> Use sightings, covered routes and visible movement. Name the important unknown entrance or flank. If repairing vision requires entering darkness without protection, waiting safely or leaving can be preferable to forcing the setup.<\/li><\/ol><p>Now choose <strong>start<\/strong>, <strong>wait<\/strong>, or <strong>leave for another reachable job<\/strong>. A start remains conditional: an ally departing or an enemy arriving can change it. Low damage, a threatened base or a blocked exit may invalidate the original call. Preparation also spends income and presence elsewhere; waiting for a perfect setup can miss the useful opportunity.<\/p><p>In solo queue, offer a simple call and adapt to actual participants. In coordinated play, confirm wave coverage and who calls continuing, fighting approaching opponents or leaving. This lesson chooses the commitment; it does not teach monster execution or Smite combinations.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>Dragon is available. Your bot carry is returning from base, your mid laner is defending a wave, and a river approach is unchecked. You choose <strong>wait<\/strong> at a safe entrance instead of starting on the assumption that both allies will arrive.<\/p><p>When the carry approaches, you reassess all four checks. If support still cannot arrive, you may leave for a reachable job. If conceding would leave the enemy with an imminent game-ending opportunity, a risky contest may be justified. The lesson is to explain that urgency explicitly, rather than silently applying the routine wait response to every situation.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma10-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma10-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Dragon is available. Check urgency, participants, wave duties and access: the carry is returning, mid is defending and one approach is unchecked. Wait at a safe entrance and reassess; imminent game-ending risk may change the choice.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Choose the commitment after checking the situation, and revisit it when cues change.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><p>Figure files: <a href=\"assets/ma10-desktop.svg\">desktop SVG<\/a> · <a href=\"assets/ma10-mobile.svg\">mobile SVG<\/a> · <a href=\"assets/ma10-desktop.png\">desktop PNG<\/a> · <a href=\"assets/ma10-mobile.png\">mobile PNG<\/a>.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> “It spawned, so we should start.” The team begins, then tries to repair missing allies and vision while committed.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Choose the call after the checks. Revisit it when conditions change. Do not risk a late blind ward solely to preserve a decision made before you checked access.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>After the objective<\/h2><p>Recheck health, available allies, waves and enemy positions before choosing another action. Convert the gain into a feasible lane push, safer map access or a reset for the next task. Winning the monster does not make a chase safe. If no useful follow-up is available, recover and preserve the advantage.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Review two objective commitments in player view. Before revealing outcomes, write the four checks and one justified choice: start, wait or leave. Then identify the first later cue that should change or confirm the call.<\/p><p>The observable check is two complete decision records. Winning the objective is not the training metric. This is a proposed teaching drill, not a validated method or promise of better results.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/lane-priority\">Lane priority<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/warding\">Warding<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/jungle-tracking\">Jungle tracking<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/resets\">Resets<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/sieging\">Sieging<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p>Both coaching pages are published by Dignitas. Riot provides official design context from dated notes, not independent validation of the whole checklist. The checklist and urgency exceptions are editorial synthesis. Historical reward values, fixed objective trades, exact timers, universal numbers advantages and guaranteed takes are excluded. No current client, match replay or teaching-efficacy test validates the example.<\/p><p>Last independently reviewed and sources accessed: <strong>3 November 2026 Pacific / 4 November 2026 UTC<\/strong>. Source dates below are historical publication/update dates, not live-patch certification.<\/p><ul><li>Riot Games / Riru, Sakaar; 7 Jan 2026.<\/li><li>Dignitas / Wave; 6 Oct 2015.<\/li><li>Dignitas / Teatones interviewing Biofrost; 15 May 2022.<\/li><\/ul><p>Editorial revision: concept scope updated for LEA-24 on 3 November 2026 Pacific. Prior review dates above refer to the inherited research package; revised prose received an integration review, not a new independent expert review. Examples and drills remain illustrative.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Objective control\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA10\n- Slug: objective-control\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: All roles; intermediate, especially Jungle and Support.\n- Summary: Use waves, resources, vision and access to influence neutral-objective contests.\n- Prerequisites: Lane priority, Warding, Jungle tracking, Resets.\n- Observable action: Before an objective commitment, state start, wait or leave with a reason tied to the available information.\n\n## Short answer\n\nObjective control is the ability to influence whether and how teams can take or contest an objective. For major neutral monsters, it involves lane pressure, prepared players, vision and access. Starting the monster is one possible use of control; waiting, denying access or conceding can also follow from the situation.\n\nBefore starting an epic objective, check who can actually help, whether they are ready, what nearby lanes need, and how enemies can approach. Choose start, wait or leave according to urgency and those conditions. Recheck when the situation changes.\n\n## Control before the start\n\nControl can exist before anyone attacks the monster. A team that can move from lane and safely watch entrances may have more options to start, threaten a start or turn toward approaching enemies. That advantage can disappear when players leave or resources run low. Vision informs those choices; it does not prevent an opponent from contesting.\n\nThis introduction focuses on preparation and the start decision. It does not cover every objective, exact rewards, pit execution or matchup-specific fights. Use the checks below to explain what control your team actually has.\n\n## Prepare for a commitment\n\nAn **epic objective** here means a major neutral monster such as Dragon or Baron. A **reset** recovers resources and purchases; **priority** is an opportunity to act before an opposing laner. **Access** means the routes you can use and the information and protection available along them. A ward covering one approach leaves others uncertain. A last jungle sighting is a past observation, not a live location.\n\nRiot's objective design discussion links commitment risk with lane cover. Dignitas' older objective-trading guide asks about strength, vision and numbers, while Biofrost's warding discussion warns against unsafe late access. [Riot, “Epic Objective Tuning”](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-gb/news/game-updates/patch-26-1-notes/); [Dignitas, “Dragon”](https://dignitas.gg/articles/an-eye-for-an-eye-tactics-for-trading-objectives); [Dignitas, “Warding Around Dragon”](https://dignitas.gg/articles/pro-level-warding-guide-with-dignitas-biofrost).\n\nUse these four checks as an editorial decision aid:\n\n1. **Urgency: what does waiting or conceding cost?** Consider the base, the enemy's route to winning and jobs available elsewhere. Routine caution may be inappropriate if conceding leaves no credible chance to continue the game. Availability alone is not a reason to start.\n2. **Participants and resources: who can act now?** Count allies actually able to arrive, their health, mana or equivalent resources, and known useful abilities. Consider recovery and purchases early enough to return, without imposing a fixed setup countdown. A player walking from base is not already beside you.\n3. **Wave duties: who can leave?** A wave is a group of lane minions; clearing removes opposing minions, while a crash brings your wave to the enemy turret. Check who is defending or collecting and what joining costs. Do not assume every lane can push or every pushed laner will join.\n4. **Access: what can approach, and where can you leave?** Use sightings, covered routes and visible movement. Name the important unknown entrance or flank. If repairing vision requires entering darkness without protection, waiting safely or leaving can be preferable to forcing the setup.\n\nNow choose **start**, **wait**, or **leave for another reachable job**. A start remains conditional: an ally departing or an enemy arriving can change it. Low damage, a threatened base or a blocked exit may invalidate the original call. Preparation also spends income and presence elsewhere; waiting for a perfect setup can miss the useful opportunity.\n\nIn solo queue, offer a simple call and adapt to actual participants. In coordinated play, confirm wave coverage and who calls continuing, fighting approaching opponents or leaving. This lesson chooses the commitment; it does not teach monster execution or Smite combinations.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nDragon is available. Your bot carry is returning from base, your mid laner is defending a wave, and a river approach is unchecked. You choose **wait** at a safe entrance instead of starting on the assumption that both allies will arrive.\n\nWhen the carry approaches, you reassess all four checks. If support still cannot arrive, you may leave for a reachable job. If conceding would leave the enemy with an imminent game-ending opportunity, a risky contest may be justified. The lesson is to explain that urgency explicitly, rather than silently applying the routine wait response to every situation.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA10; use one width variant at a time. -->\n![Dragon is available. Check urgency, participants, wave duties and access: the carry is returning, mid is defending and one approach is unchecked. Wait at a safe entrance and reassess; imminent game-ending risk may change the choice.](../visuals/previews/ma10-mobile.png)\n\n*Choose the commitment after checking the situation, and revisit it when cues change.*\n\nFigure files: [desktop SVG](../visuals/assets/ma10-desktop.svg) · [mobile SVG](../visuals/assets/ma10-mobile.svg) · [desktop PNG](../visuals/previews/ma10-desktop.png) · [mobile PNG](../visuals/previews/ma10-mobile.png).\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** “It spawned, so we should start.” The team begins, then tries to repair missing allies and vision while committed.\n\n**Correction:** Choose the call after the checks. Revisit it when conditions change. Do not risk a late blind ward solely to preserve a decision made before you checked access.\n\n## After the objective\n\nRecheck health, available allies, waves and enemy positions before choosing another action. Convert the gain into a feasible lane push, safer map access or a reset for the next task. Winning the monster does not make a chase safe. If no useful follow-up is available, recover and preserve the advantage.\n\n## Practice\n\nReview two objective commitments in player view. Before revealing outcomes, write the four checks and one justified choice: start, wait or leave. Then identify the first later cue that should change or confirm the call.\n\nThe observable check is two complete decision records. Winning the objective is not the training metric. This is a proposed teaching drill, not a validated method or promise of better results.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Lane priority](../macro/MA04-lane-priority.md) · [Warding](../macro/MA05-warding.md) · [Jungle tracking](../macro/MA06-jungle-tracking.md) · [Resets](../macro/MA23-resets.md) · [Sieging](../macro/MA34-sieging.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\nBoth coaching pages are published by Dignitas. Riot provides official design context from dated notes, not independent validation of the whole checklist. The checklist and urgency exceptions are editorial synthesis. Historical reward values, fixed objective trades, exact timers, universal numbers advantages and guaranteed takes are excluded. No current client, match replay or teaching-efficacy test validates the example.\n\nLast independently reviewed and sources accessed: **3 November 2026 Pacific / 4 November 2026 UTC**. Source dates below are historical publication/update dates, not live-patch certification.\n\n- Riot Games / Riru, Sakaar; 7 Jan 2026.\n- Dignitas / Wave; 6 Oct 2015.\n- Dignitas / Teatones interviewing Biofrost; 15 May 2022.\n\nEditorial revision: concept scope updated for LEA-24 on 3 November 2026 Pacific. Prior review dates above refer to the inherited research package; revised prose received an integration review, not a new independent expert review. Examples and drills remain illustrative.\n", "minutes": 5, "sha256": "0cee9b6e90678b5a25ff38eae911790e3803e6e805e0241effab42d07efd8c06"}, {"id": "MA11", "slug": "freezing", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Hold the wave in a useful place and recognise when the hold costs too much.", "title": "Freezing", "file": "MA11-freezing.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Wave bouncing.", "action": "Choose the position and reason for the hold: safer farming or making an opponent approach."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Freezing means keeping successive minion waves fighting near the same place. A common aim is to hold them outside your own turret range. You adjust damage to the wave instead of clearing every minion as soon as possible.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>A freeze needs a sustainable balance between the two waves. Last-hit, watch which side is winning, and thin an incoming wave if it would overwhelm the hold. Minion health, reinforcements and champion damage matter; a fixed minion count is not a universal recipe. Holding a wave can make the opponent walk farther forward to farm. It does not automatically stop them from gaining experience.<\/p><ol><li>Choose the position and reason for the hold: safer farming or making an opponent approach.<\/li><li>Observe the next collision. If the enemy wave is too strong, reduce it; if your wave wins too quickly, reduce your extra attacks.<\/li><li>Reassess when an opponent approaches, help disappears or a nearby fight starts. Let the hold go if keeping it requires a bad trade.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>You want to farm near your turret while the enemy jungler is unseen. The wave is held outside turret range, but the opponent returns with help. You retreat and collect what reaches the turret. Losing the hold is cheaper than standing in front of two champions to preserve it.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma11-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma11-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Ally turret | wave | enemy. Keep the wave outside range. Next wave joins the fight. Observe health and direction. Enemy help arrives. Retreat; collect at the turret.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for freezing; positions and timing are not game measurements.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Treating the frozen wave as something you must protect at any cost.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Set a health and safety limit before trying to hold it. A freeze is a choice about lane resources, not an obligation to fight.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>In a replay, choose three wave collisions near your turret. Before each one, predict whether the meeting point will stay, move toward you or move away. Watch the result and identify the damage or reinforcement that changed it. Then name one event that would make you abandon the hold.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>This exercise evaluates observation, not a target number of denied minions. Enemy pressure can prevent a freeze. Holding also ties you to lane when teammates may need you elsewhere.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/wave-bouncing\">Wave bouncing<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/recalling\">Recalling<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://dignitas.gg/articles/how-to-improve-wave-management-for-every-lane-in-league-of-legends\">Source and context<\/a>. The original Dignitas coaching guide by EkardD (6 July 2026) supports the basic hold, thinning and opportunity-cost concepts. Its exact minion recipes, seasonal rankings and promised advantages are not adopted.<\/p><p>Sources checked 4 November 2026. Examples and exercises are teaching proposals, not tested performance guarantees. No live patch certification or independent expert review is claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Freezing\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA11\n- Slug: freezing\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Hold the wave in a useful place and recognise when the hold costs too much.\n- Prerequisites: Wave bouncing.\n- Observable action: Choose the position and reason for the hold: safer farming or making an opponent approach.\n\n## Short answer\n\nFreezing means keeping successive minion waves fighting near the same place. A common aim is to hold them outside your own turret range. You adjust damage to the wave instead of clearing every minion as soon as possible.\n\n## How it works\n\nA freeze needs a sustainable balance between the two waves. Last-hit, watch which side is winning, and thin an incoming wave if it would overwhelm the hold. Minion health, reinforcements and champion damage matter; a fixed minion count is not a universal recipe. Holding a wave can make the opponent walk farther forward to farm. It does not automatically stop them from gaining experience.\n\n1. Choose the position and reason for the hold: safer farming or making an opponent approach.\n2. Observe the next collision. If the enemy wave is too strong, reduce it; if your wave wins too quickly, reduce your extra attacks.\n3. Reassess when an opponent approaches, help disappears or a nearby fight starts. Let the hold go if keeping it requires a bad trade.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYou want to farm near your turret while the enemy jungler is unseen. The wave is held outside turret range, but the opponent returns with help. You retreat and collect what reaches the turret. Losing the hold is cheaper than standing in front of two champions to preserve it.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA11 -->\n![Ally turret | wave | enemy. Keep the wave outside range. Next wave joins the fight. Observe health and direction. Enemy help arrives. Retreat; collect at the turret.](../visuals/assets/ma11-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for freezing; positions and timing are not game measurements.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Treating the frozen wave as something you must protect at any cost.\n\n**Correction:** Set a health and safety limit before trying to hold it. A freeze is a choice about lane resources, not an obligation to fight.\n\n## Practice\n\nIn a replay, choose three wave collisions near your turret. Before each one, predict whether the meeting point will stay, move toward you or move away. Watch the result and identify the damage or reinforcement that changed it. Then name one event that would make you abandon the hold.\n\n## Limits\n\nThis exercise evaluates observation, not a target number of denied minions. Enemy pressure can prevent a freeze. Holding also ties you to lane when teammates may need you elsewhere.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Wave bouncing](../macro/MA17-wave-bouncing.md) · [Recalling](../macro/MA02-recalling.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Source and context](https://dignitas.gg/articles/how-to-improve-wave-management-for-every-lane-in-league-of-legends). The original Dignitas coaching guide by EkardD (6 July 2026) supports the basic hold, thinning and opportunity-cost concepts. Its exact minion recipes, seasonal rankings and promised advantages are not adopted.\n\nSources checked 4 November 2026. Examples and exercises are teaching proposals, not tested performance guarantees. No live patch certification or independent expert review is claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "798b879bacd80cebceed5669a49590dc33b31e5a43cc860f8c5dd72d8fb368ec"}, {"id": "MA12", "slug": "slow-pushing", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Build an advancing wave before deciding when to send it into the turret.", "title": "Slow pushing", "file": "MA12-slow-pushing.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Wave management, Fast pushing.", "action": "Name the next use of the wave: a recall, a move toward river or pressure on the turret."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Slow pushing means letting an allied minion advantage advance gradually so more allied minions can join it. The aim is to build a wave before a later crash, when allied minions reach the enemy turret.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Create or recognise a small allied advantage, then limit extra damage while last-hitting. Check whether the wave is actually growing. When you need it to reach the turret, clear the remaining resistance. Opponents can thin the wave or contest you, so a planned buildup can fail.<\/p><ol><li>Name the next use of the wave: a recall, a move toward river or pressure on the turret.<\/li><li>Watch the buildup rather than following a fixed number of waves. Compare the remaining health and incoming reinforcements.<\/li><li>Choose a finish point. If the map changes before you can complete the crash, reconsider the plan instead of defending it blindly.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>You plan to build a wave and recall. Before the wave reaches the enemy turret, your jungler asks for help nearby. You see that leaving now would leave the wave unfinished and the route is unsafe. You signal that you cannot join immediately. The wave plan helps explain your choice; it does not make the jungler wait.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma12-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma12-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Small allied advantage. Limit extra wave damage. Allied reinforcements join. Check that minions survive. Clear toward enemy turret. Crash, then reassess the map.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for slow pushing; positions and timing are not game measurements.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Continuing to build because a guide said to stack a certain number of waves.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Use the next action as the goal. A bigger wave is useful only when you can use the time or pressure it creates.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Pause a replay before a wave starts advancing. Write down the intended next action and one reason to cancel it. Watch until the next wave arrives. Record whether your extra attacks helped the buildup or cleared too quickly, and whether your next action was still useful.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>This is a planning drill, not a guaranteed recall or dive setup. A large wave does not remove enemy champion threats. If you cannot protect the buildup safely, accept a smaller wave or a different plan.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/wave-management\">Wave management<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/fast-pushing\">Fast pushing<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/wave-crashing\">Wave crashing<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://dignitas.gg/articles/how-to-improve-wave-management-for-every-lane-in-league-of-legends\">Source and context<\/a>. Dignitas’s original wave-management guide supports gradual buildup followed by a crash. The scenario, cancellation checks and practice method here are editorial teaching examples; no fixed wave count or seasonal claim is prescribed.<\/p><p>Sources checked 4 November 2026. Examples and exercises are teaching proposals, not tested performance guarantees. No live patch certification or independent expert review is claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Slow pushing\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA12\n- Slug: slow-pushing\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Build an advancing wave before deciding when to send it into the turret.\n- Prerequisites: Wave management, Fast pushing.\n- Observable action: Name the next use of the wave: a recall, a move toward river or pressure on the turret.\n\n## Short answer\n\nSlow pushing means letting an allied minion advantage advance gradually so more allied minions can join it. The aim is to build a wave before a later crash, when allied minions reach the enemy turret.\n\n## How it works\n\nCreate or recognise a small allied advantage, then limit extra damage while last-hitting. Check whether the wave is actually growing. When you need it to reach the turret, clear the remaining resistance. Opponents can thin the wave or contest you, so a planned buildup can fail.\n\n1. Name the next use of the wave: a recall, a move toward river or pressure on the turret.\n2. Watch the buildup rather than following a fixed number of waves. Compare the remaining health and incoming reinforcements.\n3. Choose a finish point. If the map changes before you can complete the crash, reconsider the plan instead of defending it blindly.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYou plan to build a wave and recall. Before the wave reaches the enemy turret, your jungler asks for help nearby. You see that leaving now would leave the wave unfinished and the route is unsafe. You signal that you cannot join immediately. The wave plan helps explain your choice; it does not make the jungler wait.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA12 -->\n![Small allied advantage. Limit extra wave damage. Allied reinforcements join. Check that minions survive. Clear toward enemy turret. Crash, then reassess the map.](../visuals/assets/ma12-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for slow pushing; positions and timing are not game measurements.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Continuing to build because a guide said to stack a certain number of waves.\n\n**Correction:** Use the next action as the goal. A bigger wave is useful only when you can use the time or pressure it creates.\n\n## Practice\n\nPause a replay before a wave starts advancing. Write down the intended next action and one reason to cancel it. Watch until the next wave arrives. Record whether your extra attacks helped the buildup or cleared too quickly, and whether your next action was still useful.\n\n## Limits\n\nThis is a planning drill, not a guaranteed recall or dive setup. A large wave does not remove enemy champion threats. If you cannot protect the buildup safely, accept a smaller wave or a different plan.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Wave management](../macro/MA40-wave-management.md) · [Fast pushing](../macro/MA13-fast-pushing.md) · [Wave crashing](../macro/MA16-wave-crashing.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Source and context](https://dignitas.gg/articles/how-to-improve-wave-management-for-every-lane-in-league-of-legends). Dignitas’s original wave-management guide supports gradual buildup followed by a crash. The scenario, cancellation checks and practice method here are editorial teaching examples; no fixed wave count or seasonal claim is prescribed.\n\nSources checked 4 November 2026. Examples and exercises are teaching proposals, not tested performance guarantees. No live patch certification or independent expert review is claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "06a18e8128593673324e69423f565a6cc00d4b54e7cd02a5b493863a78be0c82"}, {"id": "MA13", "slug": "fast-pushing", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Clear a wave quickly to create time for a specific next action.", "title": "Fast pushing", "file": "MA13-fast-pushing.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Wave management, Resource management.", "action": "Choose the action you want after the push. Check whether there is still time to take it."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Fast pushing, also called shoving, means clearing enemy minions quickly so your wave advances. A common goal is a crash at the enemy turret before you recall or leave lane.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Use available attacks and suitable wave-clear abilities, but account for the resources and defensive tools you spend. Clearing the current enemies is not proof of a completed crash: a new wave or an opponent can stop your minions before the turret.<\/p><ol><li>Choose the action you want after the push. Check whether there is still time to take it.<\/li><li>Compare your ability to finish with the enemy’s ability to contest. Keep an escape or stop if the push becomes unsafe.<\/li><li>Observe where your wave ends. If it is held short of the turret, reassess the cost of leaving rather than assuming the lane is solved.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>You clear the visible wave to recall, but enemy reinforcements arrive before your minions reach the turret. Your health is low and the enemy laner is returning. You take the safe recall and accept a possible lane cost. Staying for a perfect crash would risk more than the missed farm.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma13-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma13-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Choose the next action. Recall, move or pressure. Spend attacks and abilities. Keep track of remaining tools. Did the wave reach turret?. An unfinished push has a cost.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for fast pushing; positions and timing are not game measurements.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Using every ability on the wave, then walking into river as if those abilities were still ready.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Check resources again after the clear. The next play must work with what you have left.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Review three times you cleared and left lane. Pause at departure and describe the actual wave position. Classify each as a completed crash, an unfinished push or unclear. Then check whether your intended next action was available and what tools you had left for it.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>There is no universal clear-time target. Champion tools, wave health, enemy interference and travel distance change the result. A safe exit can be correct even when the push is incomplete; name the cost rather than pretending it vanished.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/wave-management\">Wave management<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/resource-management\">Resource management<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/slow-pushing\">Slow pushing<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://dignitas.gg/articles/how-to-improve-wave-management-for-every-lane-in-league-of-legends\">Source and context<\/a>. Dignitas’s guide supports quick clearing, the cost in abilities and the risk of a failed push. The departure audit and hypothetical example are editorial synthesis. Current damage values and exact recall timings are outside this lesson.<\/p><p>Sources checked 4 November 2026. Examples and exercises are teaching proposals, not tested performance guarantees. No live patch certification or independent expert review is claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Fast pushing\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA13\n- Slug: fast-pushing\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Clear a wave quickly to create time for a specific next action.\n- Prerequisites: Wave management, Resource management.\n- Observable action: Choose the action you want after the push. Check whether there is still time to take it.\n\n## Short answer\n\nFast pushing, also called shoving, means clearing enemy minions quickly so your wave advances. A common goal is a crash at the enemy turret before you recall or leave lane.\n\n## How it works\n\nUse available attacks and suitable wave-clear abilities, but account for the resources and defensive tools you spend. Clearing the current enemies is not proof of a completed crash: a new wave or an opponent can stop your minions before the turret.\n\n1. Choose the action you want after the push. Check whether there is still time to take it.\n2. Compare your ability to finish with the enemy’s ability to contest. Keep an escape or stop if the push becomes unsafe.\n3. Observe where your wave ends. If it is held short of the turret, reassess the cost of leaving rather than assuming the lane is solved.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYou clear the visible wave to recall, but enemy reinforcements arrive before your minions reach the turret. Your health is low and the enemy laner is returning. You take the safe recall and accept a possible lane cost. Staying for a perfect crash would risk more than the missed farm.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA13 -->\n![Choose the next action. Recall, move or pressure. Spend attacks and abilities. Keep track of remaining tools. Did the wave reach turret?. An unfinished push has a cost.](../visuals/assets/ma13-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for fast pushing; positions and timing are not game measurements.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Using every ability on the wave, then walking into river as if those abilities were still ready.\n\n**Correction:** Check resources again after the clear. The next play must work with what you have left.\n\n## Practice\n\nReview three times you cleared and left lane. Pause at departure and describe the actual wave position. Classify each as a completed crash, an unfinished push or unclear. Then check whether your intended next action was available and what tools you had left for it.\n\n## Limits\n\nThere is no universal clear-time target. Champion tools, wave health, enemy interference and travel distance change the result. A safe exit can be correct even when the push is incomplete; name the cost rather than pretending it vanished.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Wave management](../macro/MA40-wave-management.md) · [Resource management](../micro/MI16-resource-management.md) · [Slow pushing](../macro/MA12-slow-pushing.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Source and context](https://dignitas.gg/articles/how-to-improve-wave-management-for-every-lane-in-league-of-legends). Dignitas’s guide supports quick clearing, the cost in abilities and the risk of a failed push. The departure audit and hypothetical example are editorial synthesis. Current damage values and exact recall timings are outside this lesson.\n\nSources checked 4 November 2026. Examples and exercises are teaching proposals, not tested performance guarantees. No live patch certification or independent expert review is claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "50dc06cf0c6d641f23b487e3d2ac621618cd3e03bc61afc5e6b7446ed98cb479"}, {"id": "MA14", "slug": "roaming", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Leave lane with a purpose, a safe route and a return condition.", "title": "Roaming", "file": "MA14-roaming.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Lane priority, Jungle tracking, Tempo.", "action": "State the purpose and compare it with the lane cost. Check health, resources and who can actually join."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Roaming means leaving your lane to affect another area of the map. A roam can support a teammate, threaten a gank or help secure vision. It spends time that could otherwise go to lane income and defence.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Start with the duty you leave behind. A push may create room to move, but an enemy can follow or pressure the lane. Supports must also consider whether their lane partner can stay safely. Choose a destination that still offers value when you arrive. A kill is not the only useful outcome, and walking farther is not proof of progress.<\/p><ol><li>State the purpose and compare it with the lane cost. Check health, resources and who can actually join.<\/li><li>Choose a route using observed enemies and vision. An unseen entrance is uncertain, even when it is the shortest path.<\/li><li>Set a return condition before departure. Cancel if the target retreats, allies disengage or the lane needs you back.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>After pushing mid, you move toward a possible bot-lane gank. Halfway there, the enemy bot lane backs away and your allies start recalling. You return through a known safe route instead of waiting in a bush for the original opportunity to return. The cancellation prevents a speculative roam from becoming a long absence.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma14-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma14-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Lane duty → useful target. Check the cost before moving. Target retreats; allies reset. The original chance has ended. Take a known safe route. Do not wait to justify the walk.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for roaming; positions and timing are not game measurements.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Staying because you have already spent time walking.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Decide from the remaining opportunity and cost. Time already spent does not make the next minute worthwhile.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>For three replay roams, stop at departure and write a purpose, expected lane cost and return condition. Resume in player view. Note the first visible change that should confirm or cancel the move. Compare your actual return with that moment.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>This drill measures whether you update a plan. It does not promise successful ganks. Enemy vision and communication remain uncertain; avoid treating a ping as confirmed help.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/lane-priority\">Lane priority<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/jungle-tracking\">Jungle tracking<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/tempo\">Tempo<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/lane-assignments\">Lane assignments<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://dignitas.gg/articles/the-art-of-roaming-in-league-of-legends\">Source and context<\/a>. Log’s original Dignitas roaming guide (16 January 2020) supports moving to influence another area, pushing for an opportunity and considering the lane partner. Its old item advice, role restrictions and risk-taking prescriptions are not adopted. Return conditions and drills are editorial synthesis.<\/p><p>Sources checked 4 November 2026. Examples and exercises are teaching proposals, not tested performance guarantees. No live patch certification or independent expert review is claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Roaming\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA14\n- Slug: roaming\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Leave lane with a purpose, a safe route and a return condition.\n- Prerequisites: Lane priority, Jungle tracking, Tempo.\n- Observable action: State the purpose and compare it with the lane cost. Check health, resources and who can actually join.\n\n## Short answer\n\nRoaming means leaving your lane to affect another area of the map. A roam can support a teammate, threaten a gank or help secure vision. It spends time that could otherwise go to lane income and defence.\n\n## How it works\n\nStart with the duty you leave behind. A push may create room to move, but an enemy can follow or pressure the lane. Supports must also consider whether their lane partner can stay safely. Choose a destination that still offers value when you arrive. A kill is not the only useful outcome, and walking farther is not proof of progress.\n\n1. State the purpose and compare it with the lane cost. Check health, resources and who can actually join.\n2. Choose a route using observed enemies and vision. An unseen entrance is uncertain, even when it is the shortest path.\n3. Set a return condition before departure. Cancel if the target retreats, allies disengage or the lane needs you back.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nAfter pushing mid, you move toward a possible bot-lane gank. Halfway there, the enemy bot lane backs away and your allies start recalling. You return through a known safe route instead of waiting in a bush for the original opportunity to return. The cancellation prevents a speculative roam from becoming a long absence.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA14 -->\n![Lane duty → useful target. Check the cost before moving. Target retreats; allies reset. The original chance has ended. Take a known safe route. Do not wait to justify the walk.](../visuals/assets/ma14-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for roaming; positions and timing are not game measurements.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Staying because you have already spent time walking.\n\n**Correction:** Decide from the remaining opportunity and cost. Time already spent does not make the next minute worthwhile.\n\n## Practice\n\nFor three replay roams, stop at departure and write a purpose, expected lane cost and return condition. Resume in player view. Note the first visible change that should confirm or cancel the move. Compare your actual return with that moment.\n\n## Limits\n\nThis drill measures whether you update a plan. It does not promise successful ganks. Enemy vision and communication remain uncertain; avoid treating a ping as confirmed help.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Lane priority](../macro/MA04-lane-priority.md) · [Jungle tracking](../macro/MA06-jungle-tracking.md) · [Tempo](../macro/MA22-tempo.md) · [Lane assignments](../macro/MA25-lane-assignments.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Source and context](https://dignitas.gg/articles/the-art-of-roaming-in-league-of-legends). Log’s original Dignitas roaming guide (16 January 2020) supports moving to influence another area, pushing for an opportunity and considering the lane partner. Its old item advice, role restrictions and risk-taking prescriptions are not adopted. Return conditions and drills are editorial synthesis.\n\nSources checked 4 November 2026. Examples and exercises are teaching proposals, not tested performance guarantees. No live patch certification or independent expert review is claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "a2787498f99c75d7ef16f47c8ffdce801d7d0a9c848feff562008ed97f3b59b2"}, {"id": "MA15", "slug": "split-pushing", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Coordinate side-lane pressure with what teammates can do elsewhere.", "title": "Split pushing", "file": "MA15-split-pushing.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Rotations, Jungle tracking.", "action": "Check the group’s position and readiness before advancing. State what they could gain if an enemy answers you."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Split pushing means applying lane pressure apart from the main group so the enemy must divide attention. A common shape is one player in a side lane and four elsewhere. The value comes from connected pressure across the map.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>The side player needs a credible threat and a way to respond when enemies move. The group needs a useful action while defenders are occupied, or enough safety to wait. Riot describes split pushing as a strategic alternative to a full team fight, with rotations and fights as counterplay. Being alone in a side lane is not enough to create that tradeoff.<\/p><ol><li>Check the group’s position and readiness before advancing. State what they could gain if an enemy answers you.<\/li><li>Track visible defenders and possible approaches. Move back while you still have an exit when missing enemies could reach you.<\/li><li>Reassess the exchange. If the group is recalling or cannot hold safely, delay pressure or regroup rather than demanding that they survive.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>You reach a side wave while your teammates are still buying items. Advancing to the turret now would let enemies focus on you without giving anything up elsewhere. You collect safely and wait for your group to take position. Later, a defender appears against you; you reassess the duel and your teammates’ opportunity before moving farther.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma15-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma15-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Solo player threatens turret. A defender must choose. Group is ready to act. Pressure must overlap in time. Enemies leave vision. Retreat before the exit closes.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for split pushing; positions and timing are not game measurements.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Blaming the group for losing a fight while assuming any side-lane farm counted as useful pressure.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Compare timing and actual threats. Ask what the enemy had to give up to answer you, and whether your group could act then.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Review three side-lane advances. Pause before crossing into danger and mark the group’s position, visible defenders and your exit. State one useful action available to the group. If you cannot name one, record why you continued and what you could have done instead.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>A strong duelist can still be caught, and the group can be forced into a fight. No formation guarantees a profitable trade. This lesson does not prescribe current items, Teleport timings or champion matchups.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/rotations\">Rotations<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/jungle-tracking\">Jungle tracking<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/cross-map-trading\">Cross-map trading<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-march-19/\">Source and context<\/a>. Riot Scruffy’s Split Pushing Goals (19 March 2021) is primary design context for solo pressure, distributed formations and counterplay. The timing audit, example and drill are editorial synthesis, not a claim about the current strongest strategy.<\/p><p>Sources checked 4 November 2026. Examples and exercises are teaching proposals, not tested performance guarantees. No live patch certification or independent expert review is claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Split pushing\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA15\n- Slug: split-pushing\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Coordinate side-lane pressure with what teammates can do elsewhere.\n- Prerequisites: Rotations, Jungle tracking.\n- Observable action: Check the group’s position and readiness before advancing. State what they could gain if an enemy answers you.\n\n## Short answer\n\nSplit pushing means applying lane pressure apart from the main group so the enemy must divide attention. A common shape is one player in a side lane and four elsewhere. The value comes from connected pressure across the map.\n\n## How it works\n\nThe side player needs a credible threat and a way to respond when enemies move. The group needs a useful action while defenders are occupied, or enough safety to wait. Riot describes split pushing as a strategic alternative to a full team fight, with rotations and fights as counterplay. Being alone in a side lane is not enough to create that tradeoff.\n\n1. Check the group’s position and readiness before advancing. State what they could gain if an enemy answers you.\n2. Track visible defenders and possible approaches. Move back while you still have an exit when missing enemies could reach you.\n3. Reassess the exchange. If the group is recalling or cannot hold safely, delay pressure or regroup rather than demanding that they survive.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYou reach a side wave while your teammates are still buying items. Advancing to the turret now would let enemies focus on you without giving anything up elsewhere. You collect safely and wait for your group to take position. Later, a defender appears against you; you reassess the duel and your teammates’ opportunity before moving farther.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA15 -->\n![Solo player threatens turret. A defender must choose. Group is ready to act. Pressure must overlap in time. Enemies leave vision. Retreat before the exit closes.](../visuals/assets/ma15-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for split pushing; positions and timing are not game measurements.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Blaming the group for losing a fight while assuming any side-lane farm counted as useful pressure.\n\n**Correction:** Compare timing and actual threats. Ask what the enemy had to give up to answer you, and whether your group could act then.\n\n## Practice\n\nReview three side-lane advances. Pause before crossing into danger and mark the group’s position, visible defenders and your exit. State one useful action available to the group. If you cannot name one, record why you continued and what you could have done instead.\n\n## Limits\n\nA strong duelist can still be caught, and the group can be forced into a fight. No formation guarantees a profitable trade. This lesson does not prescribe current items, Teleport timings or champion matchups.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Rotations](../macro/MA24-rotations.md) · [Jungle tracking](../macro/MA06-jungle-tracking.md) · [Cross-map trading](../macro/MA26-cross-map-trading.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Source and context](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-march-19/). Riot Scruffy’s Split Pushing Goals (19 March 2021) is primary design context for solo pressure, distributed formations and counterplay. The timing audit, example and drill are editorial synthesis, not a claim about the current strongest strategy.\n\nSources checked 4 November 2026. Examples and exercises are teaching proposals, not tested performance guarantees. No live patch certification or independent expert review is claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "cbb84b98f435d9488a5afa7b8a33890259f578c60e05b50124751e5f278c5820"}, {"id": "MA16", "slug": "wave-crashing", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Confirm that a push reaches the turret before spending the time it creates.", "title": "Wave crashing", "file": "MA16-wave-crashing.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Fast pushing, Slow pushing.", "action": "Choose why you want the crash: a recall, movement or pressure."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>A wave crash occurs when an advancing minion wave reaches the enemy turret and is taken up by its defence. Fast pushing describes how you clear; crashing describes where the wave ends.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>An opponent or arriving wave can stop a push short. Watch the actual meeting point before leaving.<\/p><ol><li>Choose why you want the crash: a recall, movement or pressure.<\/li><li>Check whether you can finish safely before another wave or enemy arrives.<\/li><li>Observe the wave reaching the turret, then recheck your next action.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>You clear mid and plan to help in river. Your minions stop against new enemies outside the turret. Your teammate retreats, so you abandon the river move. You now decide whether to finish the push or recall based on your health; the original plan no longer decides for you.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma16-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma16-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Your minions → next enemy wave. Clearing is only the start. New enemies stop the advance. Short of turret = unfinished. Your wave → enemy turret. Now reassess the next action.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for wave crashing; positions and timing are not game measurements.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Calling every cleared wave a crash.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Check the minions, not just your last attack.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>In three replay departures, pause before leaving lane. Draw the turret and the wave meeting point. Predict whether the push will finish without you. Watch to the next collision and record what stopped or completed it.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>A crash does not guarantee a free recall or a safe roam. An enemy can still intercept you. Do not risk a death just to make the wave picture look correct.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/fast-pushing\">Fast pushing<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/slow-pushing\">Slow pushing<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/wave-bouncing\">Wave bouncing<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://dignitas.gg/articles/how-to-improve-wave-management-for-every-lane-in-league-of-legends\">Source and context<\/a>. EkardD’s original coaching guide supports the distinction between clearing and completing a crash. No fixed wave number or timing is adopted.<\/p><p>Sources checked 4 November 2026. Examples and exercises are teaching proposals, not tested performance guarantees. No live patch certification or independent expert review is claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Wave crashing\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA16\n- Slug: wave-crashing\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Confirm that a push reaches the turret before spending the time it creates.\n- Prerequisites: Fast pushing, Slow pushing.\n- Observable action: Choose why you want the crash: a recall, movement or pressure.\n\n## Short answer\n\nA wave crash occurs when an advancing minion wave reaches the enemy turret and is taken up by its defence. Fast pushing describes how you clear; crashing describes where the wave ends.\n\n## How it works\n\nAn opponent or arriving wave can stop a push short. Watch the actual meeting point before leaving.\n\n1. Choose why you want the crash: a recall, movement or pressure.\n2. Check whether you can finish safely before another wave or enemy arrives.\n3. Observe the wave reaching the turret, then recheck your next action.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYou clear mid and plan to help in river. Your minions stop against new enemies outside the turret. Your teammate retreats, so you abandon the river move. You now decide whether to finish the push or recall based on your health; the original plan no longer decides for you.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA16 -->\n![Your minions → next enemy wave. Clearing is only the start. New enemies stop the advance. Short of turret = unfinished. Your wave → enemy turret. Now reassess the next action.](../visuals/assets/ma16-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for wave crashing; positions and timing are not game measurements.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Calling every cleared wave a crash.\n\n**Correction:** Check the minions, not just your last attack.\n\n## Practice\n\nIn three replay departures, pause before leaving lane. Draw the turret and the wave meeting point. Predict whether the push will finish without you. Watch to the next collision and record what stopped or completed it.\n\n## Limits\n\nA crash does not guarantee a free recall or a safe roam. An enemy can still intercept you. Do not risk a death just to make the wave picture look correct.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Fast pushing](../macro/MA13-fast-pushing.md) · [Slow pushing](../macro/MA12-slow-pushing.md) · [Wave bouncing](../macro/MA17-wave-bouncing.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Source and context](https://dignitas.gg/articles/how-to-improve-wave-management-for-every-lane-in-league-of-legends). EkardD’s original coaching guide supports the distinction between clearing and completing a crash. No fixed wave number or timing is adopted.\n\nSources checked 4 November 2026. Examples and exercises are teaching proposals, not tested performance guarantees. No live patch certification or independent expert review is claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "6beb631933d0fd04141713e75b2de2535fb5347f9c7fbd758ea9cbe1356117a3"}, {"id": "MA17", "slug": "wave-bouncing", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Read the returning wave after a crash instead of assuming an automatic reset.", "title": "Wave bouncing", "file": "MA17-wave-bouncing.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Wave crashing.", "action": "Observe what survives the crash and where reinforcements meet."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>A wave bounce is a reversal in the direction of a lane’s push, commonly after a wave crashes. The next waves can begin moving back toward the player who pushed.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Arriving reinforcements, surviving minions and champion damage determine the next direction. A crash can set up a return push; it does not guarantee one.<\/p><ol><li>Observe what survives the crash and where reinforcements meet.<\/li><li>Predict the next push direction before adding damage.<\/li><li>Recheck after the opponent clears or holds the wave.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>You return from base expecting the lane to come toward you. Instead, your opponent clears quickly and the waves meet near the centre. You change your farming position to match what is visible instead of standing where a remembered sequence says the wave should be.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma17-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma17-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Your wave → enemy turret. Observe the completed crash. Enemy reinforcements arrive. Watch survivors and damage. Next wave may push toward you. Confirm; do not assume.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for wave bouncing; positions and timing are not game measurements.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Attacking every returning minion while expecting the wave to keep moving toward you.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Decide whether to collect, hold or push before adding extra damage.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Choose three replay crashes. Hide the future timeline, predict the next meeting point, and name the uncertainty. Watch two collisions. Record one event that confirmed or changed your prediction.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>The return can be disrupted. This is an observation exercise, not a fixed minion recipe or a promise that a recall loses no farm.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/wave-crashing\">Wave crashing<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/freezing\">Freezing<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://dignitas.gg/articles/how-to-improve-wave-management-for-every-lane-in-league-of-legends\">Source and context<\/a>. EkardD’s guide describes a crash followed by a return wave. Conditions and replay exercises here are teaching synthesis, not a universal sequence.<\/p><p>Sources checked 4 November 2026. Examples and exercises are teaching proposals, not tested performance guarantees. No live patch certification or independent expert review is claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Wave bouncing\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA17\n- Slug: wave-bouncing\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Read the returning wave after a crash instead of assuming an automatic reset.\n- Prerequisites: Wave crashing.\n- Observable action: Observe what survives the crash and where reinforcements meet.\n\n## Short answer\n\nA wave bounce is a reversal in the direction of a lane’s push, commonly after a wave crashes. The next waves can begin moving back toward the player who pushed.\n\n## How it works\n\nArriving reinforcements, surviving minions and champion damage determine the next direction. A crash can set up a return push; it does not guarantee one.\n\n1. Observe what survives the crash and where reinforcements meet.\n2. Predict the next push direction before adding damage.\n3. Recheck after the opponent clears or holds the wave.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYou return from base expecting the lane to come toward you. Instead, your opponent clears quickly and the waves meet near the centre. You change your farming position to match what is visible instead of standing where a remembered sequence says the wave should be.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA17 -->\n![Your wave → enemy turret. Observe the completed crash. Enemy reinforcements arrive. Watch survivors and damage. Next wave may push toward you. Confirm; do not assume.](../visuals/assets/ma17-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for wave bouncing; positions and timing are not game measurements.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Attacking every returning minion while expecting the wave to keep moving toward you.\n\n**Correction:** Decide whether to collect, hold or push before adding extra damage.\n\n## Practice\n\nChoose three replay crashes. Hide the future timeline, predict the next meeting point, and name the uncertainty. Watch two collisions. Record one event that confirmed or changed your prediction.\n\n## Limits\n\nThe return can be disrupted. This is an observation exercise, not a fixed minion recipe or a promise that a recall loses no farm.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Wave crashing](../macro/MA16-wave-crashing.md) · [Freezing](../macro/MA11-freezing.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Source and context](https://dignitas.gg/articles/how-to-improve-wave-management-for-every-lane-in-league-of-legends). EkardD’s guide describes a crash followed by a return wave. Conditions and replay exercises here are teaching synthesis, not a universal sequence.\n\nSources checked 4 November 2026. Examples and exercises are teaching proposals, not tested performance guarantees. No live patch certification or independent expert review is claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "4de1feaa9ee71c91c9ffc69a17e698ae0c37243678ed9c2c51d07fe3d8f9f51c"}, {"id": "MA18", "slug": "flanking", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Use a side approach without arriving alone or too late.", "title": "Flanking", "file": "MA18-flanking.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Engaging, Vision denial.", "action": "Choose an angle and identify when the group can apply pressure."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Flanking means approaching from a side or rear angle instead of joining the main front. It can change which opponents you can reach and where they can retreat.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>A flank costs travel time and separates you from allies. Coordinate the arrival with the group’s pressure. A hidden route is useful only if you can reach a useful position safely; lack of visible enemies does not prove the route is clear.<\/p><ol><li>Choose an angle and identify when the group can apply pressure.<\/li><li>Check route information and preserve a way out.<\/li><li>Recheck before revealing yourself; leave if the group retreats.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>You move around a river wall while teammates face the enemy in lane. Before you arrive, your group backs away. Entering now would make you the only threat. You return by a known route and look for another window.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma18-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma18-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Allied group ↔ enemy group. Keep pressure connected. You take a separate approach. Travel time creates a gap. Group retreats before you arrive. Cancel the isolated entry.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for flanking; positions and timing are not game measurements.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Taking the longest unseen route simply because it reaches the backline.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Compare arrival time with the group’s ability to stay in the fight.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Review three side approaches. Pause at departure and at arrival. Mark your group’s position at both times. Explain whether the two threats overlapped, and identify the first moment you could have cancelled.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>Enemy vision may reveal you. A side angle is not automatically better for your champion, and leaving the group can expose an ally.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/engaging\">Engaging<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/vision-denial\">Vision denial<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/collapsing\">Collapsing<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://dignitas.gg/articles/5-tips-to-improve-your-teamfighting-ft-team-dignitas\">Source and context<\/a>. The original guide uses team positioning and a flank example to explain coordinated fight roles. Routes and cancellation exercises here are hypothetical.<\/p><p>Sources checked 4 November 2026. Examples and exercises are teaching proposals, not tested performance guarantees. No live patch certification or independent expert review is claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Flanking\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA18\n- Slug: flanking\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Use a side approach without arriving alone or too late.\n- Prerequisites: Engaging, Vision denial.\n- Observable action: Choose an angle and identify when the group can apply pressure.\n\n## Short answer\n\nFlanking means approaching from a side or rear angle instead of joining the main front. It can change which opponents you can reach and where they can retreat.\n\n## How it works\n\nA flank costs travel time and separates you from allies. Coordinate the arrival with the group’s pressure. A hidden route is useful only if you can reach a useful position safely; lack of visible enemies does not prove the route is clear.\n\n1. Choose an angle and identify when the group can apply pressure.\n2. Check route information and preserve a way out.\n3. Recheck before revealing yourself; leave if the group retreats.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYou move around a river wall while teammates face the enemy in lane. Before you arrive, your group backs away. Entering now would make you the only threat. You return by a known route and look for another window.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA18 -->\n![Allied group ↔ enemy group. Keep pressure connected. You take a separate approach. Travel time creates a gap. Group retreats before you arrive. Cancel the isolated entry.](../visuals/assets/ma18-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for flanking; positions and timing are not game measurements.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Taking the longest unseen route simply because it reaches the backline.\n\n**Correction:** Compare arrival time with the group’s ability to stay in the fight.\n\n## Practice\n\nReview three side approaches. Pause at departure and at arrival. Mark your group’s position at both times. Explain whether the two threats overlapped, and identify the first moment you could have cancelled.\n\n## Limits\n\nEnemy vision may reveal you. A side angle is not automatically better for your champion, and leaving the group can expose an ally.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Engaging](../micro/MI13-engaging.md) · [Vision denial](../macro/MA19-vision-denial.md) · [Collapsing](../macro/MA32-collapsing.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Source and context](https://dignitas.gg/articles/5-tips-to-improve-your-teamfighting-ft-team-dignitas). The original guide uses team positioning and a flank example to explain coordinated fight roles. Routes and cancellation exercises here are hypothetical.\n\nSources checked 4 November 2026. Examples and exercises are teaching proposals, not tested performance guarantees. No live patch certification or independent expert review is claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "a1b6397ba2c54aab855a4f51d20d65adba9ade3cae9666e239334e09ed0dc2ba"}, {"id": "MA19", "slug": "vision-denial", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Remove enemy information when your team can safely use the uncertainty.", "title": "Vision denial", "file": "MA19-vision-denial.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Warding.", "action": "Name the next action that benefits from denying information."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Vision denial means reducing the enemy’s ability to observe an area, often by finding and clearing their wards. It complements warding: one gives your team information, while the other limits the enemy’s.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Choose the area around your next action. Enter with enough information and support to leave safely. Clearing one ward does not prove that every route is unseen. Enemy champions and other sources of vision may still reveal you.<\/p><ol><li>Name the next action that benefits from denying information.<\/li><li>Check entrances and nearby allies before approaching a ward.<\/li><li>After clearing, reassess what the enemy can still see and what your team can do.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>Your group wants to approach an objective. A ward is visible across an unsafe entrance. Instead of walking alone to remove it, you wait for allies and check nearby threats. If the group recalls, you abandon the clear because the next action has changed.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma19-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma19-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Team → planned objective. Which area needs denial? Enemy ward across an entrance. Check help before crossing. Ward gone; other sight possible. Use uncertainty carefully.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for vision denial; positions and timing are not game measurements.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Walking into danger because a ward is a guaranteed small reward.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Compare the clear with the risk and the team action it supports.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>For three ward-clearing attempts, pause before entry. Name the intended benefit, possible enemy approach and exit. After the clear, check whether the team used the area or whether the attempt only consumed time.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>Removal tools have different detection rules. This lesson does not claim that one tool reveals all wards, traps or stealth. Check current tool descriptions in game.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/warding\">Warding<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/jungle-tracking\">Jungle tracking<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://oce.leagueoflegends.com/en/featured/preseason-2017/assassins\">Source and context<\/a>. Riot’s historical season update establishes Control Wards as an anti-vision tool. It is design context, not evidence of current ranges or detection rules. The route audit is editorial guidance.<\/p><p>Sources checked 4 November 2026. Examples and exercises are teaching proposals, not tested performance guarantees. No live patch certification or independent expert review is claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Vision denial\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA19\n- Slug: vision-denial\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Remove enemy information when your team can safely use the uncertainty.\n- Prerequisites: Warding.\n- Observable action: Name the next action that benefits from denying information.\n\n## Short answer\n\nVision denial means reducing the enemy’s ability to observe an area, often by finding and clearing their wards. It complements warding: one gives your team information, while the other limits the enemy’s.\n\n## How it works\n\nChoose the area around your next action. Enter with enough information and support to leave safely. Clearing one ward does not prove that every route is unseen. Enemy champions and other sources of vision may still reveal you.\n\n1. Name the next action that benefits from denying information.\n2. Check entrances and nearby allies before approaching a ward.\n3. After clearing, reassess what the enemy can still see and what your team can do.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYour group wants to approach an objective. A ward is visible across an unsafe entrance. Instead of walking alone to remove it, you wait for allies and check nearby threats. If the group recalls, you abandon the clear because the next action has changed.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA19 -->\n![Team → planned objective. Which area needs denial? Enemy ward across an entrance. Check help before crossing. Ward gone; other sight possible. Use uncertainty carefully.](../visuals/assets/ma19-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for vision denial; positions and timing are not game measurements.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Walking into danger because a ward is a guaranteed small reward.\n\n**Correction:** Compare the clear with the risk and the team action it supports.\n\n## Practice\n\nFor three ward-clearing attempts, pause before entry. Name the intended benefit, possible enemy approach and exit. After the clear, check whether the team used the area or whether the attempt only consumed time.\n\n## Limits\n\nRemoval tools have different detection rules. This lesson does not claim that one tool reveals all wards, traps or stealth. Check current tool descriptions in game.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Warding](../macro/MA05-warding.md) · [Jungle tracking](../macro/MA06-jungle-tracking.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Source and context](https://oce.leagueoflegends.com/en/featured/preseason-2017/assassins). Riot’s historical season update establishes Control Wards as an anti-vision tool. It is design context, not evidence of current ranges or detection rules. The route audit is editorial guidance.\n\nSources checked 4 November 2026. Examples and exercises are teaching proposals, not tested performance guarantees. No live patch certification or independent expert review is claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "07f5e3f832085a317885aa217dccc216dd7a2ea31c8c61d4bad669253cc59a30"}, {"id": "MA20", "slug": "power-spikes", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Recognise a meaningful increase in usable strength.", "title": "Power spikes", "file": "MA20-power-spikes.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Experience, Itemization.", "action": "Identify the new tool or purchase."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>A power spike is a point where a champion gains a significant new advantage, often from a level, ability unlock or completed purchase. Its value is relative to the opponent and the fight you can actually take.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Look for a change that affects the next interaction: a new tool, more survivability or enough damage to threaten. Gold in your inventory is not the same as an item already bought. Compare the opponent&#x27;s visible level and items, then check whether your advantage can be used safely.<\/p><ol><li>Identify the new tool or purchase.<\/li><li>Compare the opponent&#x27;s current strength.<\/li><li>Choose a useful action while the advantage matters.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>You return with a completed item while your opponent still holds components. You can pressure more, but their jungler appears nearby. You keep the advantage for a safer exchange instead of forcing a fight into two opponents.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma20-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma20-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Decision diagram for power spikes: GAIN — Level or purchase changes tools. COMPARE — Enemy strength also matters. USE — Choose a safe opportunity.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for power spikes; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Treating a strong level or item as permission to fight anywhere.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Combine the strength change with map information and access.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Review three purchases or level-ups. State exactly what each change enables, then identify an available action and one reason to delay it. Avoid judging only by whether you won the next fight.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>Spikes vary by champion and patch. This lesson does not claim a fixed winning level, item or matchup.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/experience\">Experience<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/itemization\">Itemization<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/warding\">Warding<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/how-to-play/\">Riot: How to Play<\/a>. Primary context for levels, purchases, structures and the base objective. It does not validate the decision routine taught here.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Power spikes\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA20\n- Slug: power-spikes\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Recognise a meaningful increase in usable strength.\n- Prerequisites: Experience, Itemization.\n- Observable action: Identify the new tool or purchase.\n\n## Short answer\n\nA power spike is a point where a champion gains a significant new advantage, often from a level, ability unlock or completed purchase. Its value is relative to the opponent and the fight you can actually take.\n\n## How it works\n\nLook for a change that affects the next interaction: a new tool, more survivability or enough damage to threaten. Gold in your inventory is not the same as an item already bought. Compare the opponent's visible level and items, then check whether your advantage can be used safely.\n\n1. Identify the new tool or purchase.\n2. Compare the opponent's current strength.\n3. Choose a useful action while the advantage matters.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYou return with a completed item while your opponent still holds components. You can pressure more, but their jungler appears nearby. You keep the advantage for a safer exchange instead of forcing a fight into two opponents.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA20 -->\n![Decision diagram for power spikes: GAIN — Level or purchase changes tools. COMPARE — Enemy strength also matters. USE — Choose a safe opportunity.](../visuals/assets/ma20-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for power spikes; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Treating a strong level or item as permission to fight anywhere.\n\n**Correction:** Combine the strength change with map information and access.\n\n## Practice\n\nReview three purchases or level-ups. State exactly what each change enables, then identify an available action and one reason to delay it. Avoid judging only by whether you won the next fight.\n\n## Limits\n\nSpikes vary by champion and patch. This lesson does not claim a fixed winning level, item or matchup.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Experience](../macro/MA38-experience.md) · [Itemization](../macro/MA21-itemization.md) · [Warding](../macro/MA05-warding.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Riot: How to Play](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/how-to-play/). Primary context for levels, purchases, structures and the base objective. It does not validate the decision routine taught here.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "b959734ef66f5c5d73202a264afb0294900b57eb53f5c5a58dcf82c5cb66e828"}, {"id": "MA21", "slug": "itemization", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Choose purchases to solve a specific problem in the match.", "title": "Itemization", "file": "MA21-itemization.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Recalling.", "action": "Name the problem the purchase should solve."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Itemization is selecting purchases and their order to support your champion&#x27;s job against the current threats. It includes deciding whether an immediate component or a later completed item best serves the next action.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Identify what stops you contributing: insufficient damage, lack of survival or a particular enemy tool. Read current item effects and compare useful stats rather than buying from memory alone. A counter purchase can help, but giving up too much of your main function also has a cost.<\/p><ol><li>Name the problem the purchase should solve.<\/li><li>Read the current effect and price.<\/li><li>Check how the purchase changes your next fight.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>You repeatedly die before using your second spell rotation. At the shop you compare a defensive option with more damage. You choose based on the damage source and your team job, not merely the last player who killed you.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma21-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma21-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Decision diagram for itemization: PROBLEM — What prevents contribution?. CHOICE — Compare current item effects. CHECK — Did the next fight improve?.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for itemization; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Copying a build without checking the opposing threats.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Keep a default plan but explain any change with a match-specific reason.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Review two recalls. Record the problem you intended to solve, your chosen purchase and a plausible alternative. Watch the next fight for evidence that the purchase affected that problem.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>Items and prices change. No best-build or current numerical recommendation is made here; use the in-game shop for details.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/recalling\">Recalling<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/power-spikes\">Power spikes<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/how-to-play/\">Riot: How to Play<\/a>. Primary context for levels, purchases, structures and the base objective. It does not validate the decision routine taught here.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Itemization\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA21\n- Slug: itemization\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Choose purchases to solve a specific problem in the match.\n- Prerequisites: Recalling.\n- Observable action: Name the problem the purchase should solve.\n\n## Short answer\n\nItemization is selecting purchases and their order to support your champion's job against the current threats. It includes deciding whether an immediate component or a later completed item best serves the next action.\n\n## How it works\n\nIdentify what stops you contributing: insufficient damage, lack of survival or a particular enemy tool. Read current item effects and compare useful stats rather than buying from memory alone. A counter purchase can help, but giving up too much of your main function also has a cost.\n\n1. Name the problem the purchase should solve.\n2. Read the current effect and price.\n3. Check how the purchase changes your next fight.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYou repeatedly die before using your second spell rotation. At the shop you compare a defensive option with more damage. You choose based on the damage source and your team job, not merely the last player who killed you.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA21 -->\n![Decision diagram for itemization: PROBLEM — What prevents contribution?. CHOICE — Compare current item effects. CHECK — Did the next fight improve?.](../visuals/assets/ma21-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for itemization; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Copying a build without checking the opposing threats.\n\n**Correction:** Keep a default plan but explain any change with a match-specific reason.\n\n## Practice\n\nReview two recalls. Record the problem you intended to solve, your chosen purchase and a plausible alternative. Watch the next fight for evidence that the purchase affected that problem.\n\n## Limits\n\nItems and prices change. No best-build or current numerical recommendation is made here; use the in-game shop for details.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Recalling](../macro/MA02-recalling.md) · [Power spikes](../macro/MA20-power-spikes.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Riot: How to Play](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/how-to-play/). Primary context for levels, purchases, structures and the base objective. It does not validate the decision routine taught here.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "64858d00829acd4ba2a9b43b070fa006fbbfabb5e3f7457616f211dd609b3646"}, {"id": "MA22", "slug": "tempo", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Use a window to act while the opponent must respond elsewhere.", "title": "Tempo", "file": "MA22-tempo.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Lane priority, Recalling.", "action": "Identify what currently delays the opponent."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Tempo is a temporary advantage in the timing of useful actions. You may be able to move first while an opponent clears a wave, returns from base or answers pressure. The advantage expires as they finish that task.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>A tempo lead needs an action you can complete within the window. Moving first without a destination can waste it. Include travel, health, resource and return costs. The opponent may choose to give up the wave and follow, so keep watching rather than assuming they are occupied.<\/p><ol><li>Identify what currently delays the opponent.<\/li><li>Choose an action that fits the likely window.<\/li><li>Finish or leave when they regain freedom to move.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>You crash a wave and move to help establish nearby vision. The opponent clears faster than expected and leaves lane. You return with your teammate instead of extending the route as if the original window still held.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma22-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma22-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Decision diagram for tempo: WINDOW — Opponent is occupied. ACTION — Complete a nearby useful task. CLOSE — Opponent can move again.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for tempo; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Treating first movement as a permanent advantage.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Track when the other team can act again.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Review three moves after a push or recall. Record the delaying task, your intended action and the moment the window closed. Note one unnecessary stop that consumed the window.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>Tempo is contextual, not a fixed number of seconds. A move can be early yet unsafe or useless.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/lane-priority\">Lane priority<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/recalling\">Recalling<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/resets\">Resets<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-march-19/\">Riot: Split Pushing Goals<\/a>. Primary design context for distributed pressure, rotations and counterplay. Its historical champion examples are not current matchup advice.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Tempo\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA22\n- Slug: tempo\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Use a window to act while the opponent must respond elsewhere.\n- Prerequisites: Lane priority, Recalling.\n- Observable action: Identify what currently delays the opponent.\n\n## Short answer\n\nTempo is a temporary advantage in the timing of useful actions. You may be able to move first while an opponent clears a wave, returns from base or answers pressure. The advantage expires as they finish that task.\n\n## How it works\n\nA tempo lead needs an action you can complete within the window. Moving first without a destination can waste it. Include travel, health, resource and return costs. The opponent may choose to give up the wave and follow, so keep watching rather than assuming they are occupied.\n\n1. Identify what currently delays the opponent.\n2. Choose an action that fits the likely window.\n3. Finish or leave when they regain freedom to move.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYou crash a wave and move to help establish nearby vision. The opponent clears faster than expected and leaves lane. You return with your teammate instead of extending the route as if the original window still held.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA22 -->\n![Decision diagram for tempo: WINDOW — Opponent is occupied. ACTION — Complete a nearby useful task. CLOSE — Opponent can move again.](../visuals/assets/ma22-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for tempo; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Treating first movement as a permanent advantage.\n\n**Correction:** Track when the other team can act again.\n\n## Practice\n\nReview three moves after a push or recall. Record the delaying task, your intended action and the moment the window closed. Note one unnecessary stop that consumed the window.\n\n## Limits\n\nTempo is contextual, not a fixed number of seconds. A move can be early yet unsafe or useless.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Lane priority](../macro/MA04-lane-priority.md) · [Recalling](../macro/MA02-recalling.md) · [Resets](../macro/MA23-resets.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Riot: Split Pushing Goals](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-march-19/). Primary design context for distributed pressure, rotations and counterplay. Its historical champion examples are not current matchup advice.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "f2b6c70449133525b45eda19a289ae23112db8cb9c77aefa9679ab6af6094a39"}, {"id": "MA23", "slug": "resets", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Coordinate a return to base with the team's next action.", "title": "Resets", "file": "MA23-resets.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Recalling, Itemization, Tempo.", "action": "Check the next team action and who needs base."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>A reset is a return to base to restore resources, make purchases and prepare to re-enter the map. This lesson focuses on team timing; Recalling covers the individual departure and channel decision.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Check who needs to buy or recover before the next contest. If one player starts a fight while others reset, the team can lose access despite holding resources. Communicate the return and choose a realistic next meeting point. A synchronised reset still needs safe wave and map conditions.<\/p><ol><li>Check the next team action and who needs base.<\/li><li>Leave with enough time to return usefully.<\/li><li>Wait for actual readiness before recommitting.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>Your team wins a small fight but has low health and unspent gold. You signal a reset before the next objective setup. One ally stays for a wave, so the group delays entering dark river until they can join.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma23-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma23-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Decision diagram for resets: LEAVE — Spend and recover. RETURN — Check who is actually ready. GROUP — Begin the next action together.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for resets; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Assuming everyone is ready because you finished shopping.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Check positions and resources after the return.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Review two team recalls before contests. Note each player&#x27;s departure, return and first useful action. Identify whether staggered timing caused a fight or vision task to happen short-handed.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>Perfectly synchronised recalls are not always possible. Do not abandon a necessary defence merely to match the group.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/recalling\">Recalling<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/itemization\">Itemization<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/tempo\">Tempo<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/roaming\">Roaming<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-march-19/\">Riot: Split Pushing Goals<\/a>. Primary design context for distributed pressure, rotations and counterplay. Its historical champion examples are not current matchup advice.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Resets\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA23\n- Slug: resets\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Coordinate a return to base with the team's next action.\n- Prerequisites: Recalling, Itemization, Tempo.\n- Observable action: Check the next team action and who needs base.\n\n## Short answer\n\nA reset is a return to base to restore resources, make purchases and prepare to re-enter the map. This lesson focuses on team timing; Recalling covers the individual departure and channel decision.\n\n## How it works\n\nCheck who needs to buy or recover before the next contest. If one player starts a fight while others reset, the team can lose access despite holding resources. Communicate the return and choose a realistic next meeting point. A synchronised reset still needs safe wave and map conditions.\n\n1. Check the next team action and who needs base.\n2. Leave with enough time to return usefully.\n3. Wait for actual readiness before recommitting.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYour team wins a small fight but has low health and unspent gold. You signal a reset before the next objective setup. One ally stays for a wave, so the group delays entering dark river until they can join.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA23 -->\n![Decision diagram for resets: LEAVE — Spend and recover. RETURN — Check who is actually ready. GROUP — Begin the next action together.](../visuals/assets/ma23-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for resets; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Assuming everyone is ready because you finished shopping.\n\n**Correction:** Check positions and resources after the return.\n\n## Practice\n\nReview two team recalls before contests. Note each player's departure, return and first useful action. Identify whether staggered timing caused a fight or vision task to happen short-handed.\n\n## Limits\n\nPerfectly synchronised recalls are not always possible. Do not abandon a necessary defence merely to match the group.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Recalling](../macro/MA02-recalling.md) · [Itemization](../macro/MA21-itemization.md) · [Tempo](../macro/MA22-tempo.md) · [Roaming](../macro/MA14-roaming.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Riot: Split Pushing Goals](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-march-19/). Primary design context for distributed pressure, rotations and counterplay. Its historical champion examples are not current matchup advice.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "9b004487eb79a49c57e8691cb391b5375c0591ceca1faf35f60e5af6bd68e788"}, {"id": "MA24", "slug": "rotations", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Move players between areas to meet a changed map need.", "title": "Rotations", "file": "MA24-rotations.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Lane assignments, Tempo.", "action": "Name the map need and the player suited to it."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>A rotation is a move from one map area to another to change who attacks, defends or collects resources there. It has value when the new position serves a more useful purpose than the duty you leave.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Identify the destination and the coverage lost at the origin. Compare routes using vision, travel and possible interceptions. A rotation is not complete when you start walking: the arriving players must still be useful, and someone may need to cover the vacated lane.<\/p><ol><li>Name the map need and the player suited to it.<\/li><li>Check the route and the duty left behind.<\/li><li>Reassess the destination before arrival.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>An enemy shifts toward mid after a side turret falls. You move to help defend mid while an ally collects the safer side wave. When the enemy backs away, you stop grouping unnecessarily and restore lane coverage.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma24-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma24-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Decision diagram for rotations: TRIGGER — Map need changes. MOVE — Use a checked route. COVER — Do not forget the old lane.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for rotations; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Moving toward teammates without a defined task.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> State what your arrival changes and who covers your previous area.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Review three lane-to-lane moves. Record the map trigger, destination job and uncovered resource. Compare the useful time at destination with the travel and missed income.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>Fast movement is not always better. A rotation through unsafe terrain can cost more than the threatened structure.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/lane-assignments\">Lane assignments<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/tempo\">Tempo<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/split-pushing\">Split pushing<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-march-19/\">Riot: Split Pushing Goals<\/a>. Primary design context for distributed pressure, rotations and counterplay. Its historical champion examples are not current matchup advice.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Rotations\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA24\n- Slug: rotations\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Move players between areas to meet a changed map need.\n- Prerequisites: Lane assignments, Tempo.\n- Observable action: Name the map need and the player suited to it.\n\n## Short answer\n\nA rotation is a move from one map area to another to change who attacks, defends or collects resources there. It has value when the new position serves a more useful purpose than the duty you leave.\n\n## How it works\n\nIdentify the destination and the coverage lost at the origin. Compare routes using vision, travel and possible interceptions. A rotation is not complete when you start walking: the arriving players must still be useful, and someone may need to cover the vacated lane.\n\n1. Name the map need and the player suited to it.\n2. Check the route and the duty left behind.\n3. Reassess the destination before arrival.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nAn enemy shifts toward mid after a side turret falls. You move to help defend mid while an ally collects the safer side wave. When the enemy backs away, you stop grouping unnecessarily and restore lane coverage.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA24 -->\n![Decision diagram for rotations: TRIGGER — Map need changes. MOVE — Use a checked route. COVER — Do not forget the old lane.](../visuals/assets/ma24-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for rotations; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Moving toward teammates without a defined task.\n\n**Correction:** State what your arrival changes and who covers your previous area.\n\n## Practice\n\nReview three lane-to-lane moves. Record the map trigger, destination job and uncovered resource. Compare the useful time at destination with the travel and missed income.\n\n## Limits\n\nFast movement is not always better. A rotation through unsafe terrain can cost more than the threatened structure.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Lane assignments](../macro/MA25-lane-assignments.md) · [Tempo](../macro/MA22-tempo.md) · [Split pushing](../macro/MA15-split-pushing.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Riot: Split Pushing Goals](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-march-19/). Primary design context for distributed pressure, rotations and counterplay. Its historical champion examples are not current matchup advice.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "4c1460eb03c6a236161de659e7dc51b0132dacbf21554e77dbe8aa83d0f32f7b"}, {"id": "MA25", "slug": "lane-assignments", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Distribute players so waves and map duties have an owner.", "title": "Lane assignments", "file": "MA25-lane-assignments.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Wave management, Weak side.", "action": "List the waves and urgent defence jobs."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Lane assignments describe which players collect or defend each lane while others handle vision, jungle resources or group pressure. Assignments change as structures fall and objectives become relevant.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Consider who can survive the lane, clear its wave and reach the next team action. Sending the strongest duelist far away is not useful if the group cannot safely wait. Avoid several players competing for the same safe wave while another lane threatens your base.<\/p><ol><li>List the waves and urgent defence jobs.<\/li><li>Match each to a suitable available player.<\/li><li>Reassign when enemies move or a contest approaches.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>Two carries approach mid while a side wave reaches your turret. One collects mid and the other takes the safe side defence with a return plan. The support stays near the area where vision is needed rather than claiming a wave by default.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma25-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma25-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Decision diagram for lane assignments: NEED — Waves and defence jobs. MATCH — Choose suitable players. UPDATE — Reassign as the map changes.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for lane assignments; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Treating the opening lane as a permanent assignment.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Choose roles from the current map needs and champion tools.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Pause a replay at three mid-game wave arrivals. Assign one player to each useful job and explain safety and travel. Compare that plan with any duplicated or abandoned duty.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>Solo-queue teammates may not follow the same plan. Adapt to observed movement instead of assuming a ping settled ownership.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/wave-management\">Wave management<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/weak-side\">Weak side<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/rotations\">Rotations<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-march-19/\">Riot: Split Pushing Goals<\/a>. Primary design context for distributed pressure, rotations and counterplay. Its historical champion examples are not current matchup advice.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Lane assignments\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA25\n- Slug: lane-assignments\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Distribute players so waves and map duties have an owner.\n- Prerequisites: Wave management, Weak side.\n- Observable action: List the waves and urgent defence jobs.\n\n## Short answer\n\nLane assignments describe which players collect or defend each lane while others handle vision, jungle resources or group pressure. Assignments change as structures fall and objectives become relevant.\n\n## How it works\n\nConsider who can survive the lane, clear its wave and reach the next team action. Sending the strongest duelist far away is not useful if the group cannot safely wait. Avoid several players competing for the same safe wave while another lane threatens your base.\n\n1. List the waves and urgent defence jobs.\n2. Match each to a suitable available player.\n3. Reassign when enemies move or a contest approaches.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nTwo carries approach mid while a side wave reaches your turret. One collects mid and the other takes the safe side defence with a return plan. The support stays near the area where vision is needed rather than claiming a wave by default.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA25 -->\n![Decision diagram for lane assignments: NEED — Waves and defence jobs. MATCH — Choose suitable players. UPDATE — Reassign as the map changes.](../visuals/assets/ma25-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for lane assignments; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Treating the opening lane as a permanent assignment.\n\n**Correction:** Choose roles from the current map needs and champion tools.\n\n## Practice\n\nPause a replay at three mid-game wave arrivals. Assign one player to each useful job and explain safety and travel. Compare that plan with any duplicated or abandoned duty.\n\n## Limits\n\nSolo-queue teammates may not follow the same plan. Adapt to observed movement instead of assuming a ping settled ownership.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Wave management](../macro/MA40-wave-management.md) · [Weak side](../macro/MA37-weak-side.md) · [Rotations](../macro/MA24-rotations.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Riot: Split Pushing Goals](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-march-19/). Primary design context for distributed pressure, rotations and counterplay. Its historical champion examples are not current matchup advice.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "3dbfad1e381bd37b3e91d49c1cf274915475f6dbc1fd9bb06fd32037d68ae5a3"}, {"id": "MA26", "slug": "cross-map-trading", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Take a useful resource elsewhere when a direct contest is poor.", "title": "Cross-map trading", "file": "MA26-cross-map-trading.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Split pushing, Tempo.", "action": "Check whether direct help can arrive usefully."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Cross-map trading means gaining something in one part of the map while the enemy spends time or players elsewhere. It is an alternative to arriving late at a fight you are unlikely to improve.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Compare what is truly available, what the enemy is gaining and how long you have before they can return. A visible enemy commitment can create room, but missing opponents may still defend your target. Tell teammates when you cannot contest and choose a realistic trade with a safe exit.<\/p><ol><li>Check whether direct help can arrive usefully.<\/li><li>Identify a reachable resource on the other side.<\/li><li>Leave before the enemy can close the route.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>Most enemies appear at a distant objective while you are near an undefended side wave. You push toward a turret with a visible escape, then leave when enemies disappear. You do not keep hitting merely to make the exchange look equal.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma26-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma26-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Decision diagram for cross-map trading: COMMIT — Enemies act far away. TRADE — Take a reachable resource. EXIT — Stop before defenders return.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for cross-map trading; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Calling any farming a good trade without comparing losses.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Name the gain, the loss and the chance of completing it.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Review three uncontested enemy objectives. Using only player-view information, list possible trades and their travel costs. Check whether your chosen gain was available before the window closed.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>Some losses are too costly to accept, especially threats to the base. Not every objective has a safe equivalent elsewhere.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/split-pushing\">Split pushing<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/tempo\">Tempo<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/positioning\">Positioning<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-march-19/\">Riot: Split Pushing Goals<\/a>. Primary design context for distributed pressure, rotations and counterplay. Its historical champion examples are not current matchup advice.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Cross-map trading\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA26\n- Slug: cross-map-trading\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Take a useful resource elsewhere when a direct contest is poor.\n- Prerequisites: Split pushing, Tempo.\n- Observable action: Check whether direct help can arrive usefully.\n\n## Short answer\n\nCross-map trading means gaining something in one part of the map while the enemy spends time or players elsewhere. It is an alternative to arriving late at a fight you are unlikely to improve.\n\n## How it works\n\nCompare what is truly available, what the enemy is gaining and how long you have before they can return. A visible enemy commitment can create room, but missing opponents may still defend your target. Tell teammates when you cannot contest and choose a realistic trade with a safe exit.\n\n1. Check whether direct help can arrive usefully.\n2. Identify a reachable resource on the other side.\n3. Leave before the enemy can close the route.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nMost enemies appear at a distant objective while you are near an undefended side wave. You push toward a turret with a visible escape, then leave when enemies disappear. You do not keep hitting merely to make the exchange look equal.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA26 -->\n![Decision diagram for cross-map trading: COMMIT — Enemies act far away. TRADE — Take a reachable resource. EXIT — Stop before defenders return.](../visuals/assets/ma26-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for cross-map trading; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Calling any farming a good trade without comparing losses.\n\n**Correction:** Name the gain, the loss and the chance of completing it.\n\n## Practice\n\nReview three uncontested enemy objectives. Using only player-view information, list possible trades and their travel costs. Check whether your chosen gain was available before the window closed.\n\n## Limits\n\nSome losses are too costly to accept, especially threats to the base. Not every objective has a safe equivalent elsewhere.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Split pushing](../macro/MA15-split-pushing.md) · [Tempo](../macro/MA22-tempo.md) · [Positioning](../micro/MI12-positioning.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Riot: Split Pushing Goals](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-march-19/). Primary design context for distributed pressure, rotations and counterplay. Its historical champion examples are not current matchup advice.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "a479c1f232938cda930ccfb7e10be5e88d97304fd1d3d1992ff8bbafb74d4dec"}, {"id": "MA27", "slug": "ganking", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Approach a lane to create a useful local advantage.", "title": "Ganking", "file": "MA27-ganking.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Jungle pathing, Crowd control, Summoner spells.", "action": "Check whether the lane can follow up."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Ganking is moving into a lane to help attack an opponent, usually from outside their immediate lane fight. The aim can be a kill, a forced retreat or relief for an ally, but the attempt costs time and resources.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Check ally readiness, enemy position, likely vision and escape routes. A favourable position can disappear during travel. Arriving when your ally is too low to help can make the apparent numbers advantage misleading. Choose the approach and the point at which waiting is no longer worthwhile.<\/p><ol><li>Check whether the lane can follow up.<\/li><li>Approach using the information you have.<\/li><li>Leave when the opening ends or help cannot arrive.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>You approach a pushed lane, but your ally recalls before you reach it. You stop outside enemy vision and return to another task. The earlier lane position does not justify attacking alone now.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma27-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma27-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Decision diagram for ganking: READ — Lane position and ally readiness. APPROACH — Check the changing opening. LEAVE — Cancel when follow-up is gone.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for ganking; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Waiting indefinitely for the enemy to walk forward.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Set a departure condition before entering the route.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Review three gank approaches. At departure and arrival, record ally readiness, enemy escape and visible information. Note which changed during travel and whether you updated the plan.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>Vision and reinforcement information are incomplete. No route or champion guarantees a successful gank.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/jungle-pathing\">Jungle pathing<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/crowd-control\">Crowd control<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/summoner-spells\">Summoner spells<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/counterganking\">Counterganking<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://dignitas.gg/articles/blogs/League-of-Legends/7931/jungle-when-should-i-gank\">Novalas / Dignitas: When should I gank?<\/a>. Original coaching context for lane readiness, escape tools and countergank risk (2015). Old routes, timings, items and matchup claims are excluded.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Ganking\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA27\n- Slug: ganking\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Approach a lane to create a useful local advantage.\n- Prerequisites: Jungle pathing, Crowd control, Summoner spells.\n- Observable action: Check whether the lane can follow up.\n\n## Short answer\n\nGanking is moving into a lane to help attack an opponent, usually from outside their immediate lane fight. The aim can be a kill, a forced retreat or relief for an ally, but the attempt costs time and resources.\n\n## How it works\n\nCheck ally readiness, enemy position, likely vision and escape routes. A favourable position can disappear during travel. Arriving when your ally is too low to help can make the apparent numbers advantage misleading. Choose the approach and the point at which waiting is no longer worthwhile.\n\n1. Check whether the lane can follow up.\n2. Approach using the information you have.\n3. Leave when the opening ends or help cannot arrive.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYou approach a pushed lane, but your ally recalls before you reach it. You stop outside enemy vision and return to another task. The earlier lane position does not justify attacking alone now.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA27 -->\n![Decision diagram for ganking: READ — Lane position and ally readiness. APPROACH — Check the changing opening. LEAVE — Cancel when follow-up is gone.](../visuals/assets/ma27-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for ganking; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Waiting indefinitely for the enemy to walk forward.\n\n**Correction:** Set a departure condition before entering the route.\n\n## Practice\n\nReview three gank approaches. At departure and arrival, record ally readiness, enemy escape and visible information. Note which changed during travel and whether you updated the plan.\n\n## Limits\n\nVision and reinforcement information are incomplete. No route or champion guarantees a successful gank.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Jungle pathing](../macro/MA29-jungle-pathing.md) · [Crowd control](../micro/MI21-crowd-control.md) · [Summoner spells](../micro/MI26-summoner-spells.md) · [Counterganking](../macro/MA28-counterganking.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Novalas / Dignitas: When should I gank?](https://dignitas.gg/articles/blogs/League-of-Legends/7931/jungle-when-should-i-gank). Original coaching context for lane readiness, escape tools and countergank risk (2015). Old routes, timings, items and matchup claims are excluded.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "912e4b9861869efe699a2aed2b08b9caf8abaa329fd311e0ad62d9172f6a92ae"}, {"id": "MA28", "slug": "counterganking", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Prepare to answer an enemy gank with nearby support.", "title": "Counterganking", "file": "MA28-counterganking.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Ganking, Jungle tracking.", "action": "Separate evidence of a gank from a guess."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Counterganking means arriving to turn or limit an enemy gank. It relies on anticipating an attack and being close enough to affect it, while avoiding a long wait for an event that may never happen.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Use observed enemy movement, lane exposure and ally condition to form a hypothesis. Check whether you win the likely fight and whether your ally can survive until you act. Sometimes the useful answer is a warning and retreat instead of a hidden counterattack.<\/p><ol><li>Separate evidence of a gank from a guess.<\/li><li>Compare arrival time and ally survival.<\/li><li>Prepare a response or warn the ally to leave.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>You see the enemy jungler enter the river toward an exposed lane. You are nearby, but your laner is low and has no exit. You signal retreat and cover their path rather than wait for them to be engaged on.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma28-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma28-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Decision diagram for counterganking: CLUE — Enemy moves toward lane. COMPARE — Can ally survive until help?. ANSWER — Counterfight or cover retreat.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for counterganking; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Assuming every predicted gank should be fought.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Choose prevention when the counterfight lacks the tools to work.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Review two enemy ganks. Pause at the last visible clue and estimate whether you could arrive in time. Compare a counterfight, warning and alternative task without using hidden replay information.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>A correct prediction can still lead to a losing fight. Observation and combat strength are separate questions.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/ganking\">Ganking<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/jungle-tracking\">Jungle tracking<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/invading\">Invading<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://dignitas.gg/articles/blogs/League-of-Legends/7931/jungle-when-should-i-gank\">Novalas / Dignitas: When should I gank?<\/a>. Original coaching context for lane readiness, escape tools and countergank risk (2015). Old routes, timings, items and matchup claims are excluded.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Counterganking\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA28\n- Slug: counterganking\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Prepare to answer an enemy gank with nearby support.\n- Prerequisites: Ganking, Jungle tracking.\n- Observable action: Separate evidence of a gank from a guess.\n\n## Short answer\n\nCounterganking means arriving to turn or limit an enemy gank. It relies on anticipating an attack and being close enough to affect it, while avoiding a long wait for an event that may never happen.\n\n## How it works\n\nUse observed enemy movement, lane exposure and ally condition to form a hypothesis. Check whether you win the likely fight and whether your ally can survive until you act. Sometimes the useful answer is a warning and retreat instead of a hidden counterattack.\n\n1. Separate evidence of a gank from a guess.\n2. Compare arrival time and ally survival.\n3. Prepare a response or warn the ally to leave.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYou see the enemy jungler enter the river toward an exposed lane. You are nearby, but your laner is low and has no exit. You signal retreat and cover their path rather than wait for them to be engaged on.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA28 -->\n![Decision diagram for counterganking: CLUE — Enemy moves toward lane. COMPARE — Can ally survive until help?. ANSWER — Counterfight or cover retreat.](../visuals/assets/ma28-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for counterganking; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Assuming every predicted gank should be fought.\n\n**Correction:** Choose prevention when the counterfight lacks the tools to work.\n\n## Practice\n\nReview two enemy ganks. Pause at the last visible clue and estimate whether you could arrive in time. Compare a counterfight, warning and alternative task without using hidden replay information.\n\n## Limits\n\nA correct prediction can still lead to a losing fight. Observation and combat strength are separate questions.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Ganking](../macro/MA27-ganking.md) · [Jungle tracking](../macro/MA06-jungle-tracking.md) · [Invading](../macro/MA30-invading.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Novalas / Dignitas: When should I gank?](https://dignitas.gg/articles/blogs/League-of-Legends/7931/jungle-when-should-i-gank). Original coaching context for lane readiness, escape tools and countergank risk (2015). Old routes, timings, items and matchup claims are excluded.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "0dd588a373410b4697eff97ed8ea0e348a9163f735803ec6be20b0b8552421ae"}, {"id": "MA29", "slug": "jungle-pathing", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Order jungle tasks around access, resources and the next opportunity.", "title": "Jungle pathing", "file": "MA29-jungle-pathing.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Jungle tracking, Lane priority.", "action": "Choose a useful destination after the next tasks."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Jungle pathing is choosing the order and route of camps, lane visits and map tasks. A path is a plan for useful movement, not a fixed sequence you must finish when conditions change.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Start with available camps and the area where you want to finish. Consider health, ability resources, lane readiness and observed enemy movement. Recheck after each task. Detouring for a weak opportunity can waste travel, while refusing a strong nearby opportunity can also cost value.<\/p><ol><li>Choose a useful destination after the next tasks.<\/li><li>Check accessible camps and safe routes.<\/li><li>Update the order when visible information changes.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>You plan to clear toward bot, but mid needs a safe escort after a fight. You compare the short detour with your camp plan and help them leave, then resume from the nearest useful task rather than returning to an obsolete route.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma29-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma29-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Decision diagram for jungle pathing: ORDER — Tasks lead to a destination. TRAVEL — Use safe accessible routes. REPLAN — New information changes order.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for jungle pathing; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Following a memorised clear regardless of the map.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Keep a default route and explicit reasons to change it.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Review one early jungle sequence. At each departure, name the next task, destination afterward and visible reason for any detour. Compare wasted travel with a plausible alternative route.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>Camp timings, clear speeds and champion starts vary. This lesson gives no current optimal clear or spawn schedule.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/jungle-tracking\">Jungle tracking<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/lane-priority\">Lane priority<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/ganking\">Ganking<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/lol-pls-preseason-2023-jungle-changes/\">Riot: Preseason 2023 Jungle Changes<\/a>. Primary historical context for balancing camp farming, ganks, vision and pathing. Proposed mechanics and old clear rules are excluded; the decision routine is editorial guidance.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Jungle pathing\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA29\n- Slug: jungle-pathing\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Order jungle tasks around access, resources and the next opportunity.\n- Prerequisites: Jungle tracking, Lane priority.\n- Observable action: Choose a useful destination after the next tasks.\n\n## Short answer\n\nJungle pathing is choosing the order and route of camps, lane visits and map tasks. A path is a plan for useful movement, not a fixed sequence you must finish when conditions change.\n\n## How it works\n\nStart with available camps and the area where you want to finish. Consider health, ability resources, lane readiness and observed enemy movement. Recheck after each task. Detouring for a weak opportunity can waste travel, while refusing a strong nearby opportunity can also cost value.\n\n1. Choose a useful destination after the next tasks.\n2. Check accessible camps and safe routes.\n3. Update the order when visible information changes.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYou plan to clear toward bot, but mid needs a safe escort after a fight. You compare the short detour with your camp plan and help them leave, then resume from the nearest useful task rather than returning to an obsolete route.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA29 -->\n![Decision diagram for jungle pathing: ORDER — Tasks lead to a destination. TRAVEL — Use safe accessible routes. REPLAN — New information changes order.](../visuals/assets/ma29-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for jungle pathing; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Following a memorised clear regardless of the map.\n\n**Correction:** Keep a default route and explicit reasons to change it.\n\n## Practice\n\nReview one early jungle sequence. At each departure, name the next task, destination afterward and visible reason for any detour. Compare wasted travel with a plausible alternative route.\n\n## Limits\n\nCamp timings, clear speeds and champion starts vary. This lesson gives no current optimal clear or spawn schedule.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Jungle tracking](../macro/MA06-jungle-tracking.md) · [Lane priority](../macro/MA04-lane-priority.md) · [Ganking](../macro/MA27-ganking.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Riot: Preseason 2023 Jungle Changes](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/lol-pls-preseason-2023-jungle-changes/). Primary historical context for balancing camp farming, ganks, vision and pathing. Proposed mechanics and old clear rules are excluded; the decision routine is editorial guidance.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "5ac79bd758d601365055dae62edf8dd587359be74e10cac6c9b440fdda2adea4"}, {"id": "MA30", "slug": "invading", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Enter enemy territory with support, a purpose and an exit.", "title": "Invading", "file": "MA30-invading.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Jungle pathing, Lane priority, Warding.", "action": "Name the task inside enemy territory."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Invading means entering the enemy jungle to contest resources, gain information or pressure an opponent. Crossing the river is not itself an advantage; you need a useful task and a way out if defenders arrive.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Check nearby lane movement, observed enemy locations and your resources. An ally being near does not mean they can leave a wave or survive the fight. Plan the exit before committing deep, and update it as enemies disappear from vision.<\/p><ol><li>Name the task inside enemy territory.<\/li><li>Check which allies and enemies can arrive first.<\/li><li>Leave when the task ends or support becomes uncertain.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>You see the opposing jungler far away and approach a nearby camp. Their mid laner disappears while yours is under pressure. You leave before finishing because the original access condition has changed.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma30-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma30-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Decision diagram for invading: PURPOSE — Resource, vision or pressure. ACCESS — Who can arrive first?. EXIT — Leave when support changes.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for invading; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Treating the enemy jungler&#x27;s absence as proof the jungle is empty.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Track nearby laners and routes too.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Review three entries into enemy jungle. At the boundary, name purpose, reachable help and exit. Identify the first visible change that should end the attempt.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>Unknown enemies can still be present. A level or item lead does not remove the cost of isolation.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/jungle-pathing\">Jungle pathing<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/lane-priority\">Lane priority<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/warding\">Warding<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/counterjungling\">Counterjungling<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-may-14/\">Riot: Champion Counterplay<\/a>. Primary context for invading as strategic counterplay. The routing, gank and camp-trade routines here are editorial applications, not Riot prescriptions.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Invading\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA30\n- Slug: invading\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Enter enemy territory with support, a purpose and an exit.\n- Prerequisites: Jungle pathing, Lane priority, Warding.\n- Observable action: Name the task inside enemy territory.\n\n## Short answer\n\nInvading means entering the enemy jungle to contest resources, gain information or pressure an opponent. Crossing the river is not itself an advantage; you need a useful task and a way out if defenders arrive.\n\n## How it works\n\nCheck nearby lane movement, observed enemy locations and your resources. An ally being near does not mean they can leave a wave or survive the fight. Plan the exit before committing deep, and update it as enemies disappear from vision.\n\n1. Name the task inside enemy territory.\n2. Check which allies and enemies can arrive first.\n3. Leave when the task ends or support becomes uncertain.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYou see the opposing jungler far away and approach a nearby camp. Their mid laner disappears while yours is under pressure. You leave before finishing because the original access condition has changed.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA30 -->\n![Decision diagram for invading: PURPOSE — Resource, vision or pressure. ACCESS — Who can arrive first?. EXIT — Leave when support changes.](../visuals/assets/ma30-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for invading; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Treating the enemy jungler's absence as proof the jungle is empty.\n\n**Correction:** Track nearby laners and routes too.\n\n## Practice\n\nReview three entries into enemy jungle. At the boundary, name purpose, reachable help and exit. Identify the first visible change that should end the attempt.\n\n## Limits\n\nUnknown enemies can still be present. A level or item lead does not remove the cost of isolation.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Jungle pathing](../macro/MA29-jungle-pathing.md) · [Lane priority](../macro/MA04-lane-priority.md) · [Warding](../macro/MA05-warding.md) · [Counterjungling](../macro/MA31-counterjungling.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Riot: Champion Counterplay](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-may-14/). Primary context for invading as strategic counterplay. The routing, gank and camp-trade routines here are editorial applications, not Riot prescriptions.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "63d070438aaf61c92880706e168a31732cf89420661e192daef3b083b5b166c1"}, {"id": "MA31", "slug": "counterjungling", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Take enemy camps when the resource and escape are available.", "title": "Counterjungling", "file": "MA31-counterjungling.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Invading.", "action": "Check camp availability and enemy information."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Counterjungling is taking enemy jungle resources to gain income and reduce what the opposing jungler can collect. It is one possible purpose of an invade, not a requirement to hunt the enemy champion.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Confirm the camp exists and compare the time to take it with other available tasks. Keep track of nearby lane movement and the return path. A stolen camp can be a poor exchange if you lose your own resources or arrive late to an important defence.<\/p><ol><li>Check camp availability and enemy information.<\/li><li>Compare the steal with your own tasks.<\/li><li>Finish only while the exit remains credible.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>The enemy jungler shows on the opposite side. You take a nearby camp, but do not walk deeper for another when nearby laners leave vision. You keep the completed gain rather than risking it on an uncertain extension.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma31-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma31-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Decision diagram for counterjungling: AVAILABLE — Visible opportunity for a camp. COMPARE — Steal versus your next task. BANK — Leave with the completed gain.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for counterjungling; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Assuming a stolen camp is always a winning exchange.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Include travel, missed tasks and danger in the comparison.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Review two steals and one abandoned attempt. Record known camp availability, alternative task and exit condition. Compare the whole sequence, not just the gold from the camp.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>Exact respawn and camp rules can change. This lesson does not prescribe leaving a specific monster alive or any reset trick.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/invading\">Invading<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/tempo\">Tempo<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/lol-pls-preseason-2023-jungle-changes/\">Riot: Preseason 2023 Jungle Changes<\/a>. Primary historical context for balancing camp farming, ganks, vision and pathing. Proposed mechanics and old clear rules are excluded; the decision routine is editorial guidance.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Counterjungling\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA31\n- Slug: counterjungling\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Take enemy camps when the resource and escape are available.\n- Prerequisites: Invading.\n- Observable action: Check camp availability and enemy information.\n\n## Short answer\n\nCounterjungling is taking enemy jungle resources to gain income and reduce what the opposing jungler can collect. It is one possible purpose of an invade, not a requirement to hunt the enemy champion.\n\n## How it works\n\nConfirm the camp exists and compare the time to take it with other available tasks. Keep track of nearby lane movement and the return path. A stolen camp can be a poor exchange if you lose your own resources or arrive late to an important defence.\n\n1. Check camp availability and enemy information.\n2. Compare the steal with your own tasks.\n3. Finish only while the exit remains credible.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nThe enemy jungler shows on the opposite side. You take a nearby camp, but do not walk deeper for another when nearby laners leave vision. You keep the completed gain rather than risking it on an uncertain extension.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA31 -->\n![Decision diagram for counterjungling: AVAILABLE — Visible opportunity for a camp. COMPARE — Steal versus your next task. BANK — Leave with the completed gain.](../visuals/assets/ma31-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for counterjungling; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Assuming a stolen camp is always a winning exchange.\n\n**Correction:** Include travel, missed tasks and danger in the comparison.\n\n## Practice\n\nReview two steals and one abandoned attempt. Record known camp availability, alternative task and exit condition. Compare the whole sequence, not just the gold from the camp.\n\n## Limits\n\nExact respawn and camp rules can change. This lesson does not prescribe leaving a specific monster alive or any reset trick.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Invading](../macro/MA30-invading.md) · [Tempo](../macro/MA22-tempo.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Riot: Preseason 2023 Jungle Changes](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/lol-pls-preseason-2023-jungle-changes/). Primary historical context for balancing camp farming, ganks, vision and pathing. Proposed mechanics and old clear rules are excluded; the decision routine is editorial guidance.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "86a0f307a43f794c7bc63114479e435e563500b992d2c0ad32193f34588195ce"}, {"id": "MA32", "slug": "collapsing", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Bring nearby allies onto a threat without arriving one at a time.", "title": "Collapsing", "file": "MA32-collapsing.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Flanking, Rotations.", "action": "Identify the enemy position and possible exits."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Collapsing means converging from different nearby positions onto an enemy or contested area. The advantage comes from coordinated arrival and useful access, not simply from several players walking toward the same marker.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Check who can leave their current duty and which routes are safe. Compare arrival times before the first player commits. If the enemy can escape or isolate the earliest arrival, waiting or protecting an exit may be more useful than rushing directly at them.<\/p><ol><li>Identify the enemy position and possible exits.<\/li><li>Compare ally routes and arrival times.<\/li><li>Commit when enough help can affect the fight.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>An enemy enters your jungle between two lanes. Both laners can move, but one is much farther away. The closer player holds a safe route rather than attacking alone, allowing the group to converge or let the enemy retreat.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma32-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma32-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Decision diagram for collapsing: SPOT — Enemy between allied positions. ALIGN — Compare routes and arrivals. ACT — Commit with help in reach.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for collapsing; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Counting moving allies as already present.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Fight with actual nearby help, not future numbers.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Review three converging moves. Mark the first ally arrival, first commitment and next arrival. Identify whether the gap allowed an isolated death or an enemy escape.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>A collapse can cost lane resources and still fail. Do not chase indefinitely once the shared opportunity ends.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/flanking\">Flanking<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/rotations\">Rotations<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/win-conditions\">Win conditions<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-march-19/\">Riot: Split Pushing Goals<\/a>. Primary design context for distributed pressure, rotations and counterplay. Its historical champion examples are not current matchup advice.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Collapsing\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA32\n- Slug: collapsing\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Bring nearby allies onto a threat without arriving one at a time.\n- Prerequisites: Flanking, Rotations.\n- Observable action: Identify the enemy position and possible exits.\n\n## Short answer\n\nCollapsing means converging from different nearby positions onto an enemy or contested area. The advantage comes from coordinated arrival and useful access, not simply from several players walking toward the same marker.\n\n## How it works\n\nCheck who can leave their current duty and which routes are safe. Compare arrival times before the first player commits. If the enemy can escape or isolate the earliest arrival, waiting or protecting an exit may be more useful than rushing directly at them.\n\n1. Identify the enemy position and possible exits.\n2. Compare ally routes and arrival times.\n3. Commit when enough help can affect the fight.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nAn enemy enters your jungle between two lanes. Both laners can move, but one is much farther away. The closer player holds a safe route rather than attacking alone, allowing the group to converge or let the enemy retreat.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA32 -->\n![Decision diagram for collapsing: SPOT — Enemy between allied positions. ALIGN — Compare routes and arrivals. ACT — Commit with help in reach.](../visuals/assets/ma32-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for collapsing; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Counting moving allies as already present.\n\n**Correction:** Fight with actual nearby help, not future numbers.\n\n## Practice\n\nReview three converging moves. Mark the first ally arrival, first commitment and next arrival. Identify whether the gap allowed an isolated death or an enemy escape.\n\n## Limits\n\nA collapse can cost lane resources and still fail. Do not chase indefinitely once the shared opportunity ends.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Flanking](../macro/MA18-flanking.md) · [Rotations](../macro/MA24-rotations.md) · [Win conditions](../macro/MA36-win-conditions.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Riot: Split Pushing Goals](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-march-19/). Primary design context for distributed pressure, rotations and counterplay. Its historical champion examples are not current matchup advice.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "e95fc10a3e110bd6dbf1a598739ba06ea92001ed209801eb9d2ab6a5accb05e9"}, {"id": "MA33", "slug": "picks", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Catch an exposed enemy and convert the opening into a useful action.", "title": "Picks", "file": "MA33-picks.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Win conditions, Collapsing.", "action": "Identify exposure and possible hidden help."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>A pick is a catch on an isolated or vulnerable opponent that creates a temporary advantage for the team. It often begins before a full fight, when vision or positioning allows focused control and damage.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Check that the target is truly exposed and that allies can follow. An apparently lone enemy may have unseen support. Plan what the catch would enable, such as a safe objective approach or a reset. Do not spend every resource chasing a target who no longer offers a useful opening.<\/p><ol><li>Identify exposure and possible hidden help.<\/li><li>Check reliable follow-up and an exit.<\/li><li>After the catch, choose a concrete map action.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>Your group catches an enemy entering river alone. Instead of chasing the next opponent into darkness, you use the temporary advantage to establish vision and prepare the nearby objective with healthy teammates.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma33-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma33-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Decision diagram for picks: EXPOSED — Target separated from support. CATCH — Control with reachable follow-up. CONVERT — Use the temporary opening.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for picks; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Turning every catch into a long chase.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Convert the opening before the enemy regroups.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Review three catches or attempts. Record the exposure, follow-up and action after it. Identify whether your team used the window or spent it chasing beyond its support.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>A single kill does not guarantee objective access. Resources, lanes and remaining enemies still matter.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/win-conditions\">Win conditions<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/collapsing\">Collapsing<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/objective-control\">Objective control<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-march-19/\">Riot: Split Pushing Goals<\/a>. Primary design context for distributed pressure, rotations and counterplay. Its historical champion examples are not current matchup advice.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Picks\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA33\n- Slug: picks\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Catch an exposed enemy and convert the opening into a useful action.\n- Prerequisites: Win conditions, Collapsing.\n- Observable action: Identify exposure and possible hidden help.\n\n## Short answer\n\nA pick is a catch on an isolated or vulnerable opponent that creates a temporary advantage for the team. It often begins before a full fight, when vision or positioning allows focused control and damage.\n\n## How it works\n\nCheck that the target is truly exposed and that allies can follow. An apparently lone enemy may have unseen support. Plan what the catch would enable, such as a safe objective approach or a reset. Do not spend every resource chasing a target who no longer offers a useful opening.\n\n1. Identify exposure and possible hidden help.\n2. Check reliable follow-up and an exit.\n3. After the catch, choose a concrete map action.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYour group catches an enemy entering river alone. Instead of chasing the next opponent into darkness, you use the temporary advantage to establish vision and prepare the nearby objective with healthy teammates.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA33 -->\n![Decision diagram for picks: EXPOSED — Target separated from support. CATCH — Control with reachable follow-up. CONVERT — Use the temporary opening.](../visuals/assets/ma33-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for picks; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Turning every catch into a long chase.\n\n**Correction:** Convert the opening before the enemy regroups.\n\n## Practice\n\nReview three catches or attempts. Record the exposure, follow-up and action after it. Identify whether your team used the window or spent it chasing beyond its support.\n\n## Limits\n\nA single kill does not guarantee objective access. Resources, lanes and remaining enemies still matter.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Win conditions](../macro/MA36-win-conditions.md) · [Collapsing](../macro/MA32-collapsing.md) · [Objective control](../macro/MA10-objective-control.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Riot: Split Pushing Goals](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-march-19/). Primary design context for distributed pressure, rotations and counterplay. Its historical champion examples are not current matchup advice.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "ee04b6b03dc1857e232cadc3b7dd62a32a166d4f82c78c96fb10509b211f84db"}, {"id": "MA34", "slug": "sieging", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Pressure a defended structure while controlling the approach.", "title": "Sieging", "file": "MA34-sieging.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Objective control, Poking, Disengaging.", "action": "Check the wave and the sides of the approach."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Sieging means applying sustained pressure to a defended structure with waves and champion threats. It is a contest over safe access: attackers need opportunities to damage the structure, while defenders try to clear waves or force a favourable fight.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Bring a wave and check flanks before stepping up. Compare your ability to pressure with enemy wave clear and engagement tools. If each wave disappears before you can act, waiting in the same place may only expose your team. Rotate, reset or pressure another lane when the siege lacks progress.<\/p><ol><li>Check the wave and the sides of the approach.<\/li><li>Damage the structure only from a useful position.<\/li><li>Reassess when defenders clear or threaten engagement.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>Your group reaches a turret with minions, but the defender clears them and an enemy vanishes from the side lane. You back away and reset vision instead of standing under the turret for one more attack.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma34-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma34-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Decision diagram for sieging: WAVE — Minions enable approach. PRESSURE — Attack with flank awareness. REASSESS — Clear or engage ends access.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for sieging; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Confusing proximity to a turret with productive pressure.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Measure safe damage or forced responses, and leave when neither is available.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Review three waves at a defended turret. Note damage achieved, abilities spent and flank information. Explain whether another wave would improve the situation or repeat the same cost.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>Turret and minion mechanics vary with game state. This lesson gives no damage threshold for tanking structures.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/objective-control\">Objective control<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/poking\">Poking<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/disengaging\">Disengaging<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/diving\">Diving<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://dignitas.gg/articles/team-composition-guide-to-poke-compositions-in-league-of-legends\">soduh / Dignitas: Poke Compositions<\/a>. Original coaching context distinguishing poke from siege and explaining disengage (2021). Historical builds, champion numbers and matchup prescriptions are excluded.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Sieging\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA34\n- Slug: sieging\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Pressure a defended structure while controlling the approach.\n- Prerequisites: Objective control, Poking, Disengaging.\n- Observable action: Check the wave and the sides of the approach.\n\n## Short answer\n\nSieging means applying sustained pressure to a defended structure with waves and champion threats. It is a contest over safe access: attackers need opportunities to damage the structure, while defenders try to clear waves or force a favourable fight.\n\n## How it works\n\nBring a wave and check flanks before stepping up. Compare your ability to pressure with enemy wave clear and engagement tools. If each wave disappears before you can act, waiting in the same place may only expose your team. Rotate, reset or pressure another lane when the siege lacks progress.\n\n1. Check the wave and the sides of the approach.\n2. Damage the structure only from a useful position.\n3. Reassess when defenders clear or threaten engagement.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYour group reaches a turret with minions, but the defender clears them and an enemy vanishes from the side lane. You back away and reset vision instead of standing under the turret for one more attack.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA34 -->\n![Decision diagram for sieging: WAVE — Minions enable approach. PRESSURE — Attack with flank awareness. REASSESS — Clear or engage ends access.](../visuals/assets/ma34-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for sieging; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Confusing proximity to a turret with productive pressure.\n\n**Correction:** Measure safe damage or forced responses, and leave when neither is available.\n\n## Practice\n\nReview three waves at a defended turret. Note damage achieved, abilities spent and flank information. Explain whether another wave would improve the situation or repeat the same cost.\n\n## Limits\n\nTurret and minion mechanics vary with game state. This lesson gives no damage threshold for tanking structures.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Objective control](../macro/MA10-objective-control.md) · [Poking](../micro/MI18-poking.md) · [Disengaging](../micro/MI23-disengaging.md) · [Diving](../macro/MA35-diving.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[soduh / Dignitas: Poke Compositions](https://dignitas.gg/articles/team-composition-guide-to-poke-compositions-in-league-of-legends). Original coaching context distinguishing poke from siege and explaining disengage (2021). Historical builds, champion numbers and matchup prescriptions are excluded.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "f5f4cf3589059208868daafac3e649354b78da84902642ae9fc98a058d2a2163"}, {"id": "MA35", "slug": "diving", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Judge the extra risks of fighting under an enemy turret.", "title": "Diving", "file": "MA35-diving.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Sieging, All-ins, Summoner spells.", "action": "Check the defender's tools and reinforcement routes."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Diving is committing to fight an enemy under their turret. It adds structure damage and a restricted exit to the normal fight, so a low-health defender does not by itself make the attempt safe.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Account for the wave, defender abilities, incoming help and who the turret is attacking. Agree on an exit before entry. Check current targeting behavior in game rather than assuming minions will always protect you. A dive can fail even after the target dies if attackers cannot leave.<\/p><ol><li>Check the defender&#x27;s tools and reinforcement routes.<\/li><li>Confirm wave position and who takes turret fire.<\/li><li>Leave before another attack makes the exchange worse.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>Two allies approach a low-health defender, but the wave is cleared before they enter. They cancel instead of continuing with the original plan. The missing wave changes access even though the enemy health has not changed.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma35-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma35-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Decision diagram for diving: SETUP — Wave, tools and exits. RISK — Turret fire adds damage. CANCEL — Lost setup means reassess.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for diving; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Using the defender&#x27;s health as the whole decision.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Include turret exposure, tools and exit distance.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Review two dives and one cancellation. Mark entry, turret target, defender response and exit. Compare the last safe cancellation point with the actual commitment.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>Turret targeting and champion interactions need current in-game verification. This lesson does not give a universal tank order or number of safe shots.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/sieging\">Sieging<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/all-ins\">All-ins<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/summoner-spells\">Summoner spells<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/how-to-play/\">Riot: How to Play<\/a>. Primary context for levels, purchases, structures and the base objective. It does not validate the decision routine taught here.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Diving\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA35\n- Slug: diving\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Judge the extra risks of fighting under an enemy turret.\n- Prerequisites: Sieging, All-ins, Summoner spells.\n- Observable action: Check the defender's tools and reinforcement routes.\n\n## Short answer\n\nDiving is committing to fight an enemy under their turret. It adds structure damage and a restricted exit to the normal fight, so a low-health defender does not by itself make the attempt safe.\n\n## How it works\n\nAccount for the wave, defender abilities, incoming help and who the turret is attacking. Agree on an exit before entry. Check current targeting behavior in game rather than assuming minions will always protect you. A dive can fail even after the target dies if attackers cannot leave.\n\n1. Check the defender's tools and reinforcement routes.\n2. Confirm wave position and who takes turret fire.\n3. Leave before another attack makes the exchange worse.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nTwo allies approach a low-health defender, but the wave is cleared before they enter. They cancel instead of continuing with the original plan. The missing wave changes access even though the enemy health has not changed.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA35 -->\n![Decision diagram for diving: SETUP — Wave, tools and exits. RISK — Turret fire adds damage. CANCEL — Lost setup means reassess.](../visuals/assets/ma35-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for diving; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Using the defender's health as the whole decision.\n\n**Correction:** Include turret exposure, tools and exit distance.\n\n## Practice\n\nReview two dives and one cancellation. Mark entry, turret target, defender response and exit. Compare the last safe cancellation point with the actual commitment.\n\n## Limits\n\nTurret targeting and champion interactions need current in-game verification. This lesson does not give a universal tank order or number of safe shots.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Sieging](../macro/MA34-sieging.md) · [All-ins](../micro/MI17-all-ins.md) · [Summoner spells](../micro/MI26-summoner-spells.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Riot: How to Play](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/how-to-play/). Primary context for levels, purchases, structures and the base objective. It does not validate the decision routine taught here.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "bb1cfd04f13834cae13b214e8ee33b52e4d795bb7d5dba4241af08259e942c18"}, {"id": "MA36", "slug": "win-conditions", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Choose the repeatable advantage your team can turn into a finish.", "title": "Win conditions", "file": "MA36-win-conditions.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Split pushing, Target selection, Resets.", "action": "Identify the team's usable advantage."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>A win condition is a plausible way your team can gain and convert enough advantage to destroy the enemy base. It might rely on team fights, side pressure, catches or sustained structure pressure, and it can change during the match.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Compare team tools with the current map and resource state. A composition&#x27;s intended strength is only useful if players can execute it now. Name the action that creates progress, the setup it needs and the enemy response that would stop it. Avoid defining the plan as simply getting more kills.<\/p><ol><li>Identify the team&#x27;s usable advantage.<\/li><li>Choose the setup that lets it matter.<\/li><li>Update the plan when key conditions disappear.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>When behind<\/h2><p>A plan must fit your current strength. If you cannot safely contest, collect reachable resources, protect the next defensible position and wait for an observable opening. Do not enter dark terrain just because an objective is available. Reassess after purchases, returns to the map or enemy commitments; waiting needs a reason and a next check.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>Your team struggles in grouped fights but has a strong side-lane duelist. You coordinate side pressure while the group stays safe. When the duelist cannot advance safely, you stop treating that plan as automatic and look for a different opening.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma36-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma36-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Decision diagram for win conditions: ADVANTAGE — What can this team do well now?. SETUP — Create the conditions to use it. CONVERT — Pressure toward the enemy base.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for win conditions; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Following a pre-game plan despite changed resources.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Use current strengths and map access to revise it.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Pause at three mid-game moments. Write one sentence naming the advantage, setup and next action. Then state the enemy response that would make you change plans.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>No composition guarantees a win. This is a planning exercise, not a current draft ranking or prediction model.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/split-pushing\">Split pushing<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/target-selection\">Target selection<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/resets\">Resets<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/picks\">Picks<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/how-to-play/\">Riot: How to Play<\/a>. Primary context for levels, purchases, structures and the base objective. It does not validate the decision routine taught here.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Win conditions\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA36\n- Slug: win-conditions\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Choose the repeatable advantage your team can turn into a finish.\n- Prerequisites: Split pushing, Target selection, Resets.\n- Observable action: Identify the team's usable advantage.\n\n## Short answer\n\nA win condition is a plausible way your team can gain and convert enough advantage to destroy the enemy base. It might rely on team fights, side pressure, catches or sustained structure pressure, and it can change during the match.\n\n## How it works\n\nCompare team tools with the current map and resource state. A composition's intended strength is only useful if players can execute it now. Name the action that creates progress, the setup it needs and the enemy response that would stop it. Avoid defining the plan as simply getting more kills.\n\n1. Identify the team's usable advantage.\n2. Choose the setup that lets it matter.\n3. Update the plan when key conditions disappear.\n\n## When behind\n\nA plan must fit your current strength. If you cannot safely contest, collect reachable resources, protect the next defensible position and wait for an observable opening. Do not enter dark terrain just because an objective is available. Reassess after purchases, returns to the map or enemy commitments; waiting needs a reason and a next check.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYour team struggles in grouped fights but has a strong side-lane duelist. You coordinate side pressure while the group stays safe. When the duelist cannot advance safely, you stop treating that plan as automatic and look for a different opening.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA36 -->\n![Decision diagram for win conditions: ADVANTAGE — What can this team do well now?. SETUP — Create the conditions to use it. CONVERT — Pressure toward the enemy base.](../visuals/assets/ma36-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for win conditions; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Following a pre-game plan despite changed resources.\n\n**Correction:** Use current strengths and map access to revise it.\n\n## Practice\n\nPause at three mid-game moments. Write one sentence naming the advantage, setup and next action. Then state the enemy response that would make you change plans.\n\n## Limits\n\nNo composition guarantees a win. This is a planning exercise, not a current draft ranking or prediction model.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Split pushing](../macro/MA15-split-pushing.md) · [Target selection](../micro/MI22-target-selection.md) · [Resets](../macro/MA23-resets.md) · [Picks](../macro/MA33-picks.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Riot: How to Play](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/how-to-play/). Primary context for levels, purchases, structures and the base objective. It does not validate the decision routine taught here.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "8c3697721f7633237c2003f6b1513b88d874d48eaef8d874590bf9460f7a77ef"}, {"id": "MA37", "slug": "weak-side", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Play with limited nearby support while preserving useful resources.", "title": "Weak side", "file": "MA37-weak-side.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Lane priority, Jungle tracking.", "action": "Check reachable allies and possible enemy arrivals."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Weak side describes an area receiving less immediate allied support than another area of the map. It is a relative allocation of attention and players, not a permanent label for a role or a command to lose lane.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Compare who can reach your lane with who can reach the enemy&#x27;s. Choose positions and farm that do not require help you do not have. Signal pressure early and give up a resource when protecting it would risk much more. Your team still needs to make useful progress elsewhere for the allocation to pay off.<\/p><ol><li>Check reachable allies and possible enemy arrivals.<\/li><li>Choose a safe limit for farm and pressure.<\/li><li>Retreat before an unsupported defence becomes a trap.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>Your jungler and support are playing toward the opposite side. Two enemies could approach your lane, so you collect only what you can reach safely and retreat from a threatened turret. You communicate the loss rather than assume help will arrive.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma37-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma37-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Decision diagram for weak side: SUPPORT — Allies are committed elsewhere. BOUNDARY — Take only accessible resources. RETREAT — Leave before the route closes.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for weak side; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Treating weak side as permission to stop gathering information.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Keep checking pressure and adjust the safety boundary.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Review three moments with allies far away. Name what you could safely collect, the first warning of added pressure and your retreat point. Compare the conceded resource with the cost of staying.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>Being weak side does not make every concession correct. If enemies show elsewhere, a safe chance to farm or pressure can reopen.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/lane-priority\">Lane priority<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/jungle-tracking\">Jungle tracking<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/jungle-pathing\">Jungle pathing<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-march-19/\">Riot: Split Pushing Goals<\/a>. Primary design context for distributed pressure, rotations and counterplay. Its historical champion examples are not current matchup advice.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Weak side\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA37\n- Slug: weak-side\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Play with limited nearby support while preserving useful resources.\n- Prerequisites: Lane priority, Jungle tracking.\n- Observable action: Check reachable allies and possible enemy arrivals.\n\n## Short answer\n\nWeak side describes an area receiving less immediate allied support than another area of the map. It is a relative allocation of attention and players, not a permanent label for a role or a command to lose lane.\n\n## How it works\n\nCompare who can reach your lane with who can reach the enemy's. Choose positions and farm that do not require help you do not have. Signal pressure early and give up a resource when protecting it would risk much more. Your team still needs to make useful progress elsewhere for the allocation to pay off.\n\n1. Check reachable allies and possible enemy arrivals.\n2. Choose a safe limit for farm and pressure.\n3. Retreat before an unsupported defence becomes a trap.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYour jungler and support are playing toward the opposite side. Two enemies could approach your lane, so you collect only what you can reach safely and retreat from a threatened turret. You communicate the loss rather than assume help will arrive.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA37 -->\n![Decision diagram for weak side: SUPPORT — Allies are committed elsewhere. BOUNDARY — Take only accessible resources. RETREAT — Leave before the route closes.](../visuals/assets/ma37-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for weak side; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Treating weak side as permission to stop gathering information.\n\n**Correction:** Keep checking pressure and adjust the safety boundary.\n\n## Practice\n\nReview three moments with allies far away. Name what you could safely collect, the first warning of added pressure and your retreat point. Compare the conceded resource with the cost of staying.\n\n## Limits\n\nBeing weak side does not make every concession correct. If enemies show elsewhere, a safe chance to farm or pressure can reopen.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Lane priority](../macro/MA04-lane-priority.md) · [Jungle tracking](../macro/MA06-jungle-tracking.md) · [Jungle pathing](../macro/MA29-jungle-pathing.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Riot: Split Pushing Goals](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-march-19/). Primary design context for distributed pressure, rotations and counterplay. Its historical champion examples are not current matchup advice.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "86c8c95f3db54fda75d998ea45526b2a368073a49583a5c10a9e8a44190de51f"}, {"id": "MA38", "slug": "experience", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Separate level progress from last-hit income.", "title": "Experience", "file": "MA38-experience.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Last-hitting, Spacing.", "action": "Check your level and progress before approaching a wave."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Experience is the resource that raises your champion’s level during a match. A level can improve stats and let you unlock or strengthen an ability. It is separate from gold and from your account level.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Watch your experience bar as well as minion health. Lane minion experience has a proximity rule: missing a last hit does not necessarily mean missing experience. Sharing a lane changes experience received per champion. Do not assume a remembered minion count gives the same level timing after missed waves or shared experience. Compare visible levels before stepping forward, and learn the actual ability you unlock rather than treating every level as the same advantage.<\/p><ol><li>Check your level and progress before approaching a wave.<\/li><li>Choose a position that preserves access without taking an unaffordable hit.<\/li><li>Recheck both champions’ levels before the next exchange.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>You cannot safely approach a low-health minion for its last hit. You stay farther back where you can still receive experience, then see your experience bar rise as it dies. On a later wave, the opponent levels before you. You give space rather than contesting as though nothing changed. This assumes the safer position is within experience range; if it is not, concede the experience too.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma38-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma38-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Experience decision diagram: OBSERVE — Read level and experience bar.. CHOOSE — Keep safe access or concede.. RECHECK — A new level can change the exchange.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative choices for experience; not measured game timing or positions.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Equating low CS with no experience, or risking death to avoid losing one minion.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Track gold opportunities and level progress separately; survival may require conceding both.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>In a practice session, watch the experience bar for three minions you last-hit and three nearby minions you do not. Then review two lane level-ups in player view. Record the visible level change, any ability it enabled, and whether your spacing changed before the next trade.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>Exact ranges, experience shares and level thresholds can change. Champion, role and mode rules can alter gains. This lesson does not prescribe an exact early level-up count.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/last-hitting\">Last-hitting<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/spacing\">Spacing<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/resource-management\">Resource management<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/how-to-play/\">Riot: How to Play<\/a> explains champion levels and ability growth. <a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/game-updates/patch-25-s1-1-notes/\">Riot: Patch 25.S1.1<\/a> documents minion experience range; <a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-gb/news/game-updates/patch-26-1-notes/\">Riot: Patch 26.1<\/a> documents solo/shared experience. Historical values are not reproduced as current rules.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026 (Pacific time). Examples and exercises are editorial teaching proposals, not validated rank-improvement methods.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Experience\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA38\n- Slug: experience\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Separate level progress from last-hit income.\n- Prerequisites: Last-hitting, Spacing.\n- Observable action: Check your level and progress before approaching a wave.\n\n## Short answer\n\nExperience is the resource that raises your champion’s level during a match. A level can improve stats and let you unlock or strengthen an ability. It is separate from gold and from your account level.\n\n## How it works\n\nWatch your experience bar as well as minion health. Lane minion experience has a proximity rule: missing a last hit does not necessarily mean missing experience. Sharing a lane changes experience received per champion. Do not assume a remembered minion count gives the same level timing after missed waves or shared experience. Compare visible levels before stepping forward, and learn the actual ability you unlock rather than treating every level as the same advantage.\n\n1. Check your level and progress before approaching a wave.\n2. Choose a position that preserves access without taking an unaffordable hit.\n3. Recheck both champions’ levels before the next exchange.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYou cannot safely approach a low-health minion for its last hit. You stay farther back where you can still receive experience, then see your experience bar rise as it dies. On a later wave, the opponent levels before you. You give space rather than contesting as though nothing changed. This assumes the safer position is within experience range; if it is not, concede the experience too.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA38 -->\n![Experience decision diagram: OBSERVE — Read level and experience bar.. CHOOSE — Keep safe access or concede.. RECHECK — A new level can change the exchange.](../visuals/assets/ma38-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative choices for experience; not measured game timing or positions.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Equating low CS with no experience, or risking death to avoid losing one minion.\n\n**Correction:** Track gold opportunities and level progress separately; survival may require conceding both.\n\n## Practice\n\nIn a practice session, watch the experience bar for three minions you last-hit and three nearby minions you do not. Then review two lane level-ups in player view. Record the visible level change, any ability it enabled, and whether your spacing changed before the next trade.\n\n## Limits\n\nExact ranges, experience shares and level thresholds can change. Champion, role and mode rules can alter gains. This lesson does not prescribe an exact early level-up count.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Last-hitting](../micro/MI01-last-hitting.md) · [Spacing](../micro/MI07-spacing.md) · [Resource management](../micro/MI16-resource-management.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Riot: How to Play](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/how-to-play/) explains champion levels and ability growth. [Riot: Patch 25.S1.1](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/game-updates/patch-25-s1-1-notes/) documents minion experience range; [Riot: Patch 26.1](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-gb/news/game-updates/patch-26-1-notes/) documents solo/shared experience. Historical values are not reproduced as current rules.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026 (Pacific time). Examples and exercises are editorial teaching proposals, not validated rank-improvement methods.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "35936d65c275792546fd7a7fb1344c56c538c5bd459ee7e5353a7e429519c4b0"}, {"id": "MA39", "slug": "map-awareness", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Turn visible map information into a decision before committing.", "title": "Map awareness", "file": "MA39-map-awareness.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Spacing.", "action": "Check the map before a commitment, when immediate movement is safe."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Map awareness means noticing relevant information beyond your immediate target and using it to choose your next action. It combines the minimap, nearby camera view and what remains unknown.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Fog of war hides areas outside your team’s vision. A missing enemy is unknown, not absent. Before moving forward or leaving lane, look for visible enemies, nearby allies and the route back. Use a brief camera check when an ally’s icon alone does not show whether they can help. Return attention to your champion before an immediate dodge or attack timing task. The aim is a useful decision, not staring at the minimap for a fixed percentage of play.<\/p><ol><li>Check the map before a commitment, when immediate movement is safe.<\/li><li>Separate a current sighting from an old sighting or guess.<\/li><li>Choose to continue, shorten the action or retreat; signal a relevant threat to allies.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>You are about to follow an opponent toward river. The minimap shows your nearby ally returning to base, while another enemy is missing. You stop at the known area and return to the wave. You do not need to prove an ambush exists to decide that the chase lacks support.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma39-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma39-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Map awareness decision diagram: LOOK — Enemies, allies and the route back.. SEPARATE — Seen now, seen earlier, unknown.. ACT — Continue, shorten or retreat.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative choices for map awareness; not measured game timing or positions.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Checking the minimap but continuing the original action unchanged.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Name the decision the new information changes. If it changes nothing, explain why.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Pause three replay moments before crossing into a less visible area. Use your team’s view. Write one current sighting, one unknown, an ally who can actually help and your chosen action. Resume and identify the first new cue that should change it. Do not use spectator information to grade the original choice.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>Icons and sightings cannot reveal intentions or future routes. This drill does not set a universal glance interval. Warding creates information; map awareness is using the information available.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/spacing\">Spacing<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/cooldown-tracking\">Cooldown tracking<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/how-to-play/\">Riot: How to Play<\/a> describes limited team vision and fog of war. The observation routine is editorial guidance, not a Riot-prescribed scan schedule.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026 (Pacific time). Examples and exercises are editorial teaching proposals, not validated rank-improvement methods.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Map awareness\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA39\n- Slug: map-awareness\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Turn visible map information into a decision before committing.\n- Prerequisites: Spacing.\n- Observable action: Check the map before a commitment, when immediate movement is safe.\n\n## Short answer\n\nMap awareness means noticing relevant information beyond your immediate target and using it to choose your next action. It combines the minimap, nearby camera view and what remains unknown.\n\n## How it works\n\nFog of war hides areas outside your team’s vision. A missing enemy is unknown, not absent. Before moving forward or leaving lane, look for visible enemies, nearby allies and the route back. Use a brief camera check when an ally’s icon alone does not show whether they can help. Return attention to your champion before an immediate dodge or attack timing task. The aim is a useful decision, not staring at the minimap for a fixed percentage of play.\n\n1. Check the map before a commitment, when immediate movement is safe.\n2. Separate a current sighting from an old sighting or guess.\n3. Choose to continue, shorten the action or retreat; signal a relevant threat to allies.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYou are about to follow an opponent toward river. The minimap shows your nearby ally returning to base, while another enemy is missing. You stop at the known area and return to the wave. You do not need to prove an ambush exists to decide that the chase lacks support.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA39 -->\n![Map awareness decision diagram: LOOK — Enemies, allies and the route back.. SEPARATE — Seen now, seen earlier, unknown.. ACT — Continue, shorten or retreat.](../visuals/assets/ma39-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative choices for map awareness; not measured game timing or positions.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Checking the minimap but continuing the original action unchanged.\n\n**Correction:** Name the decision the new information changes. If it changes nothing, explain why.\n\n## Practice\n\nPause three replay moments before crossing into a less visible area. Use your team’s view. Write one current sighting, one unknown, an ally who can actually help and your chosen action. Resume and identify the first new cue that should change it. Do not use spectator information to grade the original choice.\n\n## Limits\n\nIcons and sightings cannot reveal intentions or future routes. This drill does not set a universal glance interval. Warding creates information; map awareness is using the information available.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Spacing](../micro/MI07-spacing.md) · [Cooldown tracking](../micro/MI15-cooldown-tracking.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Riot: How to Play](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/how-to-play/) describes limited team vision and fog of war. The observation routine is editorial guidance, not a Riot-prescribed scan schedule.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026 (Pacific time). Examples and exercises are editorial teaching proposals, not validated rank-improvement methods.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "ed2f8235f8519e355271c8ee1fa2846306dfbbcc788bd20532452e788964e54d"}, {"id": "MA40", "slug": "wave-management", "category": "Macro", "summary": "Choose a wave plan before choosing how quickly to attack.", "title": "Wave management", "file": "MA40-wave-management.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Last-hitting, Experience, Map awareness.", "action": "Name the next job: stay and collect, prepare a departure, or create pressure."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Wave management means influencing where lane minions fight and how quickly they advance to support your next action. Last-hitting collects individual minions; wave management considers the whole wave and the next reinforcements.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Start with position, surviving minion health, incoming reinforcements and who can safely add damage. These affect the direction and speed of a push. Choose a purpose before adding attacks. A fast push clears quickly; a slow push builds an advancing wave; a freeze tries to keep successive collisions near one place. A crash is the wave reaching the enemy turret, while a bounce is a later reversal in push direction. Those are results to verify, not promises attached to your first attack.<\/p><ol><li>Name the next job: stay and collect, prepare a departure, or create pressure.<\/li><li>Choose a feasible hold, buildup or clear using the wave and nearby threats.<\/li><li>Observe the next collision and revise the plan if it does not produce the intended result.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>You want to return to base. If you can safely clear and finish the crash, a fast push may support that departure. If clearing would leave you exposed with no escape, leave with an imperfect wave instead. In a different state, when you are healthy and do not need to leave yet, a slow buildup may be useful. The purpose and constraints determine the choice.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/ma40-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/ma40-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Wave management decision diagram: PURPOSE — Collect, leave or create pressure.. PLAN — Hold, build or clear if feasible.. VERIFY — Watch the collision and revise.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative choices for wave management; not measured game timing or positions.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Always attacking the wave as fast as possible, or forcing a freeze regardless of the map.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Match the wave plan to a useful next action and abandon it when keeping it costs too much.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Pause three lane replays before you add damage. Record wave position, likely direction, your next job and chosen plan. Watch the next collision. Identify the damage or reinforcement that confirmed or changed your prediction. Use the following lessons to practise the specific plan you found hardest.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>No fixed minion count guarantees a hold or bounce. Champion damage and enemy interference can change the result. This overview introduces the choices; the following lessons explain each technique.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/last-hitting\">Last-hitting<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/experience\">Experience<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/map-awareness\">Map awareness<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/fast-pushing\">Fast pushing<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/event/hwei-abilities-rundown/\">Riot: Hwei Abilities Rundown<\/a> gives a champion-specific example of clearing to recall or roam and warns about exposure. It supports the purpose-and-risk framing, not a universal wave formula. The broader choice framework synthesizes the existing wave lessons.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026 (Pacific time). Examples and exercises are editorial teaching proposals, not validated rank-improvement methods.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Wave management\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MA40\n- Slug: wave-management\n- Category: Macro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Choose a wave plan before choosing how quickly to attack.\n- Prerequisites: Last-hitting, Experience, Map awareness.\n- Observable action: Name the next job: stay and collect, prepare a departure, or create pressure.\n\n## Short answer\n\nWave management means influencing where lane minions fight and how quickly they advance to support your next action. Last-hitting collects individual minions; wave management considers the whole wave and the next reinforcements.\n\n## How it works\n\nStart with position, surviving minion health, incoming reinforcements and who can safely add damage. These affect the direction and speed of a push. Choose a purpose before adding attacks. A fast push clears quickly; a slow push builds an advancing wave; a freeze tries to keep successive collisions near one place. A crash is the wave reaching the enemy turret, while a bounce is a later reversal in push direction. Those are results to verify, not promises attached to your first attack.\n\n1. Name the next job: stay and collect, prepare a departure, or create pressure.\n2. Choose a feasible hold, buildup or clear using the wave and nearby threats.\n3. Observe the next collision and revise the plan if it does not produce the intended result.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYou want to return to base. If you can safely clear and finish the crash, a fast push may support that departure. If clearing would leave you exposed with no escape, leave with an imperfect wave instead. In a different state, when you are healthy and do not need to leave yet, a slow buildup may be useful. The purpose and constraints determine the choice.\n\n<!-- Figure: MA40 -->\n![Wave management decision diagram: PURPOSE — Collect, leave or create pressure.. PLAN — Hold, build or clear if feasible.. VERIFY — Watch the collision and revise.](../visuals/assets/ma40-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative choices for wave management; not measured game timing or positions.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Always attacking the wave as fast as possible, or forcing a freeze regardless of the map.\n\n**Correction:** Match the wave plan to a useful next action and abandon it when keeping it costs too much.\n\n## Practice\n\nPause three lane replays before you add damage. Record wave position, likely direction, your next job and chosen plan. Watch the next collision. Identify the damage or reinforcement that confirmed or changed your prediction. Use the following lessons to practise the specific plan you found hardest.\n\n## Limits\n\nNo fixed minion count guarantees a hold or bounce. Champion damage and enemy interference can change the result. This overview introduces the choices; the following lessons explain each technique.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Last-hitting](../micro/MI01-last-hitting.md) · [Experience](../macro/MA38-experience.md) · [Map awareness](../macro/MA39-map-awareness.md) · [Fast pushing](../macro/MA13-fast-pushing.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Riot: Hwei Abilities Rundown](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/event/hwei-abilities-rundown/) gives a champion-specific example of clearing to recall or roam and warns about exposure. It supports the purpose-and-risk framing, not a universal wave formula. The broader choice framework synthesizes the existing wave lessons.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026 (Pacific time). Examples and exercises are editorial teaching proposals, not validated rank-improvement methods.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "e3af354df2cab8e33253113862c0fbd6dee59d2902dfc17a4eb922f24f36af5f"}, {"id": "MI01", "slug": "last-hitting", "category": "Micro", "summary": "Secure minion kills by matching damage arrival to the falling health bar.", "title": "Last-hitting", "file": "MI01-last-hitting.md", "meta": {"roles": "Top, Mid, Bot; Support when responsible for a wave", "skill_level": "Beginner", "prerequisites": "Basic movement and attack inputs; terms are explained here.", "action": ""}, "aliases": ["time-the-last-hit-before-the-health-bar-empties"], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Last-hitting means dealing the killing hit to a minion to collect its last-hit reward. Timing, target choice and the risk of approaching all matter.<\/p><p>Time your attack so its damage reaches the minion while that damage can finish it. Watch the falling health bar <strong>and incoming attacks<\/strong>. A ranged attack can arrive too late if you wait until the minion already looks ready to die. Riot explicitly discusses firing early in its <a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/game-updates/league-of-legends-patch-26-5-notes/\">last-hit indicator explanation<\/a>.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Read the health trend, then attack<\/h2><p>A <strong>last hit<\/strong> is the hit that kills a minion. <strong>Farming<\/strong> means collecting minions for income; <strong>CS<\/strong>, or creep score, records minions and monsters killed. A basic attack is your champion&#x27;s ordinary attack rather than an ability cast. This lesson practices lane minions, with no monster-clear instructions.<\/p><p>Your input and the damage arriving are different moments. For a ranged attack, notice the projectile leaving your champion and reaching its target. Treat the health bar as a changing situation: allied attacks may arrive before yours. The indicator documentation also lists limits on represented damage modifiers, so an assist marker is not a universal instruction to click now. This lesson does not require that feature or claim it is available in your queue. See Riot&#x27;s <a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/game-updates/league-of-legends-patch-26-5-notes/\">Last Hit Indicators section<\/a>.<\/p><p>Use this teaching sequence:<\/p><ol><li><strong>Choose a likely target.<\/strong> Look for a minion losing health, then notice visible allied attacks travelling toward it.<\/li><li><strong>Estimate the arrival window.<\/strong> Ask whether your hit would finish it when your damage arrives, rather than at the instant you click.<\/li><li><strong>Commit when the approach is affordable.<\/strong> If reaching the minion exposes you to an enemy threat, letting it go can be the better choice.<\/li><li><strong>Name the miss before changing your timing.<\/strong> Did your hit land too early, arrive after the minion died, select the wrong target, or encounter an unexpected incoming hit?<\/li><\/ol><p>Visible information supports an estimate. You cannot know every future minion target. Changes in your damage, temporary effects or attack rhythm can require a fresh estimate; one successful attempt is not a permanent threshold.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>You are farming with an ordinary ranged attack. A low-health caster minion has an allied shot approaching it. You click, but that shot kills the minion before yours arrives.<\/p><p>On the next comparable attempt, begin earlier and watch the arrival. If your damage lands while the minion still survives, you went too early for that situation. Adjust from what actually happened rather than deciding every miss needs an earlier click. These are illustrative attempts, not a recorded match or a champion-specific timing demonstration.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/mi01-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/mi01-desktop.svg\" alt=\"In a late attempt, an allied shot kills the minion before the player&#x27;s projectile arrives. An earlier input can secure a last hit only if the arriving damage is lethal.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>The click starts the attempt; damage arrival decides the last hit. An earlier input helps only if the arriving damage is enough to finish the minion.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><p>Figure files: <a href=\"assets/mi01-desktop.svg\">desktop SVG<\/a> · <a href=\"assets/mi01-mobile.svg\">mobile SVG<\/a> · <a href=\"assets/mi01-desktop.png\">desktop PNG<\/a> · <a href=\"assets/mi01-mobile.png\">mobile PNG<\/a>.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake → correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> You count every missed minion as bad timing and walk farther forward to rescue the next one.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Separate timing misses from minions conceded to danger or competing demands. Approaching has a cost. The lesson&#x27;s action is to time an affordable last hit; it does not require collecting every minion. Spending a spell may be reasonable in play, but it changes this isolated basic-attack exercise.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice: classify the arrival<\/h2><p>In a practice session, keep one champion and setup stable. Take three short samples of lane farming using basic attacks. For each attempted last hit, record secured, early arrival, late arrival or other/uncertain. Record deliberately conceded minions separately.<\/p><p>Compare the raw counts and choose one timing adjustment for the next sample. <a href=\"https://mobalytics.gg/lol/guides/essential-skills-mid-laners\">Mobalytics&#x27; Farming and CSing section<\/a> supports isolated farming practice; this recording method is our teaching proposal. A lower miss count is descriptive feedback, not a validated improvement promise or rank standard.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/spacing\">Spacing<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p>Reviewed basis: Riot Games, <em>Patch 26.5<\/em>, March 3, 2026, Last Hit Indicators; Mobalytics, <em>Essential Skills to Master for Mid Laners<\/em>, displayed publisher byline Mobalytics, updated October 31, 2025, Farming and CSing. Links above reopened for this draft. Riot supports the arrival principle; the second publisher supports practice. No universal turret recipe, current assist-access claim or exact damage threshold is included. Last editorial check: November 3, 2026 Pacific; independent article review completed on 3 November 2026 Pacific / 4 November UTC.<\/p><p>Editorial revision: concept scope updated for LEA-24 on 3 November 2026 Pacific. Prior review dates above refer to the inherited research package; revised prose received an integration review, not a new independent expert review. Examples and drills remain illustrative.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "---\nlesson_id: MI01\nslug: last-hitting\ncategory: Micro\nsummary: Secure minion kills by matching damage arrival to the falling health bar.\nroles: Top, Mid, Bot; Support when responsible for a wave\nskill_level: Beginner\nprerequisites: Basic movement and attack inputs; terms are explained here.\neditorial_check_date: 2026-11-03 US/Pacific\n---\n\n# Last-hitting\n\n## Short answer\n\nLast-hitting means dealing the killing hit to a minion to collect its last-hit reward. Timing, target choice and the risk of approaching all matter.\n\nTime your attack so its damage reaches the minion while that damage can finish it. Watch the falling health bar **and incoming attacks**. A ranged attack can arrive too late if you wait until the minion already looks ready to die. Riot explicitly discusses firing early in its [last-hit indicator explanation](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/game-updates/league-of-legends-patch-26-5-notes/).\n\n## Read the health trend, then attack\n\nA **last hit** is the hit that kills a minion. **Farming** means collecting minions for income; **CS**, or creep score, records minions and monsters killed. A basic attack is your champion's ordinary attack rather than an ability cast. This lesson practices lane minions, with no monster-clear instructions.\n\nYour input and the damage arriving are different moments. For a ranged attack, notice the projectile leaving your champion and reaching its target. Treat the health bar as a changing situation: allied attacks may arrive before yours. The indicator documentation also lists limits on represented damage modifiers, so an assist marker is not a universal instruction to click now. This lesson does not require that feature or claim it is available in your queue. See Riot's [Last Hit Indicators section](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/game-updates/league-of-legends-patch-26-5-notes/).\n\nUse this teaching sequence:\n\n1. **Choose a likely target.** Look for a minion losing health, then notice visible allied attacks travelling toward it.\n2. **Estimate the arrival window.** Ask whether your hit would finish it when your damage arrives, rather than at the instant you click.\n3. **Commit when the approach is affordable.** If reaching the minion exposes you to an enemy threat, letting it go can be the better choice.\n4. **Name the miss before changing your timing.** Did your hit land too early, arrive after the minion died, select the wrong target, or encounter an unexpected incoming hit?\n\nVisible information supports an estimate. You cannot know every future minion target. Changes in your damage, temporary effects or attack rhythm can require a fresh estimate; one successful attempt is not a permanent threshold.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYou are farming with an ordinary ranged attack. A low-health caster minion has an allied shot approaching it. You click, but that shot kills the minion before yours arrives.\n\nOn the next comparable attempt, begin earlier and watch the arrival. If your damage lands while the minion still survives, you went too early for that situation. Adjust from what actually happened rather than deciding every miss needs an earlier click. These are illustrative attempts, not a recorded match or a champion-specific timing demonstration.\n\n<!-- Figure: MI01; use one width variant at a time. -->\n![In a late attempt, an allied shot kills the minion before the player's projectile arrives. An earlier input can secure a last hit only if the arriving damage is lethal.](../visuals/previews/mi01-mobile.png)\n\n*The click starts the attempt; damage arrival decides the last hit. An earlier input helps only if the arriving damage is enough to finish the minion.*\n\nFigure files: [desktop SVG](../visuals/assets/mi01-desktop.svg) · [mobile SVG](../visuals/assets/mi01-mobile.svg) · [desktop PNG](../visuals/previews/mi01-desktop.png) · [mobile PNG](../visuals/previews/mi01-mobile.png).\n\n## Common mistake → correction\n\n**Mistake:** You count every missed minion as bad timing and walk farther forward to rescue the next one.\n\n**Correction:** Separate timing misses from minions conceded to danger or competing demands. Approaching has a cost. The lesson's action is to time an affordable last hit; it does not require collecting every minion. Spending a spell may be reasonable in play, but it changes this isolated basic-attack exercise.\n\n## Practice: classify the arrival\n\nIn a practice session, keep one champion and setup stable. Take three short samples of lane farming using basic attacks. For each attempted last hit, record secured, early arrival, late arrival or other/uncertain. Record deliberately conceded minions separately.\n\nCompare the raw counts and choose one timing adjustment for the next sample. [Mobalytics' Farming and CSing section](https://mobalytics.gg/lol/guides/essential-skills-mid-laners) supports isolated farming practice; this recording method is our teaching proposal. A lower miss count is descriptive feedback, not a validated improvement promise or rank standard.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Spacing](../micro/MI07-spacing.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\nReviewed basis: Riot Games, *Patch 26.5*, March 3, 2026, Last Hit Indicators; Mobalytics, *Essential Skills to Master for Mid Laners*, displayed publisher byline Mobalytics, updated October 31, 2025, Farming and CSing. Links above reopened for this draft. Riot supports the arrival principle; the second publisher supports practice. No universal turret recipe, current assist-access claim or exact damage threshold is included. Last editorial check: November 3, 2026 Pacific; independent article review completed on 3 November 2026 Pacific / 4 November UTC.\n\nEditorial revision: concept scope updated for LEA-24 on 3 November 2026 Pacific. Prior review dates above refer to the inherited research package; revised prose received an integration review, not a new independent expert review. Examples and drills remain illustrative.\n", "minutes": 4, "sha256": "d94720e6b52492977a28baa07a53690eec8e08f2a63396bba1d557fc0fcc9508"}, {"id": "MI06", "slug": "trading", "category": "Micro", "summary": "Exchange damage with a purpose, account for the cost, and choose when to stop.", "title": "Trading", "file": "MI06-trading.md", "meta": {"roles": "All five, in lane or champion exchanges", "skill_level": "Beginner/intermediate", "prerequisites": "Spacing, Cooldown tracking, Resource management.", "action": ""}, "aliases": ["trade-inside-an-observed-cooldown-window"], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Trading is an exchange of attacks or abilities between champions. A useful trade improves your position for the next action without spending more health, resources or farm than you can afford.<\/p><p>When you see an enemy spend the ability that most limits your approach, consider one short response. Check their remaining threats, your own tools and your way out before moving forward. A spent spell can create an opportunity; it does not establish a safe countdown. Both <a href=\"https://dignitas.gg/articles/top-5-tips-diamond-players-use-that-you-dont\">Dignitas&#x27; trading advice<\/a> and <a href=\"https://mobalytics.gg/lol/guides/essential-skills-mid-laners\">Mobalytics&#x27; Trading section<\/a> support looking for important cooldown openings.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Choose the kind of trade<\/h2><p>A short trade aims to end after one attack or a small set of spells. An extended trade continues through more attacks and decisions. Poke aims to deal damage from a position that limits a reply; retaliation answers an opponent’s commitment. An all-in commits to trying for a kill and is a different risk from a limited exchange. These describe intentions, not guaranteed outcomes.<\/p><p>Compare health lost and gained, casting resources spent, abilities left, minions missed and the position afterward. Dealing more damage does not make a trade useful if it leaves you unable to farm or escape. Matchups and the current state determine which length is affordable; this article supplies no universal winning sequence.<\/p><p>Two common cues are a spent ability and a last-hit attempt. When a low allied minion gives the opponent a reason to approach, you can consider pressure, but they may abandon it or answer you. Check your own farm and the second opponent in a duo lane. Use spacing to decide whether you can approach and leave safely.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Turn the cooldown cue into a bounded decision<\/h2><p>A <strong>cooldown<\/strong> is the wait before an ability can be used again. A <strong>trade<\/strong> is a limited exchange of attacks or abilities. The relevant <strong>window<\/strong> is the opportunity you assess after a cast, not a timer you can necessarily see. An ability with a <strong>recast<\/strong> may still have another activation; a reset may restore availability.<\/p><p>Two foundations make the cue useful. <strong>Spacing<\/strong> means managing distance from an enemy&#x27;s available reach, including movement abilities, while preserving an exit. <strong>Resources<\/strong> include health, mana or another casting resource, and abilities you still need after the exchange. Check what would remain if you take the action. The reviewed <a href=\"https://mobalytics.gg/lol/guides/how-to-retailiation-trade\">retaliation guide<\/a> supports considering state and resources; it does not supply a universal trade rule.<\/p><p>Use this editorial decision sequence:<\/p><ol><li><strong>Name the limiting tool.<\/strong> Pick the enemy ability that makes approaching dangerous. A minor cast is not automatically the opening you need.<\/li><li><strong>Observe its use.<\/strong> “I saw it cast” is evidence. “They have not cast it recently” leaves availability unknown. Account for a remaining recast or reset if relevant; decline when you cannot assess it.<\/li><li><strong>Check the whole exchange.<\/strong> Look at your health and usable tools, the minion wave, other enemies and any ally you would need. An ally busy farming may not follow. A missing enemy is not known to be far away.<\/li><li><strong>Choose an action and endpoint.<\/strong> Take one affordable attack or spell, use the space to farm or reposition, or decline. Name where you will stop before advancing. Reassess if the enemy changes the situation.<\/li><\/ol><p>The procedure is teaching synthesis. It does not reveal hidden skill ranks, cooldown modifiers or unseen opponents. Chasing may spend farm, time and the defensive ability you needed to leave.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>The opposing support misses the catch spell that has kept you back. Your lane partner is close enough to act, your own spell is ready, and your retreat route is open. You choose one poke, then return to your earlier position.<\/p><p>Change one condition: your partner is occupied under the turret and cannot help. You let the same cast create breathing room to farm instead. The observed ability use is unchanged; your response changes because support for the exchange is different. No duration or matchup victory is assumed.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/mi06-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/mi06-desktop.svg\" alt=\"A trade starts with a purpose, compares enemy responses and your costs, then ends at a chosen exit. A spent enemy spell alone does not justify continuing.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Trading is a complete exchange: include the reply and the position afterward. This is a decision schematic, not a damage calculation.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake → correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> “Their important spell is down, so I keep chasing.”<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> The cast justifies assessing an opening. It does not answer whether their other tools win the exchange or whether you can leave. End the action at the position you chose, and abandon it sooner if new threats appear. Taking space or declining can be a complete response.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice: record one response per cast<\/h2><p>Choose one important enemy ability in a replay. For its first five visible uses, pause before the outcome. Record <strong>trade, reposition or decline<\/strong>, plus one visible reason and your stopping point. Resume and note information you missed without rewriting the original decision.<\/p><p>The observable task is five explicit responses grounded in the cast and surrounding state. This is a proposed recognition drill, not a validated training method; winning the trade is not its score.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/spacing\">Spacing<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/cooldown-tracking\">Cooldown tracking<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/resource-management\">Resource management<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/poking\">Poking<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p>Dignitas / lolmjlauer, June 17, 2021, When to Trade / Knowing Key Cooldowns; Mobalytics, displayed byline Mobalytics, updated October 31, 2025, Trading; Mobalytics, updated October 30, 2025, retaliation guide, body credits PicklePants. Links above reopened for drafting. The opening principle has two coaching publishers; the conservative response sequence is editorial synthesis. No old champion timer, universal engage instruction or omniscient availability claim is imported. Last editorial check: November 3, 2026 Pacific; independent article review completed on 3 November 2026 Pacific / 4 November UTC.<\/p><p>Revision source note: Riot’s <a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/event/hwei-abilities-rundown/\">Hwei designer rundown, General Weaknesses<\/a> was checked for the principle that spent defensive tools and limited resources can create vulnerability. It is historical design context, not a current matchup prescription. The former last-hit pressure application also draws on Dignitas / Teatones, <a href=\"https://dignitas.gg/articles/how-to-improve-your-skillshots-in-league-of-legends\">Punish Positioning<\/a>, March 3, 2024, and Dignitas / Papril, <a href=\"https://dignitas.gg/articles/how-to-apply-pressure-as-a-support-during-lane\">support pressure<\/a>, April 5, 2026; retained from the reviewed package.<\/p><p>Editorial revision: concept scope updated for LEA-24 on 3 November 2026 Pacific. Prior review dates above refer to the inherited research package; revised prose received an integration review, not a new independent expert review. Examples and drills remain illustrative.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "---\nlesson_id: MI06\nslug: trading\ncategory: Micro\nsummary: Exchange damage with a purpose, account for the cost, and choose when to stop.\nroles: All five, in lane or champion exchanges\nskill_level: Beginner/intermediate\nprerequisites: Spacing, Cooldown tracking, Resource management.\neditorial_check_date: 2026-11-03 US/Pacific\n---\n\n# Trading\n\n## Short answer\n\nTrading is an exchange of attacks or abilities between champions. A useful trade improves your position for the next action without spending more health, resources or farm than you can afford.\n\nWhen you see an enemy spend the ability that most limits your approach, consider one short response. Check their remaining threats, your own tools and your way out before moving forward. A spent spell can create an opportunity; it does not establish a safe countdown. Both [Dignitas' trading advice](https://dignitas.gg/articles/top-5-tips-diamond-players-use-that-you-dont) and [Mobalytics' Trading section](https://mobalytics.gg/lol/guides/essential-skills-mid-laners) support looking for important cooldown openings.\n\n## Choose the kind of trade\n\nA short trade aims to end after one attack or a small set of spells. An extended trade continues through more attacks and decisions. Poke aims to deal damage from a position that limits a reply; retaliation answers an opponent’s commitment. An all-in commits to trying for a kill and is a different risk from a limited exchange. These describe intentions, not guaranteed outcomes.\n\nCompare health lost and gained, casting resources spent, abilities left, minions missed and the position afterward. Dealing more damage does not make a trade useful if it leaves you unable to farm or escape. Matchups and the current state determine which length is affordable; this article supplies no universal winning sequence.\n\nTwo common cues are a spent ability and a last-hit attempt. When a low allied minion gives the opponent a reason to approach, you can consider pressure, but they may abandon it or answer you. Check your own farm and the second opponent in a duo lane. Use spacing to decide whether you can approach and leave safely.\n\n## Turn the cooldown cue into a bounded decision\n\nA **cooldown** is the wait before an ability can be used again. A **trade** is a limited exchange of attacks or abilities. The relevant **window** is the opportunity you assess after a cast, not a timer you can necessarily see. An ability with a **recast** may still have another activation; a reset may restore availability.\n\nTwo foundations make the cue useful. **Spacing** means managing distance from an enemy's available reach, including movement abilities, while preserving an exit. **Resources** include health, mana or another casting resource, and abilities you still need after the exchange. Check what would remain if you take the action. The reviewed [retaliation guide](https://mobalytics.gg/lol/guides/how-to-retailiation-trade) supports considering state and resources; it does not supply a universal trade rule.\n\nUse this editorial decision sequence:\n\n1. **Name the limiting tool.** Pick the enemy ability that makes approaching dangerous. A minor cast is not automatically the opening you need.\n2. **Observe its use.** “I saw it cast” is evidence. “They have not cast it recently” leaves availability unknown. Account for a remaining recast or reset if relevant; decline when you cannot assess it.\n3. **Check the whole exchange.** Look at your health and usable tools, the minion wave, other enemies and any ally you would need. An ally busy farming may not follow. A missing enemy is not known to be far away.\n4. **Choose an action and endpoint.** Take one affordable attack or spell, use the space to farm or reposition, or decline. Name where you will stop before advancing. Reassess if the enemy changes the situation.\n\nThe procedure is teaching synthesis. It does not reveal hidden skill ranks, cooldown modifiers or unseen opponents. Chasing may spend farm, time and the defensive ability you needed to leave.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nThe opposing support misses the catch spell that has kept you back. Your lane partner is close enough to act, your own spell is ready, and your retreat route is open. You choose one poke, then return to your earlier position.\n\nChange one condition: your partner is occupied under the turret and cannot help. You let the same cast create breathing room to farm instead. The observed ability use is unchanged; your response changes because support for the exchange is different. No duration or matchup victory is assumed.\n\n<!-- Figure: MI06; use one width variant at a time. -->\n![A trade starts with a purpose, compares enemy responses and your costs, then ends at a chosen exit. A spent enemy spell alone does not justify continuing.](../visuals/assets/mi06-mobile.svg)\n\n*Trading is a complete exchange: include the reply and the position afterward. This is a decision schematic, not a damage calculation.*\n\n## Common mistake → correction\n\n**Mistake:** “Their important spell is down, so I keep chasing.”\n\n**Correction:** The cast justifies assessing an opening. It does not answer whether their other tools win the exchange or whether you can leave. End the action at the position you chose, and abandon it sooner if new threats appear. Taking space or declining can be a complete response.\n\n## Practice: record one response per cast\n\nChoose one important enemy ability in a replay. For its first five visible uses, pause before the outcome. Record **trade, reposition or decline**, plus one visible reason and your stopping point. Resume and note information you missed without rewriting the original decision.\n\nThe observable task is five explicit responses grounded in the cast and surrounding state. This is a proposed recognition drill, not a validated training method; winning the trade is not its score.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Spacing](../micro/MI07-spacing.md) · [Cooldown tracking](../micro/MI15-cooldown-tracking.md) · [Resource management](../micro/MI16-resource-management.md) · [Poking](../micro/MI18-poking.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\nDignitas / lolmjlauer, June 17, 2021, When to Trade / Knowing Key Cooldowns; Mobalytics, displayed byline Mobalytics, updated October 31, 2025, Trading; Mobalytics, updated October 30, 2025, retaliation guide, body credits PicklePants. Links above reopened for drafting. The opening principle has two coaching publishers; the conservative response sequence is editorial synthesis. No old champion timer, universal engage instruction or omniscient availability claim is imported. Last editorial check: November 3, 2026 Pacific; independent article review completed on 3 November 2026 Pacific / 4 November UTC.\n\nRevision source note: Riot’s [Hwei designer rundown, General Weaknesses](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/event/hwei-abilities-rundown/) was checked for the principle that spent defensive tools and limited resources can create vulnerability. It is historical design context, not a current matchup prescription. The former last-hit pressure application also draws on Dignitas / Teatones, [Punish Positioning](https://dignitas.gg/articles/how-to-improve-your-skillshots-in-league-of-legends), March 3, 2024, and Dignitas / Papril, [support pressure](https://dignitas.gg/articles/how-to-apply-pressure-as-a-support-during-lane), April 5, 2026; retained from the reviewed package.\n\nEditorial revision: concept scope updated for LEA-24 on 3 November 2026 Pacific. Prior review dates above refer to the inherited research package; revised prose received an integration review, not a new independent expert review. Examples and drills remain illustrative.\n", "minutes": 5, "sha256": "18d237ff53a6ced21d6a34c7da3fd55d8550b4882a625b342288f9d124800d75"}, {"id": "MI07", "slug": "spacing", "category": "Micro", "summary": "Manage distance from enemy threats while staying close enough to act.", "title": "Spacing", "file": "MI07-spacing.md", "meta": {"roles": "All five roles", "skill_level": "Beginner/intermediate", "prerequisites": "Basic movement and attack inputs; terms are explained here.", "action": ""}, "aliases": ["punish-a-last-hit-commitment-safely"], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p><strong>Spacing<\/strong> is managing the distance between your champion and other champions so you can act while limiting their available replies. It is an ongoing adjustment as players move and abilities become available. Good spacing balances safety with useful reach; standing too far away can stop you from contributing.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Compare reach, not just distance<\/h2><p><strong>Attack range<\/strong> is the distance at which a basic attack can reach a target. For this lesson, <strong>threat reach<\/strong> means the area an opponent could threaten with their currently available attacks, spells and movement. It is a teaching description, not a single in-game statistic. A dash or a spell can matter even when you stand outside ordinary attack range. Different tools threaten different shapes and directions.<\/p><p>Start with three questions: what can reach me, what can I reach, and where can I move next? If your chosen action reaches farther than the reply you are considering, a useful distance may let you act without entering that reply’s reach. That does not make the position safe from every other spell or enemy. If the opponent has more reach, you may need to stay back, approach only after a relevant tool is spent, or give up the action.<\/p><p>Spacing is different from <a href=\"#/lesson/dodging\">Dodging<\/a>, which avoids an aimed ability, and <a href=\"#/lesson/kiting\">Kiting<\/a>, which combines damage with movement. Spacing supplies the distance decisions for both. You can space without attacking at all, for example while waiting for an ally or declining a dangerous minion.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Adjust as the situation moves<\/h2><ol><li><strong>Name the relevant threat.<\/strong> Identify the enemy tool that limits your next action. Account for other enemies who can reach the same space. If a tool’s availability or reach is unknown, keep a margin instead of assuming an exact safe edge.<\/li><li><strong>Choose a useful position.<\/strong> Stay close enough for your intended action when the cost is acceptable, with room to leave. Terrain, the wave and allied positions can make equal distances very different choices.<\/li><li><strong>Respond to movement.<\/strong> When the enemy advances, reconsider your distance before continuing. When they retreat, moving forward may preserve pressure, but following into an unchecked area can give up your exit.<\/li><li><strong>Reassess after a cast.<\/strong> A spent ability can change the available threat. Other tools, recasts or a second enemy can still prevent an approach. Return to a safer distance when the opening ends or the information becomes unclear.<\/li><\/ol><p>Do not treat a drawn range edge as a guaranteed safety line. These are moving decisions, not a fixed circle that protects you. Reaction time, uncertainty and unfamiliar abilities justify extra room. Sometimes the useful choice is to stop dealing damage and leave.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>You are a ranged champion facing an opponent who, in this simplified example, has only a shorter-range attack available. You stay within your own reach while outside that attack’s reach. When the opponent walks toward you, you step back to preserve the gap; when they move away, you may follow only as far as your exit remains open.<\/p><p>Now change one condition: the opponent has an available gap-closing ability, or a second enemy approaches from the side. The old distance no longer answers the threat. You widen the gap or retreat toward help, even if that means losing a chance to attack. No champion range, guaranteed free hit or actual match outcome is asserted.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/mi07-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/mi07-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Two hypothetical positions compare your longer reach with an enemy’s shorter attack reach. When a gap closer or second enemy becomes available, the first distance is no longer sufficient; retreat toward an open exit.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Spacing changes with available threats. The schematic distances are illustrative, not champion ranges; the second case invalidates the first case’s assumption.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>A last-hit opportunity is only one application<\/h2><p>A dying allied minion can give the opponent a reason to step forward. You may use that movement to choose a trade, but the opponent can abandon the minion or answer with another tool. Spacing is the distance assessment before, during and after that attempt. The pressure decision belongs in <a href=\"#/lesson/trading\">Trading<\/a>; recognizing the minion’s health belongs in <a href=\"#/lesson/last-hitting\">Last-hitting<\/a>.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> “I am outside their basic attack range, so I am safe.” You hold position while a different ability or another enemy can reach you.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Name the actual threat to your intended action and keep an exit. Check it again when either player moves. Also avoid the opposite mistake: retreating so far that you cannot help an ally when a useful, affordable position is available.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice: explain the distance<\/h2><p>Pause a replay at five moments before an exchange. Mark your intended action, the nearest relevant enemy threat and an exit. Write whether you would hold, advance or retreat, and why. Resume from player view and note what movement or cast should change that answer. If you cannot identify a tool’s reach, record that gap for practice instead of filling in a precise number.<\/p><p>This is an editorial drill, not a validated performance test. Review the reason and the information available, not only whether you avoided damage.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/experience\">Experience<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p>Primary-source check: Riot Games / Myles “Riot Emizery” Salholm, <a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/event/hwei-abilities-rundown/\">Hwei Abilities Rundown<\/a>, General Weaknesses, Team Fighting and Late Game; opened for this revision on 3 November 2026 Pacific. The designer describes vulnerability after defensive tools are spent and the balance between maintaining distance and being too far away to support a team. This is historical design context, not certification of Hwei’s current numbers or a current matchup guide.<\/p><p>The distance comparison, hypothetical opponent and four-step process are editorial teaching synthesis. No exact ranges, cooldowns, hitbox rules or guaranteed safe margin are supplied. Previous MI07 research concerned last-hit pressure: Dignitas / Teatones, March 3, 2024, Punish Positioning; Dignitas / Papril, April 5, 2026, support pressure; Mobalytics / PicklePants, retaliation conditions, updated October 30, 2025. That research is retained with Trading and in the prior reviewed package; it is not passed off as a complete source for spacing. This rewrite has an integration review, not a new independent expert review.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "---\nlesson_id: MI07\nslug: spacing\ncategory: Micro\nsummary: Manage distance from enemy threats while staying close enough to act.\nroles: All five roles\nskill_level: Beginner/intermediate\nprerequisites: Basic movement and attack inputs; terms are explained here.\neditorial_check_date: 2026-11-03 US/Pacific\n---\n\n# Spacing\n\n## Short answer\n\n**Spacing** is managing the distance between your champion and other champions so you can act while limiting their available replies. It is an ongoing adjustment as players move and abilities become available. Good spacing balances safety with useful reach; standing too far away can stop you from contributing.\n\n## Compare reach, not just distance\n\n**Attack range** is the distance at which a basic attack can reach a target. For this lesson, **threat reach** means the area an opponent could threaten with their currently available attacks, spells and movement. It is a teaching description, not a single in-game statistic. A dash or a spell can matter even when you stand outside ordinary attack range. Different tools threaten different shapes and directions.\n\nStart with three questions: what can reach me, what can I reach, and where can I move next? If your chosen action reaches farther than the reply you are considering, a useful distance may let you act without entering that reply’s reach. That does not make the position safe from every other spell or enemy. If the opponent has more reach, you may need to stay back, approach only after a relevant tool is spent, or give up the action.\n\nSpacing is different from [Dodging](MI09-dodging.md), which avoids an aimed ability, and [Kiting](MI10-kiting.md), which combines damage with movement. Spacing supplies the distance decisions for both. You can space without attacking at all, for example while waiting for an ally or declining a dangerous minion.\n\n## Adjust as the situation moves\n\n1. **Name the relevant threat.** Identify the enemy tool that limits your next action. Account for other enemies who can reach the same space. If a tool’s availability or reach is unknown, keep a margin instead of assuming an exact safe edge.\n2. **Choose a useful position.** Stay close enough for your intended action when the cost is acceptable, with room to leave. Terrain, the wave and allied positions can make equal distances very different choices.\n3. **Respond to movement.** When the enemy advances, reconsider your distance before continuing. When they retreat, moving forward may preserve pressure, but following into an unchecked area can give up your exit.\n4. **Reassess after a cast.** A spent ability can change the available threat. Other tools, recasts or a second enemy can still prevent an approach. Return to a safer distance when the opening ends or the information becomes unclear.\n\nDo not treat a drawn range edge as a guaranteed safety line. These are moving decisions, not a fixed circle that protects you. Reaction time, uncertainty and unfamiliar abilities justify extra room. Sometimes the useful choice is to stop dealing damage and leave.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYou are a ranged champion facing an opponent who, in this simplified example, has only a shorter-range attack available. You stay within your own reach while outside that attack’s reach. When the opponent walks toward you, you step back to preserve the gap; when they move away, you may follow only as far as your exit remains open.\n\nNow change one condition: the opponent has an available gap-closing ability, or a second enemy approaches from the side. The old distance no longer answers the threat. You widen the gap or retreat toward help, even if that means losing a chance to attack. No champion range, guaranteed free hit or actual match outcome is asserted.\n\n<!-- Figure: MI07; use one width variant at a time. -->\n![Two hypothetical positions compare your longer reach with an enemy’s shorter attack reach. When a gap closer or second enemy becomes available, the first distance is no longer sufficient; retreat toward an open exit.](../visuals/assets/mi07-mobile.svg)\n\n*Spacing changes with available threats. The schematic distances are illustrative, not champion ranges; the second case invalidates the first case’s assumption.*\n\n## A last-hit opportunity is only one application\n\nA dying allied minion can give the opponent a reason to step forward. You may use that movement to choose a trade, but the opponent can abandon the minion or answer with another tool. Spacing is the distance assessment before, during and after that attempt. The pressure decision belongs in [Trading](MI06-trading.md); recognizing the minion’s health belongs in [Last-hitting](MI01-last-hitting.md).\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** “I am outside their basic attack range, so I am safe.” You hold position while a different ability or another enemy can reach you.\n\n**Correction:** Name the actual threat to your intended action and keep an exit. Check it again when either player moves. Also avoid the opposite mistake: retreating so far that you cannot help an ally when a useful, affordable position is available.\n\n## Practice: explain the distance\n\nPause a replay at five moments before an exchange. Mark your intended action, the nearest relevant enemy threat and an exit. Write whether you would hold, advance or retreat, and why. Resume from player view and note what movement or cast should change that answer. If you cannot identify a tool’s reach, record that gap for practice instead of filling in a precise number.\n\nThis is an editorial drill, not a validated performance test. Review the reason and the information available, not only whether you avoided damage.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Experience](../macro/MA38-experience.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\nPrimary-source check: Riot Games / Myles “Riot Emizery” Salholm, [Hwei Abilities Rundown](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/event/hwei-abilities-rundown/), General Weaknesses, Team Fighting and Late Game; opened for this revision on 3 November 2026 Pacific. The designer describes vulnerability after defensive tools are spent and the balance between maintaining distance and being too far away to support a team. This is historical design context, not certification of Hwei’s current numbers or a current matchup guide.\n\nThe distance comparison, hypothetical opponent and four-step process are editorial teaching synthesis. No exact ranges, cooldowns, hitbox rules or guaranteed safe margin are supplied. Previous MI07 research concerned last-hit pressure: Dignitas / Teatones, March 3, 2024, Punish Positioning; Dignitas / Papril, April 5, 2026, support pressure; Mobalytics / PicklePants, retaliation conditions, updated October 30, 2025. That research is retained with Trading and in the prior reviewed package; it is not passed off as a complete source for spacing. This rewrite has an integration review, not a new independent expert review.\n", "minutes": 5, "sha256": "8c7d2e0738b9073821ad817400483dbb6bed6c57c1e5a0e33c6f7ac5fb8261ff"}, {"id": "MI09", "slug": "dodging", "category": "Micro", "summary": "Avoid an aimed ability and choose a safe position after the movement.", "title": "Dodging", "file": "MI09-dodging.md", "meta": {"roles": "All five", "skill_level": "Beginner", "prerequisites": "Spacing, Skillshots.", "action": ""}, "aliases": ["dodge-a-linear-skillshot-toward-safety"], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Dodging means moving to avoid an aimed ability. It includes positioning before a cast and movement after one starts. This introduction develops the straight-projectile case; cones, ground areas and returning spells need their own path checks.<\/p><p>For a visible projectile travelling along a straight segment, move out to a reachable side when you have enough room and time. Choose the side that leaves a safer next position. <a href=\"https://dignitas.gg/articles/skills-versus-shots-a-guide-on-how-to-dodge-the-different-types-of-skillshots-in-league-of\">Dignitas&#x27; linear-skillshot guide<\/a> supports stepping off the path rather than retreating along it. That principle does not make every incoming spell reactively avoidable.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Check the path and the destination<\/h2><p>A <strong>skillshot<\/strong> is an aimed ability whose path or area can miss you. A <strong>projectile<\/strong> travels through space. This lesson concerns a visible, straight travelling segment. Imagine its <strong>corridor<\/strong> as the strip it threatens, allowing for the space your champion occupies; the drawn center line alone is not the whole danger.<\/p><p><strong>Collision<\/strong> means what the spell can hit or pass through. A nearby minion is useful cover only if that particular spell is blocked by it. <strong>Spacing<\/strong> means keeping distance from available threats while retaining somewhere to move. You need an exit before the projectile arrives, not merely empty ground elsewhere on the screen.<\/p><p>Our teaching sequence is:<\/p><ol><li><strong>Recognize the segment.<\/strong> Notice where the visible projectile is travelling. If you cannot identify the path in time, do not assume a reaction was available.<\/li><li><strong>Choose a reachable side exit.<\/strong> Compare nearby terrain, enemy positions and your onward route. A wall can remove an apparent exit; an enemy can make the other side dangerous.<\/li><li><strong>Move outside the corridor.<\/strong> If retreat would keep you on the shot&#x27;s line, consider the side route that actually leaves it. Avoid treating a tiny sideways click as proof you cleared the threatened strip.<\/li><li><strong>Reassess the next position.<\/strong> Continue toward safety when possible. Handle any return, split or follow-up as a new threat rather than assuming the first dodge solved it.<\/li><\/ol><p><a href=\"https://dignitas.gg/articles/blogs/League-of-Legends/4567/skillshot-dodging-general-theory-and-specific-tips\">Dignitas&#x27; dodging theory<\/a> discusses positional consequences, while its <a href=\"https://dignitas.gg/articles/improving-the-effectiveness-of-your-skillshots\">dodge-options analysis<\/a> illustrates constrained routes. The corridor explanation is geometric reasoning and the sequence is editorial synthesis, not measured dodge-success evidence.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>A visible spear-like projectile travels down an open lane toward you. Assume you have enough time and movement space to leave its corridor. One side leads toward allies; the other leads toward an enemy who can threaten you. You step toward the ally side, clear the path and continue along your retreat route.<\/p><p>Now put a wall beside that exit. The same click may no longer work. You must assess the remaining space rather than repeat the earlier direction. If neither side is reachable before contact, record that limit; this example supplies no guaranteed escape or champion-specific spell rule.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/mi09-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/mi09-desktop.svg\" alt=\"With enough time and room, the player can leave a straight projectile corridor toward allies. The other side leads toward an enemy. A wall can remove the preferred exit, requiring reassessment.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Leaving the projectile path is only part of the choice. Check the destination and whether an exit is reachable in time.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><p>Figure files: <a href=\"assets/mi09-desktop.svg\">desktop SVG<\/a> · <a href=\"assets/mi09-mobile.svg\">mobile SVG<\/a> · <a href=\"assets/mi09-desktop.png\">desktop PNG<\/a> · <a href=\"assets/mi09-mobile.png\">mobile PNG<\/a>.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake → correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> You avoid the projectile but finish beside another enemy, then call the dodge successful because the first spell missed.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Check where the movement leaves you. Avoiding one hit can still worsen your position. Conversely, being hit does not alone establish bad execution: a close cast, wide path or blocked exit may leave no reactive walking option. Earlier positioning and mobility decisions are separate questions.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice: mark the exit or its absence<\/h2><p>In a replay, choose ten visible straight projectiles aimed near you. Pause before contact. For each, mark a reachable side exit and its next position, or write <strong>no clear exit / uncertain<\/strong> with a reason. Resume and compare your chosen route with what happened.<\/p><p>The observable action is to identify a viable destination, including acknowledging its absence. This proposed drill has no validated pass threshold. Do not count an impossible reaction as a timing mistake or turn ten clips into a claim about your overall dodge rate.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/spacing\">Spacing<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/skillshots\">Skillshots<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/kiting\">Kiting<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p>Linked sources: Dignitas / Lucklepto, September 3, 2021, Dodging Linear Skillshots; Dignitas / k0nduit, October 29, 2018, projectile movement and positional discussion; Dignitas / Luigimash, July 5, 2021, Understanding Dodge Options. All reopened for this draft; all share one educational publisher. No experimental corroboration or probability is claimed. Targeted, tracking and circular spells, full return/split behavior, and universal beam reactions are outside scope. No exact hitbox widths or current map measurements are supplied. Last editorial check: November 3, 2026 Pacific; independent article review completed on 3 November 2026 Pacific / 4 November UTC.<\/p><p>Editorial revision: concept scope updated for LEA-24 on 3 November 2026 Pacific. Prior review dates above refer to the inherited research package; revised prose received an integration review, not a new independent expert review. Examples and drills remain illustrative.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "---\nlesson_id: MI09\nslug: dodging\ncategory: Micro\nsummary: Avoid an aimed ability and choose a safe position after the movement.\nroles: All five\nskill_level: Beginner\nprerequisites: Spacing, Skillshots.\neditorial_check_date: 2026-11-03 US/Pacific\n---\n\n# Dodging\n\n## Short answer\n\nDodging means moving to avoid an aimed ability. It includes positioning before a cast and movement after one starts. This introduction develops the straight-projectile case; cones, ground areas and returning spells need their own path checks.\n\nFor a visible projectile travelling along a straight segment, move out to a reachable side when you have enough room and time. Choose the side that leaves a safer next position. [Dignitas' linear-skillshot guide](https://dignitas.gg/articles/skills-versus-shots-a-guide-on-how-to-dodge-the-different-types-of-skillshots-in-league-of) supports stepping off the path rather than retreating along it. That principle does not make every incoming spell reactively avoidable.\n\n## Check the path and the destination\n\nA **skillshot** is an aimed ability whose path or area can miss you. A **projectile** travels through space. This lesson concerns a visible, straight travelling segment. Imagine its **corridor** as the strip it threatens, allowing for the space your champion occupies; the drawn center line alone is not the whole danger.\n\n**Collision** means what the spell can hit or pass through. A nearby minion is useful cover only if that particular spell is blocked by it. **Spacing** means keeping distance from available threats while retaining somewhere to move. You need an exit before the projectile arrives, not merely empty ground elsewhere on the screen.\n\nOur teaching sequence is:\n\n1. **Recognize the segment.** Notice where the visible projectile is travelling. If you cannot identify the path in time, do not assume a reaction was available.\n2. **Choose a reachable side exit.** Compare nearby terrain, enemy positions and your onward route. A wall can remove an apparent exit; an enemy can make the other side dangerous.\n3. **Move outside the corridor.** If retreat would keep you on the shot's line, consider the side route that actually leaves it. Avoid treating a tiny sideways click as proof you cleared the threatened strip.\n4. **Reassess the next position.** Continue toward safety when possible. Handle any return, split or follow-up as a new threat rather than assuming the first dodge solved it.\n\n[Dignitas' dodging theory](https://dignitas.gg/articles/blogs/League-of-Legends/4567/skillshot-dodging-general-theory-and-specific-tips) discusses positional consequences, while its [dodge-options analysis](https://dignitas.gg/articles/improving-the-effectiveness-of-your-skillshots) illustrates constrained routes. The corridor explanation is geometric reasoning and the sequence is editorial synthesis, not measured dodge-success evidence.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nA visible spear-like projectile travels down an open lane toward you. Assume you have enough time and movement space to leave its corridor. One side leads toward allies; the other leads toward an enemy who can threaten you. You step toward the ally side, clear the path and continue along your retreat route.\n\nNow put a wall beside that exit. The same click may no longer work. You must assess the remaining space rather than repeat the earlier direction. If neither side is reachable before contact, record that limit; this example supplies no guaranteed escape or champion-specific spell rule.\n\n<!-- Figure: MI09; use one width variant at a time. -->\n![With enough time and room, the player can leave a straight projectile corridor toward allies. The other side leads toward an enemy. A wall can remove the preferred exit, requiring reassessment.](../visuals/previews/mi09-mobile.png)\n\n*Leaving the projectile path is only part of the choice. Check the destination and whether an exit is reachable in time.*\n\nFigure files: [desktop SVG](../visuals/assets/mi09-desktop.svg) · [mobile SVG](../visuals/assets/mi09-mobile.svg) · [desktop PNG](../visuals/previews/mi09-desktop.png) · [mobile PNG](../visuals/previews/mi09-mobile.png).\n\n## Common mistake → correction\n\n**Mistake:** You avoid the projectile but finish beside another enemy, then call the dodge successful because the first spell missed.\n\n**Correction:** Check where the movement leaves you. Avoiding one hit can still worsen your position. Conversely, being hit does not alone establish bad execution: a close cast, wide path or blocked exit may leave no reactive walking option. Earlier positioning and mobility decisions are separate questions.\n\n## Practice: mark the exit or its absence\n\nIn a replay, choose ten visible straight projectiles aimed near you. Pause before contact. For each, mark a reachable side exit and its next position, or write **no clear exit / uncertain** with a reason. Resume and compare your chosen route with what happened.\n\nThe observable action is to identify a viable destination, including acknowledging its absence. This proposed drill has no validated pass threshold. Do not count an impossible reaction as a timing mistake or turn ten clips into a claim about your overall dodge rate.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Spacing](../micro/MI07-spacing.md) · [Skillshots](../micro/MI14-skillshots.md) · [Kiting](../micro/MI10-kiting.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\nLinked sources: Dignitas / Lucklepto, September 3, 2021, Dodging Linear Skillshots; Dignitas / k0nduit, October 29, 2018, projectile movement and positional discussion; Dignitas / Luigimash, July 5, 2021, Understanding Dodge Options. All reopened for this draft; all share one educational publisher. No experimental corroboration or probability is claimed. Targeted, tracking and circular spells, full return/split behavior, and universal beam reactions are outside scope. No exact hitbox widths or current map measurements are supplied. Last editorial check: November 3, 2026 Pacific; independent article review completed on 3 November 2026 Pacific / 4 November UTC.\n\nEditorial revision: concept scope updated for LEA-24 on 3 November 2026 Pacific. Prior review dates above refer to the inherited research package; revised prose received an integration review, not a new independent expert review. Examples and drills remain illustrative.\n", "minutes": 4, "sha256": "cc011a20005e27f502c33c31a682fc38155ba68c0b8a5a2b98f557714ee2e7fe"}, {"id": "MI10", "slug": "kiting", "category": "Micro", "summary": "Combine attacks and movement to manage distance from a target.", "title": "Kiting", "file": "MI10-kiting.md", "meta": {"roles": "Bot, Jungle, Top; Mid and Support using ordinary attacks", "skill_level": "Beginner/intermediate", "prerequisites": "Last-hitting, Spacing.", "action": ""}, "aliases": ["move-between-attacks-without-cancelling-them"], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Kiting combines damage with movement to manage distance from a target, often while retreating from a pursuer. Spacing decides where you want to be; attack timing determines when you can take the next step. This introduction uses ordinary basic attacks.<\/p><p>For an <strong>ordinary melee attack<\/strong>, move after its damage registers. For an <strong>ordinary ranged attack<\/strong>, move after the projectile leaves your champion&#x27;s model. Use the following recovery for a useful step, then attack again when ready if it remains safe. These distinct cues come from <a href=\"https://dignitas.gg/articles/the-fundamentals-of-auto-attacking-like-a-pro-in-league-of-legends\">Dignitas&#x27; ordinary-attack discussion<\/a>. Special attacks, resets and empowered exceptions are outside this lesson.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Learn one ordinary attack rhythm<\/h2><p>A basic or <strong>auto attack<\/strong> is your champion&#x27;s attack rather than a spell cast. Its <strong>windup<\/strong> is the part before the damage event or projectile release. <strong>Recovery<\/strong> is the following part before another attack is ready. Moving too early can cancel an intended ordinary attack. <strong>Kiting<\/strong> combines attacks with movement to manage distance.<\/p><p>Do not use the same visual event for both attack types. A melee learner checks that damage registered; a ranged learner checks that the projectile left the model. Damage reaching the ranged target is a later event. The source also explains that attack speed changes the rhythm. Its timing discussion is single-source coaching, not validation of every champion&#x27;s animations. See <a href=\"https://dignitas.gg/articles/the-fundamentals-of-auto-attacking-like-a-pro-in-league-of-legends\">What Makes Pros Good<\/a>.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/mi10-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/mi10-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Ordinary melee: start attack, wait for registered damage, move in recovery. Ordinary ranged: start attack, wait for projectile release, move in recovery without waiting for later impact. Reassess readiness and safety before attacking again.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Use registered damage for ordinary melee attacks and projectile release for ordinary ranged attacks. Movement still needs a useful destination.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><p>Figure files: <a href=\"assets/mi10-desktop.svg\">desktop SVG<\/a> · <a href=\"assets/mi10-mobile.svg\">mobile SVG<\/a> · <a href=\"assets/mi10-desktop.png\">desktop PNG<\/a> · <a href=\"assets/mi10-mobile.png\">mobile PNG<\/a>.<\/p><p>Apply this proposed learning sequence:<\/p><ol><li><strong>Choose one ordinary attack and stable setup.<\/strong> Use a practice target. Avoid empowered states, attack resets and unusual attack patterns so the exercise has one clear question.<\/li><li><strong>Observe before adding movement.<\/strong> Make stationary attacks and identify the relevant cue. If it is unclear, retain that uncertainty rather than inventing an exact frame or delay.<\/li><li><strong>Add one short, purposeful move.<\/strong> After the cue, step toward a chosen position, then issue the next attack when ready. Keep the target in reach for this isolated exercise.<\/li><li><strong>Reassess before repeating.<\/strong> A useful destination preserves distance from an available enemy threat and leaves an exit. If another attack would cost too much escape time, keep moving. Relearn the rhythm when attack speed changes.<\/li><\/ol><p>The actionable goal is one useful movement between preserved ordinary attacks. Clicking more often is not itself the goal. This lesson does not promise correct targeting from a particular control setting or teach pulling jungle monsters; camp boundaries and resets need separate instruction.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>A ranged carry has room to attack a pursuer and retreat toward allies. The carry lets the ordinary projectile leave the model, takes a short step toward those allies and considers the next attack. As the pursuer gains ground, the carry stops attacking and retreats continuously. The value of another hit no longer justifies the pause.<\/p><p>For a separate ordinary melee practice attempt, the movement cue would be registered damage, not a projectile. Neither illustration specifies a champion or demonstrates an exact animation. A precise champion cue demonstration requires validation before it is added.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake → correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> You copy a fast attack–move rhythm without checking whether your intended attacks completed.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Slow the exercise down. Watch the correct cue for your attack type, then verify the result. Also inspect the destination: movement that carries you toward an available threat can be poor even when the attack completes. When retreat is urgent, omitting the attack may be the better action. <a href=\"https://mobalytics.gg/lol/guides/how-to-retailiation-trade\">Mobalytics&#x27; retreat discussion<\/a> supports that safety limit, not the precise release cue.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice: preserve an attack while adding a step<\/h2><p>Use the same ordinary attack, target, items and attack speed for paired stationary and moving samples of equal duration. Exclude approach time. Record intended attacks that visibly fail to complete, completed attacks and whether each step had a chosen destination. Repeat after one deliberate adjustment.<\/p><p>Compare your own samples without a percentage pass mark. If the cue remains ambiguous, mark it uncertain for review. This is a teaching proposal, not a validated performance test, and dummy results do not establish combat safety.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/last-hitting\">Last-hitting<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/spacing\">Spacing<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/map-awareness\">Map awareness<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p>Dignitas / lolmjlauer, March 29, 2022, <em>The Fundamentals of Auto Attacking Like a Pro<\/em>, What Makes Pros Good; Mobalytics, updated October 30, 2025, retaliation guide, retreat section, body credit PicklePants. Links above reopened for drafting. The exact ordinary-attack cue remains supported by one educational source. No client experiment, champion cue demo, exact timing, special/empowered attack rule or Target Champions Only interaction is asserted. Last editorial check: November 3, 2026 Pacific; independent article review completed on 3 November 2026 Pacific / 4 November UTC.<\/p><p>Editorial revision: concept scope updated for LEA-24 on 3 November 2026 Pacific. Prior review dates above refer to the inherited research package; revised prose received an integration review, not a new independent expert review. Examples and drills remain illustrative.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "---\nlesson_id: MI10\nslug: kiting\ncategory: Micro\nsummary: Combine attacks and movement to manage distance from a target.\nroles: Bot, Jungle, Top; Mid and Support using ordinary attacks\nskill_level: Beginner/intermediate\nprerequisites: Last-hitting, Spacing.\neditorial_check_date: 2026-11-03 US/Pacific\n---\n\n# Kiting\n\n## Short answer\n\nKiting combines damage with movement to manage distance from a target, often while retreating from a pursuer. Spacing decides where you want to be; attack timing determines when you can take the next step. This introduction uses ordinary basic attacks.\n\nFor an **ordinary melee attack**, move after its damage registers. For an **ordinary ranged attack**, move after the projectile leaves your champion's model. Use the following recovery for a useful step, then attack again when ready if it remains safe. These distinct cues come from [Dignitas' ordinary-attack discussion](https://dignitas.gg/articles/the-fundamentals-of-auto-attacking-like-a-pro-in-league-of-legends). Special attacks, resets and empowered exceptions are outside this lesson.\n\n## Learn one ordinary attack rhythm\n\nA basic or **auto attack** is your champion's attack rather than a spell cast. Its **windup** is the part before the damage event or projectile release. **Recovery** is the following part before another attack is ready. Moving too early can cancel an intended ordinary attack. **Kiting** combines attacks with movement to manage distance.\n\nDo not use the same visual event for both attack types. A melee learner checks that damage registered; a ranged learner checks that the projectile left the model. Damage reaching the ranged target is a later event. The source also explains that attack speed changes the rhythm. Its timing discussion is single-source coaching, not validation of every champion's animations. See [What Makes Pros Good](https://dignitas.gg/articles/the-fundamentals-of-auto-attacking-like-a-pro-in-league-of-legends).\n\n<!-- Figure: MI10; use one width variant at a time. -->\n![Ordinary melee: start attack, wait for registered damage, move in recovery. Ordinary ranged: start attack, wait for projectile release, move in recovery without waiting for later impact. Reassess readiness and safety before attacking again.](../visuals/previews/mi10-mobile.png)\n\n*Use registered damage for ordinary melee attacks and projectile release for ordinary ranged attacks. Movement still needs a useful destination.*\n\nFigure files: [desktop SVG](../visuals/assets/mi10-desktop.svg) · [mobile SVG](../visuals/assets/mi10-mobile.svg) · [desktop PNG](../visuals/previews/mi10-desktop.png) · [mobile PNG](../visuals/previews/mi10-mobile.png).\n\nApply this proposed learning sequence:\n\n1. **Choose one ordinary attack and stable setup.** Use a practice target. Avoid empowered states, attack resets and unusual attack patterns so the exercise has one clear question.\n2. **Observe before adding movement.** Make stationary attacks and identify the relevant cue. If it is unclear, retain that uncertainty rather than inventing an exact frame or delay.\n3. **Add one short, purposeful move.** After the cue, step toward a chosen position, then issue the next attack when ready. Keep the target in reach for this isolated exercise.\n4. **Reassess before repeating.** A useful destination preserves distance from an available enemy threat and leaves an exit. If another attack would cost too much escape time, keep moving. Relearn the rhythm when attack speed changes.\n\nThe actionable goal is one useful movement between preserved ordinary attacks. Clicking more often is not itself the goal. This lesson does not promise correct targeting from a particular control setting or teach pulling jungle monsters; camp boundaries and resets need separate instruction.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nA ranged carry has room to attack a pursuer and retreat toward allies. The carry lets the ordinary projectile leave the model, takes a short step toward those allies and considers the next attack. As the pursuer gains ground, the carry stops attacking and retreats continuously. The value of another hit no longer justifies the pause.\n\nFor a separate ordinary melee practice attempt, the movement cue would be registered damage, not a projectile. Neither illustration specifies a champion or demonstrates an exact animation. A precise champion cue demonstration requires validation before it is added.\n\n## Common mistake → correction\n\n**Mistake:** You copy a fast attack–move rhythm without checking whether your intended attacks completed.\n\n**Correction:** Slow the exercise down. Watch the correct cue for your attack type, then verify the result. Also inspect the destination: movement that carries you toward an available threat can be poor even when the attack completes. When retreat is urgent, omitting the attack may be the better action. [Mobalytics' retreat discussion](https://mobalytics.gg/lol/guides/how-to-retailiation-trade) supports that safety limit, not the precise release cue.\n\n## Practice: preserve an attack while adding a step\n\nUse the same ordinary attack, target, items and attack speed for paired stationary and moving samples of equal duration. Exclude approach time. Record intended attacks that visibly fail to complete, completed attacks and whether each step had a chosen destination. Repeat after one deliberate adjustment.\n\nCompare your own samples without a percentage pass mark. If the cue remains ambiguous, mark it uncertain for review. This is a teaching proposal, not a validated performance test, and dummy results do not establish combat safety.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Last-hitting](../micro/MI01-last-hitting.md) · [Spacing](../micro/MI07-spacing.md) · [Map awareness](../macro/MA39-map-awareness.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\nDignitas / lolmjlauer, March 29, 2022, *The Fundamentals of Auto Attacking Like a Pro*, What Makes Pros Good; Mobalytics, updated October 30, 2025, retaliation guide, retreat section, body credit PicklePants. Links above reopened for drafting. The exact ordinary-attack cue remains supported by one educational source. No client experiment, champion cue demo, exact timing, special/empowered attack rule or Target Champions Only interaction is asserted. Last editorial check: November 3, 2026 Pacific; independent article review completed on 3 November 2026 Pacific / 4 November UTC.\n\nEditorial revision: concept scope updated for LEA-24 on 3 November 2026 Pacific. Prior review dates above refer to the inherited research package; revised prose received an integration review, not a new independent expert review. Examples and drills remain illustrative.\n", "minutes": 4, "sha256": "375b4372224bb535d2ca35b7bf0c7808cd5f63c2da895bf892ac73a8fee8aa42"}, {"id": "MI11", "slug": "peeling", "category": "Micro", "summary": "Protect an ally by disrupting the threat that can reach them.", "title": "Peeling", "file": "MI11-peeling.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Crowd control, Positioning.", "action": "Name the ally you need to protect and the enemy approach that threatens them. Check whether your relevant tool is ready."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Peeling means preventing an enemy from reaching or continuing to attack a vulnerable ally. You can use suitable crowd control, positioning or protective tools to give that ally room to move and act.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Identify the threat to the ally before choosing your target. The closest enemy to you may not be the one about to reach them. Stay close enough to help without placing both of you in the same danger. Save a suitable response for the approach you expect, then reassess after it is used. The goal is useful space for the ally, not necessarily a kill on the attacker.<\/p><ol><li>Name the ally you need to protect and the enemy approach that threatens them. Check whether your relevant tool is ready.<\/li><li>Position so the tool can reach the threat or ally. Avoid chasing so far forward that you cannot respond.<\/li><li>Use the tool when it can change the approach, then move with the ally’s exit. Look for the next threat instead of assuming the fight is solved.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>An enemy frontline champion is within your attack range, but a second opponent approaches your damage dealer from the side. You stop following the frontline and keep your control ability available for the side approach. Once you disrupt that attacker, you retreat with your ally instead of chasing the attacker away from them.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/mi11-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/mi11-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Side threat → vulnerable ally. Watch the dangerous approach. Protector uses a ready tool. Create space for the ally. Ally moves toward safety. Stay able to answer next threat.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for peeling; positions and timing are not game measurements.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Using every control ability to start a fight, then having no response when an enemy reaches the ally behind you.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Before committing, decide whether your current job is to start the fight or protect someone. Change that job when the visible threats change.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>In three team-fight replays, pause just before an enemy reaches an ally. Identify the threat, your relevant tool and your distance from the ally. Watch whether your previous action left you able to help. Write one earlier position or ability choice that would preserve that option.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>Peeling does not guarantee survival. Some attacks cannot be stopped by the tool you have, and multiple threats can exceed your resources. Check the specific ability interaction in game; this lesson does not assert that every dash or cast can be interrupted.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/crowd-control\">Crowd control<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/positioning\">Positioning<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/engaging\">Engaging<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/game-updates/patch-11-15-notes/\">Source and context<\/a>. Riot’s patch 11.15 Thresh commentary (21 July 2021) explicitly describes peeling enemies away from vulnerable allies. It supports the concept, not current ability numbers. The threat assessment, hypothetical example and drill are editorial synthesis.<\/p><p>Sources checked 4 November 2026. Examples and exercises are teaching proposals, not tested performance guarantees. No live patch certification or independent expert review is claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Peeling\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MI11\n- Slug: peeling\n- Category: Micro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Protect an ally by disrupting the threat that can reach them.\n- Prerequisites: Crowd control, Positioning.\n- Observable action: Name the ally you need to protect and the enemy approach that threatens them. Check whether your relevant tool is ready.\n\n## Short answer\n\nPeeling means preventing an enemy from reaching or continuing to attack a vulnerable ally. You can use suitable crowd control, positioning or protective tools to give that ally room to move and act.\n\n## How it works\n\nIdentify the threat to the ally before choosing your target. The closest enemy to you may not be the one about to reach them. Stay close enough to help without placing both of you in the same danger. Save a suitable response for the approach you expect, then reassess after it is used. The goal is useful space for the ally, not necessarily a kill on the attacker.\n\n1. Name the ally you need to protect and the enemy approach that threatens them. Check whether your relevant tool is ready.\n2. Position so the tool can reach the threat or ally. Avoid chasing so far forward that you cannot respond.\n3. Use the tool when it can change the approach, then move with the ally’s exit. Look for the next threat instead of assuming the fight is solved.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nAn enemy frontline champion is within your attack range, but a second opponent approaches your damage dealer from the side. You stop following the frontline and keep your control ability available for the side approach. Once you disrupt that attacker, you retreat with your ally instead of chasing the attacker away from them.\n\n<!-- Figure: MI11 -->\n![Side threat → vulnerable ally. Watch the dangerous approach. Protector uses a ready tool. Create space for the ally. Ally moves toward safety. Stay able to answer next threat.](../visuals/assets/mi11-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for peeling; positions and timing are not game measurements.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Using every control ability to start a fight, then having no response when an enemy reaches the ally behind you.\n\n**Correction:** Before committing, decide whether your current job is to start the fight or protect someone. Change that job when the visible threats change.\n\n## Practice\n\nIn three team-fight replays, pause just before an enemy reaches an ally. Identify the threat, your relevant tool and your distance from the ally. Watch whether your previous action left you able to help. Write one earlier position or ability choice that would preserve that option.\n\n## Limits\n\nPeeling does not guarantee survival. Some attacks cannot be stopped by the tool you have, and multiple threats can exceed your resources. Check the specific ability interaction in game; this lesson does not assert that every dash or cast can be interrupted.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Crowd control](../micro/MI21-crowd-control.md) · [Positioning](../micro/MI12-positioning.md) · [Engaging](../micro/MI13-engaging.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Source and context](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/game-updates/patch-11-15-notes/). Riot’s patch 11.15 Thresh commentary (21 July 2021) explicitly describes peeling enemies away from vulnerable allies. It supports the concept, not current ability numbers. The threat assessment, hypothetical example and drill are editorial synthesis.\n\nSources checked 4 November 2026. Examples and exercises are teaching proposals, not tested performance guarantees. No live patch certification or independent expert review is claimed.\n", "minutes": 3, "sha256": "f701993c67689697474f1d058231ebdbe18009fc27a4f0fe876a479ba8eef8e4"}, {"id": "MI12", "slug": "positioning", "category": "Micro", "summary": "Choose a place where you can do your job and respond to danger.", "title": "Positioning", "file": "MI12-positioning.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Spacing, Map awareness.", "action": "Name your current job and the enemy most able to stop it."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Positioning means choosing where to stand and move relative to allies, enemies and terrain. In a fight, a useful position lets you contribute while keeping a response to the threats that matter.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Your role changes the useful position. A damage dealer needs access to a safe target; a protector needs access to an ally. Distance from one opponent is only part of the picture. Spacing handles that local range relationship; positioning includes the whole fight.<\/p><ol><li>Name your current job and the enemy most able to stop it.<\/li><li>Choose a reachable target or ally and an exit.<\/li><li>Move again when a threat appears or an important ability is used.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>You stand behind your frontline, but an enemy approaches from a side entrance. The frontline no longer separates you from that threat. You move toward a teammate who can help and attack the reachable target instead of crossing the fight to reach a preferred one.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/mi12-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/mi12-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Damage dealer → reachable target. Stay able to contribute. Enemy approaches from the side. Frontline is no longer cover. You → ally and an exit. Rebuild a useful position.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for positioning; positions and timing are not game measurements.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Treating “behind the tank” as safe in every situation.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Check side approaches and the path you would take if forced back.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Pause three fights just before you take damage. Mark your job, the dangerous approach and an exit. Rewind one action. Identify a position that would preserve your contribution and a response.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>No spot removes all risk. Very safe positions can also put you too far away to help. This lesson does not prescribe champion-specific ranges.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/spacing\">Spacing<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/map-awareness\">Map awareness<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/target-selection\">Target selection<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://dignitas.gg/articles/positioning-kiting-and-tanking-how-to-win-every-teamfight\">Source and context<\/a>. Ahrivve’s original guide supports role-based positioning and staying able to contribute. Old champion and item examples are not carried over.<\/p><p>Sources checked 4 November 2026. Examples and exercises are teaching proposals, not tested performance guarantees. No live patch certification or independent expert review is claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Positioning\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MI12\n- Slug: positioning\n- Category: Micro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Choose a place where you can do your job and respond to danger.\n- Prerequisites: Spacing, Map awareness.\n- Observable action: Name your current job and the enemy most able to stop it.\n\n## Short answer\n\nPositioning means choosing where to stand and move relative to allies, enemies and terrain. In a fight, a useful position lets you contribute while keeping a response to the threats that matter.\n\n## How it works\n\nYour role changes the useful position. A damage dealer needs access to a safe target; a protector needs access to an ally. Distance from one opponent is only part of the picture. Spacing handles that local range relationship; positioning includes the whole fight.\n\n1. Name your current job and the enemy most able to stop it.\n2. Choose a reachable target or ally and an exit.\n3. Move again when a threat appears or an important ability is used.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYou stand behind your frontline, but an enemy approaches from a side entrance. The frontline no longer separates you from that threat. You move toward a teammate who can help and attack the reachable target instead of crossing the fight to reach a preferred one.\n\n<!-- Figure: MI12 -->\n![Damage dealer → reachable target. Stay able to contribute. Enemy approaches from the side. Frontline is no longer cover. You → ally and an exit. Rebuild a useful position.](../visuals/assets/mi12-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for positioning; positions and timing are not game measurements.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Treating “behind the tank” as safe in every situation.\n\n**Correction:** Check side approaches and the path you would take if forced back.\n\n## Practice\n\nPause three fights just before you take damage. Mark your job, the dangerous approach and an exit. Rewind one action. Identify a position that would preserve your contribution and a response.\n\n## Limits\n\nNo spot removes all risk. Very safe positions can also put you too far away to help. This lesson does not prescribe champion-specific ranges.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Spacing](../micro/MI07-spacing.md) · [Map awareness](../macro/MA39-map-awareness.md) · [Target selection](../micro/MI22-target-selection.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Source and context](https://dignitas.gg/articles/positioning-kiting-and-tanking-how-to-win-every-teamfight). Ahrivve’s original guide supports role-based positioning and staying able to contribute. Old champion and item examples are not carried over.\n\nSources checked 4 November 2026. Examples and exercises are teaching proposals, not tested performance guarantees. No live patch certification or independent expert review is claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "0fb00a198735451ea3ec471056f0d91cbb0347fbfadbdcf92e031f8424c3c2f7"}, {"id": "MI13", "slug": "engaging", "category": "Micro", "summary": "Start a fight when allies can follow and the target is worth the commitment.", "title": "Engaging", "file": "MI13-engaging.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Crowd control, Target selection.", "action": "Name the intended target and what winning this fight would enable."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Engaging means initiating a fight or forcing an opponent to respond to a committed threat. A successful opening needs more than contact: your team must be able to use it.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Compare your reach with the path your allies must travel. A target in your range may still be outside their useful range. Consider health, available abilities and the purpose of fighting before you go.<\/p><ol><li>Name the intended target and what winning this fight would enable.<\/li><li>Check that allies can follow without crossing an unmanageable threat.<\/li><li>Signal the attempt, then cancel if the opening or support disappears.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>You can dash onto an enemy near a wall, but your damage dealer must walk around it. You wait rather than treating the dash as permission. When the enemy steps toward your team, you recheck the opening from the new positions.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/mi13-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/mi13-desktop.svg\" alt=\"You → target across a wall. Your reach is not team reach. Allies must walk around. Check their route and tools. Wait or commit together. Cancel if support disappears.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for engaging; positions and timing are not game measurements.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Judging an engage only by whether your first ability lands.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Review whether teammates could contribute during the opening.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>For three attempted engages, pause before the first committed movement. Trace each nearby ally’s path to the target. Write “follow-up available”, “delayed” or “unclear”, then compare with what happened.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>Teammates may choose not to follow. A ping is a signal, not a guarantee. Sometimes protecting an ally is more valuable than starting a fight.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/crowd-control\">Crowd control<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/target-selection\">Target selection<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/disengaging\">Disengaging<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://dignitas.gg/articles/a-comprehensive-support-guide-with-dignitas-biofrost\">Source and context<\/a>. The Biofrost interview supports engage and protection as different team responsibilities. The follow-up audit is an original teaching exercise.<\/p><p>Sources checked 4 November 2026. Examples and exercises are teaching proposals, not tested performance guarantees. No live patch certification or independent expert review is claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Engaging\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MI13\n- Slug: engaging\n- Category: Micro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Start a fight when allies can follow and the target is worth the commitment.\n- Prerequisites: Crowd control, Target selection.\n- Observable action: Name the intended target and what winning this fight would enable.\n\n## Short answer\n\nEngaging means initiating a fight or forcing an opponent to respond to a committed threat. A successful opening needs more than contact: your team must be able to use it.\n\n## How it works\n\nCompare your reach with the path your allies must travel. A target in your range may still be outside their useful range. Consider health, available abilities and the purpose of fighting before you go.\n\n1. Name the intended target and what winning this fight would enable.\n2. Check that allies can follow without crossing an unmanageable threat.\n3. Signal the attempt, then cancel if the opening or support disappears.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYou can dash onto an enemy near a wall, but your damage dealer must walk around it. You wait rather than treating the dash as permission. When the enemy steps toward your team, you recheck the opening from the new positions.\n\n<!-- Figure: MI13 -->\n![You → target across a wall. Your reach is not team reach. Allies must walk around. Check their route and tools. Wait or commit together. Cancel if support disappears.](../visuals/assets/mi13-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for engaging; positions and timing are not game measurements.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Judging an engage only by whether your first ability lands.\n\n**Correction:** Review whether teammates could contribute during the opening.\n\n## Practice\n\nFor three attempted engages, pause before the first committed movement. Trace each nearby ally’s path to the target. Write “follow-up available”, “delayed” or “unclear”, then compare with what happened.\n\n## Limits\n\nTeammates may choose not to follow. A ping is a signal, not a guarantee. Sometimes protecting an ally is more valuable than starting a fight.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Crowd control](../micro/MI21-crowd-control.md) · [Target selection](../micro/MI22-target-selection.md) · [Disengaging](../micro/MI23-disengaging.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Source and context](https://dignitas.gg/articles/a-comprehensive-support-guide-with-dignitas-biofrost). The Biofrost interview supports engage and protection as different team responsibilities. The follow-up audit is an original teaching exercise.\n\nSources checked 4 November 2026. Examples and exercises are teaching proposals, not tested performance guarantees. No live patch certification or independent expert review is claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "2665b2b4b09211e9134f836db0ebf216eced12a64c9a6a07cae5ff1dc581036e"}, {"id": "MI14", "slug": "skillshots", "category": "Micro", "summary": "Aim at a plausible movement path, then check what made the shot land or miss.", "title": "Skillshots", "file": "MI14-skillshots.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Spacing, Resource management.", "action": "Check the spell's shape and blockers."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Skillshots are aimed abilities whose success depends on where and when you cast, rather than simply selecting a target. Their shapes, travel and collision rules differ.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Watch the target&#x27;s available movement before aiming. A narrow route can reduce their choices; open ground gives them more exits. Check whether this particular spell passes through units. A blocked shot is a different error from aiming behind a moving target.<\/p><ol><li>Check the spell&#x27;s shape and blockers.<\/li><li>Watch the target&#x27;s next likely movement.<\/li><li>Cast only when the hit is worth the spent ability.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>An opponent walks toward a minion to attack it. You aim along that approach, but they turn away. You keep your distance while the spell recovers instead of following to force a second attempt.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/mi14-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/mi14-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Decision diagram for skillshots: AIM — Target approaches minion; predict route. CAST — Check blockers before release. REVIEW — Compare aim with arrival.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for skillshots; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Aiming at the current position every time.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Compare cast position with the target&#x27;s position when the spell arrives.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Review five casts. Label each miss as aim, timing, blocker or target movement. Choose the most common cause and repeat five attempts with that single correction.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>Prediction is uncertain. Some spells have different collision rules or can change direction; read the current ability description.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/spacing\">Spacing<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/resource-management\">Resource management<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/dodging\">Dodging<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-may-14/\">Riot: Champion Counterplay<\/a>. Primary design context for tactical responses and champion strengths and weaknesses. It is not a current ability reference.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Skillshots\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MI14\n- Slug: skillshots\n- Category: Micro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Aim at a plausible movement path, then check what made the shot land or miss.\n- Prerequisites: Spacing, Resource management.\n- Observable action: Check the spell's shape and blockers.\n\n## Short answer\n\nSkillshots are aimed abilities whose success depends on where and when you cast, rather than simply selecting a target. Their shapes, travel and collision rules differ.\n\n## How it works\n\nWatch the target's available movement before aiming. A narrow route can reduce their choices; open ground gives them more exits. Check whether this particular spell passes through units. A blocked shot is a different error from aiming behind a moving target.\n\n1. Check the spell's shape and blockers.\n2. Watch the target's next likely movement.\n3. Cast only when the hit is worth the spent ability.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nAn opponent walks toward a minion to attack it. You aim along that approach, but they turn away. You keep your distance while the spell recovers instead of following to force a second attempt.\n\n<!-- Figure: MI14 -->\n![Decision diagram for skillshots: AIM — Target approaches minion; predict route. CAST — Check blockers before release. REVIEW — Compare aim with arrival.](../visuals/assets/mi14-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for skillshots; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Aiming at the current position every time.\n\n**Correction:** Compare cast position with the target's position when the spell arrives.\n\n## Practice\n\nReview five casts. Label each miss as aim, timing, blocker or target movement. Choose the most common cause and repeat five attempts with that single correction.\n\n## Limits\n\nPrediction is uncertain. Some spells have different collision rules or can change direction; read the current ability description.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Spacing](../micro/MI07-spacing.md) · [Resource management](../micro/MI16-resource-management.md) · [Dodging](../micro/MI09-dodging.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Riot: Champion Counterplay](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-may-14/). Primary design context for tactical responses and champion strengths and weaknesses. It is not a current ability reference.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "cc5817d4538447ee6f8dd2dcafaa667dae93d96cff8497d352443016f5391e4b"}, {"id": "MI15", "slug": "cooldown-tracking", "category": "Micro", "summary": "Use observed casts to estimate which threats are temporarily unavailable.", "title": "Cooldown tracking", "file": "MI15-cooldown-tracking.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Skillshots, Resource management.", "action": "Name the important ability you saw used."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Cooldown tracking means keeping track of when an ability has been used and when it may return. It helps you judge a threat window without treating a guess as a visible timer.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Separate your own displayed cooldowns from enemy estimates. An observed cast is evidence; an unseen cast is not. Rank, haste and reset effects can change return times. Use a conservative window when the exact timing is unknown, and watch for other tools that still threaten you.<\/p><ol><li>Name the important ability you saw used.<\/li><li>Check the remaining enemy tools and nearby allies.<\/li><li>End the advance before your estimate becomes unsafe.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>The enemy misses a control spell. You take a short trade while it is unavailable, then retreat. Their damage spell remains ready, so you do not treat the missed control spell as permission for an all-in.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/mi15-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/mi15-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Decision diagram for cooldown tracking: OBSERVE — Control spell used. WINDOW — Other threats still ready. EXIT — Leave before uncertain return.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for cooldown tracking; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Counting a remembered base cooldown as exact.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Use a cautious estimate and update it when you learn more.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Review three trades after a visible enemy cast. Record the evidence, the tool still available and when you stopped. Check whether you stayed after the original opening ended.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>Resets and champion-specific rules can invalidate a count. This lesson gives no patch-specific timer.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/skillshots\">Skillshots<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/resource-management\">Resource management<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/crowd-control\">Crowd control<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-may-14/\">Riot: Champion Counterplay<\/a>. Primary design context for tactical responses and champion strengths and weaknesses. It is not a current ability reference.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Cooldown tracking\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MI15\n- Slug: cooldown-tracking\n- Category: Micro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Use observed casts to estimate which threats are temporarily unavailable.\n- Prerequisites: Skillshots, Resource management.\n- Observable action: Name the important ability you saw used.\n\n## Short answer\n\nCooldown tracking means keeping track of when an ability has been used and when it may return. It helps you judge a threat window without treating a guess as a visible timer.\n\n## How it works\n\nSeparate your own displayed cooldowns from enemy estimates. An observed cast is evidence; an unseen cast is not. Rank, haste and reset effects can change return times. Use a conservative window when the exact timing is unknown, and watch for other tools that still threaten you.\n\n1. Name the important ability you saw used.\n2. Check the remaining enemy tools and nearby allies.\n3. End the advance before your estimate becomes unsafe.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nThe enemy misses a control spell. You take a short trade while it is unavailable, then retreat. Their damage spell remains ready, so you do not treat the missed control spell as permission for an all-in.\n\n<!-- Figure: MI15 -->\n![Decision diagram for cooldown tracking: OBSERVE — Control spell used. WINDOW — Other threats still ready. EXIT — Leave before uncertain return.](../visuals/assets/mi15-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for cooldown tracking; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Counting a remembered base cooldown as exact.\n\n**Correction:** Use a cautious estimate and update it when you learn more.\n\n## Practice\n\nReview three trades after a visible enemy cast. Record the evidence, the tool still available and when you stopped. Check whether you stayed after the original opening ended.\n\n## Limits\n\nResets and champion-specific rules can invalidate a count. This lesson gives no patch-specific timer.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Skillshots](../micro/MI14-skillshots.md) · [Resource management](../micro/MI16-resource-management.md) · [Crowd control](../micro/MI21-crowd-control.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Riot: Champion Counterplay](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-may-14/). Primary design context for tactical responses and champion strengths and weaknesses. It is not a current ability reference.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "585a7fcbec23e6345ae07b2b491cf7610957de4640aeafea8c9eb6b907b079ab"}, {"id": "MI16", "slug": "resource-management", "category": "Micro", "summary": "Spend health and ability resources with the next action in mind.", "title": "Resource management", "file": "MI16-resource-management.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Last-hitting.", "action": "Name your next likely action."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Resource management is choosing how to spend and preserve health, mana, energy or other champion resources. The cost of an action includes what you will be unable to do afterward.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Read your champion&#x27;s actual resource bar and costs. Before using an ability for farm or poke, consider whether the remaining resources support an escape, another wave or a fight. Health also changes which spaces you can safely occupy. An unused resource has no value if saving it costs a more important opportunity.<\/p><ol><li>Name your next likely action.<\/li><li>Check its resource requirement in game.<\/li><li>Choose the current cast with that reserve in mind.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>You could use a spell to secure a difficult minion, but doing so would leave too little mana for your escape. With the enemy jungler unaccounted for, you let the minion go and keep the escape option.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/mi16-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/mi16-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Decision diagram for resource management: PLAN — Next action needs a resource. SPEND — Check what remains after cast. ADAPT — Change plan if reserve is gone.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for resource management; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Saving every spell until you lose useful farm or pressure.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Reserve for a specific purpose instead of refusing all spending.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Review one lane sequence. At three casts, pause and record the resource remaining and your next intended action. Identify one cast that helped that plan and one that prevented it.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>Some champions do not use mana, and resource recovery differs. Use the current champion&#x27;s rules rather than a universal reserve percentage.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/last-hitting\">Last-hitting<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/skillshots\">Skillshots<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-may-14/\">Riot: Champion Counterplay<\/a>. Primary design context for tactical responses and champion strengths and weaknesses. It is not a current ability reference.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Resource management\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MI16\n- Slug: resource-management\n- Category: Micro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Spend health and ability resources with the next action in mind.\n- Prerequisites: Last-hitting.\n- Observable action: Name your next likely action.\n\n## Short answer\n\nResource management is choosing how to spend and preserve health, mana, energy or other champion resources. The cost of an action includes what you will be unable to do afterward.\n\n## How it works\n\nRead your champion's actual resource bar and costs. Before using an ability for farm or poke, consider whether the remaining resources support an escape, another wave or a fight. Health also changes which spaces you can safely occupy. An unused resource has no value if saving it costs a more important opportunity.\n\n1. Name your next likely action.\n2. Check its resource requirement in game.\n3. Choose the current cast with that reserve in mind.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYou could use a spell to secure a difficult minion, but doing so would leave too little mana for your escape. With the enemy jungler unaccounted for, you let the minion go and keep the escape option.\n\n<!-- Figure: MI16 -->\n![Decision diagram for resource management: PLAN — Next action needs a resource. SPEND — Check what remains after cast. ADAPT — Change plan if reserve is gone.](../visuals/assets/mi16-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for resource management; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Saving every spell until you lose useful farm or pressure.\n\n**Correction:** Reserve for a specific purpose instead of refusing all spending.\n\n## Practice\n\nReview one lane sequence. At three casts, pause and record the resource remaining and your next intended action. Identify one cast that helped that plan and one that prevented it.\n\n## Limits\n\nSome champions do not use mana, and resource recovery differs. Use the current champion's rules rather than a universal reserve percentage.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Last-hitting](../micro/MI01-last-hitting.md) · [Skillshots](../micro/MI14-skillshots.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Riot: Champion Counterplay](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-may-14/). Primary design context for tactical responses and champion strengths and weaknesses. It is not a current ability reference.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "4636ba71f472bb6963ed1f12926ea769cd7ee8e8655ad5a88141090713885b75"}, {"id": "MI17", "slug": "all-ins", "category": "Micro", "summary": "Recognise when a short exchange becomes a committed fight.", "title": "All-ins", "file": "MI17-all-ins.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Trading, Crowd control, Summoner spells.", "action": "Check what can stop your damage."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>An all-in is a committed attempt to finish a fight, usually spending major tools and accepting that a quick exit may no longer be available. It differs from a trade you intend to end after a small exchange.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Compare health, levels, available abilities, escape routes and possible reinforcements. Decide whether you can keep dealing damage long enough to finish. The opponent may survive the first burst or retreat toward help. A low health bar alone is not enough evidence.<\/p><ol><li>Check what can stop your damage.<\/li><li>Identify enemy escape tools and incoming help.<\/li><li>Commit only with a credible finish or exit plan.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>An enemy is low, but your main ability is still unavailable and their ally is moving up. You stop after a short trade. Later, with your tools ready and the ally elsewhere, you reassess instead of assuming the first chance still exists.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/mi17-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/mi17-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Decision diagram for all-ins: CHECK — Health plus tools plus help. COMMIT — Exit becomes harder. UPDATE — Finish or take remaining exit.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for all-ins; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Confusing low enemy health with a guaranteed kill.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Compare usable tools and positions as well as health.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Review three committed fights. Mark the last moment you could stop cheaply, the information available then and the tool you needed to finish. Judge that decision before checking the result.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>Matchups and mechanics can change the outcome. This is a commitment check, not a damage calculator.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/trading\">Trading<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/crowd-control\">Crowd control<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/summoner-spells\">Summoner spells<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/zoning\">Zoning<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-may-14/\">Riot: Champion Counterplay<\/a>. Primary design context for tactical responses and champion strengths and weaknesses. It is not a current ability reference.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# All-ins\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MI17\n- Slug: all-ins\n- Category: Micro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Recognise when a short exchange becomes a committed fight.\n- Prerequisites: Trading, Crowd control, Summoner spells.\n- Observable action: Check what can stop your damage.\n\n## Short answer\n\nAn all-in is a committed attempt to finish a fight, usually spending major tools and accepting that a quick exit may no longer be available. It differs from a trade you intend to end after a small exchange.\n\n## How it works\n\nCompare health, levels, available abilities, escape routes and possible reinforcements. Decide whether you can keep dealing damage long enough to finish. The opponent may survive the first burst or retreat toward help. A low health bar alone is not enough evidence.\n\n1. Check what can stop your damage.\n2. Identify enemy escape tools and incoming help.\n3. Commit only with a credible finish or exit plan.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nAn enemy is low, but your main ability is still unavailable and their ally is moving up. You stop after a short trade. Later, with your tools ready and the ally elsewhere, you reassess instead of assuming the first chance still exists.\n\n<!-- Figure: MI17 -->\n![Decision diagram for all-ins: CHECK — Health plus tools plus help. COMMIT — Exit becomes harder. UPDATE — Finish or take remaining exit.](../visuals/assets/mi17-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for all-ins; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Confusing low enemy health with a guaranteed kill.\n\n**Correction:** Compare usable tools and positions as well as health.\n\n## Practice\n\nReview three committed fights. Mark the last moment you could stop cheaply, the information available then and the tool you needed to finish. Judge that decision before checking the result.\n\n## Limits\n\nMatchups and mechanics can change the outcome. This is a commitment check, not a damage calculator.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Trading](../micro/MI06-trading.md) · [Crowd control](../micro/MI21-crowd-control.md) · [Summoner spells](../micro/MI26-summoner-spells.md) · [Zoning](../micro/MI19-zoning.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Riot: Champion Counterplay](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-may-14/). Primary design context for tactical responses and champion strengths and weaknesses. It is not a current ability reference.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "eab5ecf8bd55c4c5f5ae400ccc3c66a69b21658a0cb410582a1f9e3dbe70ddca"}, {"id": "MI18", "slug": "poking", "category": "Micro", "summary": "Deal damage at low commitment while preserving a safe position.", "title": "Poking", "file": "MI18-poking.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Trading, Skillshots.", "action": "Choose the target whose lost health matters."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Poking means using repeated attacks or abilities to reduce enemy health before a full fight. The purpose is to change their next decision, such as forcing them away from an entrance or making a contest harder.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Compare the health pressure you create with the resources and safety you spend. A hit can be unhelpful if it costs your only escape or prevents the next wave clear. Move after casting so the opponent cannot answer along the same predictable line.<\/p><ol><li>Choose the target whose lost health matters.<\/li><li>Keep an exit while using the poke tool.<\/li><li>Recheck resources and enemy engagement range.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>Before a river contest, you land a ranged spell on the enemy frontline. Your team is still arriving, so you step back instead of chasing for another hit. The damage can help later without starting a fight now.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/mi18-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/mi18-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Decision diagram for poking: PRESSURE — Chip health before a fight. COST — Resources and position change. RESET — Step back; keep the exit.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for poking; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Walking into engagement range to land one more spell.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Keep the low-commitment purpose of poke in mind.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Review five poke attempts. Record damage dealt, resources spent and whether you stayed safe. Pick one cast that was technically accurate but strategically costly, and explain why.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>Enemy sustain can reduce the value of poke. Do not assume chip damage creates a lasting advantage.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/trading\">Trading<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/skillshots\">Skillshots<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/all-ins\">All-ins<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://dignitas.gg/articles/team-composition-guide-to-poke-compositions-in-league-of-legends\">soduh / Dignitas: Poke Compositions<\/a>. Original coaching context distinguishing poke from siege and explaining disengage (2021). Historical builds, champion numbers and matchup prescriptions are excluded.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Poking\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MI18\n- Slug: poking\n- Category: Micro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Deal damage at low commitment while preserving a safe position.\n- Prerequisites: Trading, Skillshots.\n- Observable action: Choose the target whose lost health matters.\n\n## Short answer\n\nPoking means using repeated attacks or abilities to reduce enemy health before a full fight. The purpose is to change their next decision, such as forcing them away from an entrance or making a contest harder.\n\n## How it works\n\nCompare the health pressure you create with the resources and safety you spend. A hit can be unhelpful if it costs your only escape or prevents the next wave clear. Move after casting so the opponent cannot answer along the same predictable line.\n\n1. Choose the target whose lost health matters.\n2. Keep an exit while using the poke tool.\n3. Recheck resources and enemy engagement range.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nBefore a river contest, you land a ranged spell on the enemy frontline. Your team is still arriving, so you step back instead of chasing for another hit. The damage can help later without starting a fight now.\n\n<!-- Figure: MI18 -->\n![Decision diagram for poking: PRESSURE — Chip health before a fight. COST — Resources and position change. RESET — Step back; keep the exit.](../visuals/assets/mi18-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for poking; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Walking into engagement range to land one more spell.\n\n**Correction:** Keep the low-commitment purpose of poke in mind.\n\n## Practice\n\nReview five poke attempts. Record damage dealt, resources spent and whether you stayed safe. Pick one cast that was technically accurate but strategically costly, and explain why.\n\n## Limits\n\nEnemy sustain can reduce the value of poke. Do not assume chip damage creates a lasting advantage.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Trading](../micro/MI06-trading.md) · [Skillshots](../micro/MI14-skillshots.md) · [All-ins](../micro/MI17-all-ins.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[soduh / Dignitas: Poke Compositions](https://dignitas.gg/articles/team-composition-guide-to-poke-compositions-in-league-of-legends). Original coaching context distinguishing poke from siege and explaining disengage (2021). Historical builds, champion numbers and matchup prescriptions are excluded.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "7660425c8b1e433e62775887b7ed7da814f0b44250a8278362626148c8aefa91"}, {"id": "MI19", "slug": "zoning", "category": "Micro", "summary": "Use a credible threat to restrict where an opponent can stand.", "title": "Zoning", "file": "MI19-zoning.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Spacing, Trading.", "action": "Name the space you want to deny."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Zoning means making an area costly for an enemy to enter through position, abilities or the threat of an attack. It can protect an ally, deny access to a wave or delay an approach without dealing damage.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>A zone depends on a threat you can actually use. If your main ability is down or enemies can overwhelm you, standing forward may offer no control. Check the ally or resource you are protecting, then hold a position from which you can respond and leave.<\/p><ol><li>Name the space you want to deny.<\/li><li>Check whether your threat is credible.<\/li><li>Release the space when the threat or support disappears.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>Your team is taking an objective. You hold a nearby entrance with a control spell ready. When a second enemy appears and your allies cannot help, you back toward them instead of defending the entrance alone.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/mi19-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/mi19-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Decision diagram for zoning: HOLD — Entrance covered by ready tool. DENY — Opponent chooses another route. RELEASE — Support leaves; retreat.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for zoning; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Thinking an aggressive position automatically controls space.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Tie the position to a ready tool and reachable support.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Watch three moments when an opponent changes route near you. Identify the likely threat and what would remove it. Then review one failed attempt to zone and name the missing condition.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>Some opponents can ignore or cross your threat. Zoning is conditional pressure, not an invisible wall.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/spacing\">Spacing<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/trading\">Trading<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/baiting\">Baiting<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-may-14/\">Riot: Champion Counterplay<\/a>. Primary design context for tactical responses and champion strengths and weaknesses. It is not a current ability reference.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Zoning\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MI19\n- Slug: zoning\n- Category: Micro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Use a credible threat to restrict where an opponent can stand.\n- Prerequisites: Spacing, Trading.\n- Observable action: Name the space you want to deny.\n\n## Short answer\n\nZoning means making an area costly for an enemy to enter through position, abilities or the threat of an attack. It can protect an ally, deny access to a wave or delay an approach without dealing damage.\n\n## How it works\n\nA zone depends on a threat you can actually use. If your main ability is down or enemies can overwhelm you, standing forward may offer no control. Check the ally or resource you are protecting, then hold a position from which you can respond and leave.\n\n1. Name the space you want to deny.\n2. Check whether your threat is credible.\n3. Release the space when the threat or support disappears.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYour team is taking an objective. You hold a nearby entrance with a control spell ready. When a second enemy appears and your allies cannot help, you back toward them instead of defending the entrance alone.\n\n<!-- Figure: MI19 -->\n![Decision diagram for zoning: HOLD — Entrance covered by ready tool. DENY — Opponent chooses another route. RELEASE — Support leaves; retreat.](../visuals/assets/mi19-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for zoning; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Thinking an aggressive position automatically controls space.\n\n**Correction:** Tie the position to a ready tool and reachable support.\n\n## Practice\n\nWatch three moments when an opponent changes route near you. Identify the likely threat and what would remove it. Then review one failed attempt to zone and name the missing condition.\n\n## Limits\n\nSome opponents can ignore or cross your threat. Zoning is conditional pressure, not an invisible wall.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Spacing](../micro/MI07-spacing.md) · [Trading](../micro/MI06-trading.md) · [Baiting](../micro/MI24-baiting.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Riot: Champion Counterplay](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-may-14/). Primary design context for tactical responses and champion strengths and weaknesses. It is not a current ability reference.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "c456ba58550863c541de461ec5f235b9c3c2b6d7760b64e3c85858722395b971"}, {"id": "MI20", "slug": "body-blocking", "category": "Micro", "summary": "Intercept a blockable threat only when the exchange helps your team.", "title": "Body blocking", "file": "MI20-body-blocking.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Skillshots, Positioning.", "action": "Check that the threat can hit an intervening unit."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Body blocking means placing your champion between a threat and its intended target to intercept it. This lesson concerns attacks or projectiles that can be blocked by another unit, not a promise to obstruct every movement or spell.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Confirm the ability&#x27;s collision rule first. Consider whether the intercepted hit also stops you, damages nearby allies or exposes you to a follow-up. Protecting an ally can be worthwhile, but moving into every projectile may create a worse target for the enemy.<\/p><ol><li>Check that the threat can hit an intervening unit.<\/li><li>Compare your survival with the ally&#x27;s risk.<\/li><li>Move onto the line while preserving a useful escape.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>A vulnerable teammate retreats from a single-target projectile that stops at the first champion. You step into its line with enough health to survive, then both retreat. You do not stack beside the ally against an area attack.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/mi20-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/mi20-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Decision diagram for body blocking: LINE — Caster → interceptor → ally. CHECK — Does this spell stop on contact?. EXIT — Survive the follow-up together.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for body blocking; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Trying to block a spell that passes through you.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Verify the specific spell interaction before relying on it.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>In a replay, find three incoming projectiles. Draw the caster-to-target line and identify collision behavior from the ability description. State whether interception would help and what you would risk.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>Piercing, area and targeted effects behave differently. Current ability text and controlled practice must confirm the interaction.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/skillshots\">Skillshots<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/positioning\">Positioning<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/peeling\">Peeling<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-may-14/\">Riot: Champion Counterplay<\/a>. Primary design context for tactical responses and champion strengths and weaknesses. It is not a current ability reference.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Body blocking\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MI20\n- Slug: body-blocking\n- Category: Micro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Intercept a blockable threat only when the exchange helps your team.\n- Prerequisites: Skillshots, Positioning.\n- Observable action: Check that the threat can hit an intervening unit.\n\n## Short answer\n\nBody blocking means placing your champion between a threat and its intended target to intercept it. This lesson concerns attacks or projectiles that can be blocked by another unit, not a promise to obstruct every movement or spell.\n\n## How it works\n\nConfirm the ability's collision rule first. Consider whether the intercepted hit also stops you, damages nearby allies or exposes you to a follow-up. Protecting an ally can be worthwhile, but moving into every projectile may create a worse target for the enemy.\n\n1. Check that the threat can hit an intervening unit.\n2. Compare your survival with the ally's risk.\n3. Move onto the line while preserving a useful escape.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nA vulnerable teammate retreats from a single-target projectile that stops at the first champion. You step into its line with enough health to survive, then both retreat. You do not stack beside the ally against an area attack.\n\n<!-- Figure: MI20 -->\n![Decision diagram for body blocking: LINE — Caster → interceptor → ally. CHECK — Does this spell stop on contact?. EXIT — Survive the follow-up together.](../visuals/assets/mi20-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for body blocking; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Trying to block a spell that passes through you.\n\n**Correction:** Verify the specific spell interaction before relying on it.\n\n## Practice\n\nIn a replay, find three incoming projectiles. Draw the caster-to-target line and identify collision behavior from the ability description. State whether interception would help and what you would risk.\n\n## Limits\n\nPiercing, area and targeted effects behave differently. Current ability text and controlled practice must confirm the interaction.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Skillshots](../micro/MI14-skillshots.md) · [Positioning](../micro/MI12-positioning.md) · [Peeling](../micro/MI11-peeling.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Riot: Champion Counterplay](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-may-14/). Primary design context for tactical responses and champion strengths and weaknesses. It is not a current ability reference.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "4e32058c4fb9e0429ec2211279a0472faa4a5139b117a301f41c5e4619459041"}, {"id": "MI21", "slug": "crowd-control", "category": "Micro", "summary": "Use movement and action restrictions for a clear follow-up.", "title": "Crowd control", "file": "MI21-crowd-control.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Skillshots.", "action": "Identify the action you need to stop."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Crowd control is an effect that restricts an enemy&#x27;s movement or actions. Different types restrict different things; a slow, root and stun are not interchangeable.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Choose the effect for the job: interrupt an approach, hold a target for damage or give an ally time to escape. Coordinate multiple effects so useful control is not wasted, while allowing overlap when immediate certainty matters. Check which actions the target can still take.<\/p><ol><li>Identify the action you need to stop.<\/li><li>Confirm your effect can stop that action.<\/li><li>Use it when allies can exploit the result.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>An enemy approaches your carry. You use a movement restriction to delay them, but keep moving because they may still cast. Your teammate uses the space to retreat rather than standing still to attack.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/mi21-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/mi21-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Decision diagram for crowd control: JOB — Stop movement or another action. EFFECT — Choose a matching control tool. FOLLOW — Allies act during the window.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for crowd control; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Assuming any control effect prevents every response.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Learn what your effect restricts and what remains possible.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Review three control casts. Name the intended job and the action the target could still take. Note whether follow-up arrived during the useful window and one timing adjustment.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>Immunities, cleanses and spell-specific interactions matter. This lesson does not claim every channel or dash can be interrupted.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/skillshots\">Skillshots<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/summoner-spells\">Summoner spells<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-may-14/\">Riot: Champion Counterplay<\/a>. Primary design context for tactical responses and champion strengths and weaknesses. It is not a current ability reference.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Crowd control\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MI21\n- Slug: crowd-control\n- Category: Micro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Use movement and action restrictions for a clear follow-up.\n- Prerequisites: Skillshots.\n- Observable action: Identify the action you need to stop.\n\n## Short answer\n\nCrowd control is an effect that restricts an enemy's movement or actions. Different types restrict different things; a slow, root and stun are not interchangeable.\n\n## How it works\n\nChoose the effect for the job: interrupt an approach, hold a target for damage or give an ally time to escape. Coordinate multiple effects so useful control is not wasted, while allowing overlap when immediate certainty matters. Check which actions the target can still take.\n\n1. Identify the action you need to stop.\n2. Confirm your effect can stop that action.\n3. Use it when allies can exploit the result.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nAn enemy approaches your carry. You use a movement restriction to delay them, but keep moving because they may still cast. Your teammate uses the space to retreat rather than standing still to attack.\n\n<!-- Figure: MI21 -->\n![Decision diagram for crowd control: JOB — Stop movement or another action. EFFECT — Choose a matching control tool. FOLLOW — Allies act during the window.](../visuals/assets/mi21-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for crowd control; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Assuming any control effect prevents every response.\n\n**Correction:** Learn what your effect restricts and what remains possible.\n\n## Practice\n\nReview three control casts. Name the intended job and the action the target could still take. Note whether follow-up arrived during the useful window and one timing adjustment.\n\n## Limits\n\nImmunities, cleanses and spell-specific interactions matter. This lesson does not claim every channel or dash can be interrupted.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Skillshots](../micro/MI14-skillshots.md) · [Summoner spells](../micro/MI26-summoner-spells.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Riot: Champion Counterplay](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-may-14/). Primary design context for tactical responses and champion strengths and weaknesses. It is not a current ability reference.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "ceb257f114bffa5e66674d480c1fcc5323a238e4e671be23d63ad829037ce5d9"}, {"id": "MI22", "slug": "target-selection", "category": "Micro", "summary": "Choose a target you can affect without abandoning your job.", "title": "Target selection", "file": "MI22-target-selection.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Positioning, Cooldown tracking.", "action": "Name your current job in the fight."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Target selection means deciding which enemy to damage or control at a given moment. The best target depends on access, threat and team purpose, not only on how much damage that enemy deals.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Compare reachable targets with the cost of reaching others. A frontline enemy may be the useful damage target when walking past them would expose you. A diver threatening an ally may demand control even if another enemy is low. Reassess after movement or ability use changes access.<\/p><ol><li>Name your current job in the fight.<\/li><li>Compare reachable threats and the cost of crossing.<\/li><li>Switch targets when the job or safe access changes.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>An enemy carry stands behind two defenders. You damage the closest defender from safety. When a diver reaches your ally, you switch to that immediate threat rather than walking toward the distant carry.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/mi22-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/mi22-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Decision diagram for target selection: ROLE — Damage, protect or disrupt?. ACCESS — Which target can you affect?. SWITCH — New threat changes priority.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for target selection; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Chasing the highest-value enemy regardless of access.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Value safe, sustained contribution and protection as well as kills.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Pause three fights before your first attack. List two possible targets and the cost of reaching each. Watch when that comparison changes and whether you update your choice.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>Target value is situational. This lesson does not prescribe always hitting the closest enemy.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/positioning\">Positioning<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/cooldown-tracking\">Cooldown tracking<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/body-blocking\">Body blocking<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-may-14/\">Riot: Champion Counterplay<\/a>. Primary design context for tactical responses and champion strengths and weaknesses. It is not a current ability reference.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Target selection\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MI22\n- Slug: target-selection\n- Category: Micro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Choose a target you can affect without abandoning your job.\n- Prerequisites: Positioning, Cooldown tracking.\n- Observable action: Name your current job in the fight.\n\n## Short answer\n\nTarget selection means deciding which enemy to damage or control at a given moment. The best target depends on access, threat and team purpose, not only on how much damage that enemy deals.\n\n## How it works\n\nCompare reachable targets with the cost of reaching others. A frontline enemy may be the useful damage target when walking past them would expose you. A diver threatening an ally may demand control even if another enemy is low. Reassess after movement or ability use changes access.\n\n1. Name your current job in the fight.\n2. Compare reachable threats and the cost of crossing.\n3. Switch targets when the job or safe access changes.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nAn enemy carry stands behind two defenders. You damage the closest defender from safety. When a diver reaches your ally, you switch to that immediate threat rather than walking toward the distant carry.\n\n<!-- Figure: MI22 -->\n![Decision diagram for target selection: ROLE — Damage, protect or disrupt?. ACCESS — Which target can you affect?. SWITCH — New threat changes priority.](../visuals/assets/mi22-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for target selection; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Chasing the highest-value enemy regardless of access.\n\n**Correction:** Value safe, sustained contribution and protection as well as kills.\n\n## Practice\n\nPause three fights before your first attack. List two possible targets and the cost of reaching each. Watch when that comparison changes and whether you update your choice.\n\n## Limits\n\nTarget value is situational. This lesson does not prescribe always hitting the closest enemy.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Positioning](../micro/MI12-positioning.md) · [Cooldown tracking](../micro/MI15-cooldown-tracking.md) · [Body blocking](../micro/MI20-body-blocking.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Riot: Champion Counterplay](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-may-14/). Primary design context for tactical responses and champion strengths and weaknesses. It is not a current ability reference.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "db0d50b98609b391899eb4ca1c3ca17d1754f5672c72b247ca3e49f4394a2ad5"}, {"id": "MI23", "slug": "disengaging", "category": "Micro", "summary": "End contact before the fight becomes harder to leave.", "title": "Disengaging", "file": "MI23-disengaging.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Peeling, Engaging.", "action": "Signal the retreat and choose a safe direction."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Disengaging means creating enough separation to stop or avoid a fight. It can use movement, control, terrain or a defensive ability, with the aim of reaching a safer position together.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Choose an exit before spending your tools. A backward step can fail if it leads through another threat or leaves a slower ally behind. Sometimes a control spell should cover the retreat instead of starting a counterattack. Recheck whether the pursuers can still reach you.<\/p><ol><li>Signal the retreat and choose a safe direction.<\/li><li>Use a suitable tool to interrupt pursuit.<\/li><li>Leave together instead of restarting for one more hit.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>Your team loses its frontliner before a contest. You slow the pursuers and move toward a visible route with your carry. You give up the objective rather than turn alone to poke again.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/mi23-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/mi23-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Decision diagram for disengaging: SIGNAL — Choose a shared exit. COVER — Delay pursuit with a tool. LEAVE — Do not reopen the fight.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for disengaging; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Using the retreat tool and immediately re-entering range.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Treat separation as the objective until conditions change.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Review three retreat attempts. Mark the chosen exit, the first covering ability and the teammate most at risk. Identify the moment another attack delayed escape.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>Some fights cannot be escaped after commitment. Earlier recognition may matter more than a better final cast.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/peeling\">Peeling<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/engaging\">Engaging<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/flanking\">Flanking<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://dignitas.gg/articles/team-composition-guide-to-poke-compositions-in-league-of-legends\">soduh / Dignitas: Poke Compositions<\/a>. Original coaching context distinguishing poke from siege and explaining disengage (2021). Historical builds, champion numbers and matchup prescriptions are excluded.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Disengaging\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MI23\n- Slug: disengaging\n- Category: Micro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: End contact before the fight becomes harder to leave.\n- Prerequisites: Peeling, Engaging.\n- Observable action: Signal the retreat and choose a safe direction.\n\n## Short answer\n\nDisengaging means creating enough separation to stop or avoid a fight. It can use movement, control, terrain or a defensive ability, with the aim of reaching a safer position together.\n\n## How it works\n\nChoose an exit before spending your tools. A backward step can fail if it leads through another threat or leaves a slower ally behind. Sometimes a control spell should cover the retreat instead of starting a counterattack. Recheck whether the pursuers can still reach you.\n\n1. Signal the retreat and choose a safe direction.\n2. Use a suitable tool to interrupt pursuit.\n3. Leave together instead of restarting for one more hit.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYour team loses its frontliner before a contest. You slow the pursuers and move toward a visible route with your carry. You give up the objective rather than turn alone to poke again.\n\n<!-- Figure: MI23 -->\n![Decision diagram for disengaging: SIGNAL — Choose a shared exit. COVER — Delay pursuit with a tool. LEAVE — Do not reopen the fight.](../visuals/assets/mi23-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for disengaging; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Using the retreat tool and immediately re-entering range.\n\n**Correction:** Treat separation as the objective until conditions change.\n\n## Practice\n\nReview three retreat attempts. Mark the chosen exit, the first covering ability and the teammate most at risk. Identify the moment another attack delayed escape.\n\n## Limits\n\nSome fights cannot be escaped after commitment. Earlier recognition may matter more than a better final cast.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Peeling](../micro/MI11-peeling.md) · [Engaging](../micro/MI13-engaging.md) · [Flanking](../macro/MA18-flanking.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[soduh / Dignitas: Poke Compositions](https://dignitas.gg/articles/team-composition-guide-to-poke-compositions-in-league-of-legends). Original coaching context distinguishing poke from siege and explaining disengage (2021). Historical builds, champion numbers and matchup prescriptions are excluded.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "696526048953f0b98355d93838f818d56f7baf915c21e08fcdee1f764f5e737a"}, {"id": "MI24", "slug": "baiting", "category": "Micro", "summary": "Invite a commitment that your team is prepared to punish.", "title": "Baiting", "file": "MI24-baiting.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "All-ins, Map awareness.", "action": "Define the enemy action you want."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Baiting means presenting an opportunity to encourage an enemy action you intend to punish. The apparent opening might be a vulnerable target or an objective start, but the plan needs a real response when the enemy commits.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Check whether allies can act at the same time and whether you can survive the first response. The enemy may decline the invitation or bring more help than expected. Set a stop condition so waiting for the desired reaction does not become wasted time or a death.<\/p><ol><li>Define the enemy action you want.<\/li><li>Confirm the punishment and your survival plan.<\/li><li>Cancel if allies leave or extra enemies arrive.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>You stand within reach while an ally waits nearby. The opponent does not advance and your ally must return to lane. You leave too, rather than keep offering the same opening after the support is gone.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/mi24-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/mi24-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Decision diagram for baiting: INVITE — Offer an apparent opening. TRIGGER — Enemy commits; ally can act. CANCEL — Support gone or threat grows.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for baiting; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Assuming hidden allies make any exposed position safe.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Check actual distance, readiness and enemy follow-up.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Review one attempted bait. Write the intended trigger, available follow-up and cancellation condition before watching the result. Identify whether the enemy had a safer choice.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>The opponent controls whether they accept. A successful bait cannot be guaranteed from positioning alone.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/all-ins\">All-ins<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/map-awareness\">Map awareness<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/wave-management\">Wave management<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-may-14/\">Riot: Champion Counterplay<\/a>. Primary design context for tactical responses and champion strengths and weaknesses. It is not a current ability reference.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Baiting\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MI24\n- Slug: baiting\n- Category: Micro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Invite a commitment that your team is prepared to punish.\n- Prerequisites: All-ins, Map awareness.\n- Observable action: Define the enemy action you want.\n\n## Short answer\n\nBaiting means presenting an opportunity to encourage an enemy action you intend to punish. The apparent opening might be a vulnerable target or an objective start, but the plan needs a real response when the enemy commits.\n\n## How it works\n\nCheck whether allies can act at the same time and whether you can survive the first response. The enemy may decline the invitation or bring more help than expected. Set a stop condition so waiting for the desired reaction does not become wasted time or a death.\n\n1. Define the enemy action you want.\n2. Confirm the punishment and your survival plan.\n3. Cancel if allies leave or extra enemies arrive.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYou stand within reach while an ally waits nearby. The opponent does not advance and your ally must return to lane. You leave too, rather than keep offering the same opening after the support is gone.\n\n<!-- Figure: MI24 -->\n![Decision diagram for baiting: INVITE — Offer an apparent opening. TRIGGER — Enemy commits; ally can act. CANCEL — Support gone or threat grows.](../visuals/assets/mi24-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for baiting; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Assuming hidden allies make any exposed position safe.\n\n**Correction:** Check actual distance, readiness and enemy follow-up.\n\n## Practice\n\nReview one attempted bait. Write the intended trigger, available follow-up and cancellation condition before watching the result. Identify whether the enemy had a safer choice.\n\n## Limits\n\nThe opponent controls whether they accept. A successful bait cannot be guaranteed from positioning alone.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[All-ins](../micro/MI17-all-ins.md) · [Map awareness](../macro/MA39-map-awareness.md) · [Wave management](../macro/MA40-wave-management.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Riot: Champion Counterplay](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-may-14/). Primary design context for tactical responses and champion strengths and weaknesses. It is not a current ability reference.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "7f2a3bdcf4d28dc24a498ffb12c479a2780e8ebc3cb8138c8b61b9cad44bc12d"}, {"id": "MI25", "slug": "combos", "category": "Micro", "summary": "Sequence abilities and attacks around the effect you need.", "title": "Combos", "file": "MI25-combos.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Skillshots, Cooldown tracking, Resource management, Crowd control.", "action": "Choose a result and a simple sequence."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>A combo is a planned sequence of attacks and abilities intended to produce a result, such as reliable damage, control or escape. A useful sequence accounts for what the opponent can do between its parts.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Learn a simple sequence for your champion, then identify its purpose and failure points. Some sequences require a hit before the next cast, while others should stop when the target moves away. Preserve a defensive tool when spending it would make the result unsafe.<\/p><ol><li>Choose a result and a simple sequence.<\/li><li>Check each required hit or position.<\/li><li>Continue only while the next step remains useful.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>You plan to land control before a damage spell. The first cast misses, so you keep the second ability for wave clear instead of firing it automatically at an enemy who can now move freely.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/mi25-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/mi25-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Decision diagram for combos: SETUP — First action creates an opening. CONFIRM — Did the required effect land?. CONTINUE — Use next step or stop.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative decision sequence for combos; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Finishing a memorised sequence after its setup fails.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Treat each step as a decision with a visible condition.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Use practice mode to repeat a simple champion sequence five times. Then deliberately miss the setup and practise stopping. In a replay, compare one completed sequence with one you should have cancelled.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>Champion mechanics differ and can change. Verify current ability behavior in game; this is not a universal optimal combo.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/skillshots\">Skillshots<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/cooldown-tracking\">Cooldown tracking<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/resource-management\">Resource management<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/crowd-control\">Crowd control<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/trading\">Trading<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-may-14/\">Riot: Champion Counterplay<\/a>. Primary design context for tactical responses and champion strengths and weaknesses. It is not a current ability reference.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Combos\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MI25\n- Slug: combos\n- Category: Micro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Sequence abilities and attacks around the effect you need.\n- Prerequisites: Skillshots, Cooldown tracking, Resource management, Crowd control.\n- Observable action: Choose a result and a simple sequence.\n\n## Short answer\n\nA combo is a planned sequence of attacks and abilities intended to produce a result, such as reliable damage, control or escape. A useful sequence accounts for what the opponent can do between its parts.\n\n## How it works\n\nLearn a simple sequence for your champion, then identify its purpose and failure points. Some sequences require a hit before the next cast, while others should stop when the target moves away. Preserve a defensive tool when spending it would make the result unsafe.\n\n1. Choose a result and a simple sequence.\n2. Check each required hit or position.\n3. Continue only while the next step remains useful.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYou plan to land control before a damage spell. The first cast misses, so you keep the second ability for wave clear instead of firing it automatically at an enemy who can now move freely.\n\n<!-- Figure: MI25 -->\n![Decision diagram for combos: SETUP — First action creates an opening. CONFIRM — Did the required effect land?. CONTINUE — Use next step or stop.](../visuals/assets/mi25-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative decision sequence for combos; arrows show decisions, not measured game positions or timing.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Finishing a memorised sequence after its setup fails.\n\n**Correction:** Treat each step as a decision with a visible condition.\n\n## Practice\n\nUse practice mode to repeat a simple champion sequence five times. Then deliberately miss the setup and practise stopping. In a replay, compare one completed sequence with one you should have cancelled.\n\n## Limits\n\nChampion mechanics differ and can change. Verify current ability behavior in game; this is not a universal optimal combo.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Skillshots](../micro/MI14-skillshots.md) · [Cooldown tracking](../micro/MI15-cooldown-tracking.md) · [Resource management](../micro/MI16-resource-management.md) · [Crowd control](../micro/MI21-crowd-control.md) · [Trading](../micro/MI06-trading.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Riot: Champion Counterplay](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/dev/quick-gameplay-thoughts-may-14/). Primary design context for tactical responses and champion strengths and weaknesses. It is not a current ability reference.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026. The explanations, hypothetical scenarios, diagrams and exercises are editorial teaching guidance. Source context is not proof that a drill improves rank. No patch-specific numbers or independent expert review are claimed.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "f7a2ab99dd11a339cc9c96fb3ef4bd7753d627937cd74720b4d51f78192ea708"}, {"id": "MI26", "slug": "summoner-spells", "category": "Micro", "summary": "Plan a spell’s purpose and the options left after using it.", "title": "Summoner spells", "file": "MI26-summoner-spells.md", "meta": {"roles": "All roles; beginner to intermediate.", "skill_level": "", "prerequisites": "Cooldown tracking, Crowd control.", "action": "Read the effect, restrictions and availability of your own spell."}, "aliases": [], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Summoner spells are additional abilities selected for a match, separate from your champion’s own kit. Their availability can change whether an escape, chase or team action is feasible.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>How it works<\/h2><p>Read your selected spells in the current client and check your own displayed cooldowns. Give each spell a purpose before a dangerous action: reaching safety, supporting a commitment or serving your role’s task. Compare the expected benefit with what becomes harder while it is unavailable. Do not spend a spell only because you have already started chasing. Conversely, saving it until no useful escape remains can waste the opportunity it offered.<\/p><ol><li>Read the effect, restrictions and availability of your own spell.<\/li><li>Choose the problem it must solve and a useful destination or target.<\/li><li>After using it, adjust your position and next commitment to the missing option.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Hypothetical example<\/h2><p>Your movement spell is ready and an enemy approaches. In this hypothetical case, the spell can reach a safer position near allies. You use it before the escape closes, then play farther back while it is unavailable. Later, you decline a chase that would need that same escape. No particular wall crossing or crowd-control interaction is assumed.<\/p><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 760px)\" srcset=\"assets/mi26-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"assets/mi26-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Summoner spells decision diagram: PURPOSE — Choose what the spell must solve.. USE — Check conditions and destination.. ADAPT — Play around the option now spent.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/picture><figcaption><em>Illustrative choices for summoner spells; not measured game timing or positions.<\/em><\/figcaption><\/figure><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Common mistake and correction<\/h2><p><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Treating a ready spell as a guarantee of escape, or assuming an enemy’s spell is down without seeing it used.<\/p><p><strong>Correction:<\/strong> Check the specific interaction; record observed enemy use separately from estimated return time.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice<\/h2><p>Review three uses or missed chances to use one selected spell. Before seeing the outcome, write its intended purpose, the needed conditions and the next action you must avoid while it is unavailable. Rehearse one unfamiliar use in practice mode using current client descriptions.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Limits<\/h2><p>Spell availability, upgrades, restrictions and cooldown modifiers vary by mode and setup. This is not a spell loadout recommendation or a complete interaction reference. Enemy timing estimates retain uncertainty.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/cooldown-tracking\">Cooldown tracking<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/crowd-control\">Crowd control<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/combos\">Combos<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p><a href=\"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/how-to-play/\">Riot: How to Play<\/a> identifies summoner spells as additional champion tools. Specific effects and restrictions must be checked in the current client; the use-and-reassess routine is editorial guidance.<\/p><p>Checked 4 November 2026 (Pacific time). Examples and exercises are editorial teaching proposals, not validated rank-improvement methods.<\/p><\/section>", "search": "# Summoner spells\n\n- Stable lesson ID: MI26\n- Slug: summoner-spells\n- Category: Micro\n- Roles and level: All roles; beginner to intermediate.\n- Summary: Plan a spell’s purpose and the options left after using it.\n- Prerequisites: Cooldown tracking, Crowd control.\n- Observable action: Read the effect, restrictions and availability of your own spell.\n\n## Short answer\n\nSummoner spells are additional abilities selected for a match, separate from your champion’s own kit. Their availability can change whether an escape, chase or team action is feasible.\n\n## How it works\n\nRead your selected spells in the current client and check your own displayed cooldowns. Give each spell a purpose before a dangerous action: reaching safety, supporting a commitment or serving your role’s task. Compare the expected benefit with what becomes harder while it is unavailable. Do not spend a spell only because you have already started chasing. Conversely, saving it until no useful escape remains can waste the opportunity it offered.\n\n1. Read the effect, restrictions and availability of your own spell.\n2. Choose the problem it must solve and a useful destination or target.\n3. After using it, adjust your position and next commitment to the missing option.\n\n## Hypothetical example\n\nYour movement spell is ready and an enemy approaches. In this hypothetical case, the spell can reach a safer position near allies. You use it before the escape closes, then play farther back while it is unavailable. Later, you decline a chase that would need that same escape. No particular wall crossing or crowd-control interaction is assumed.\n\n<!-- Figure: MI26 -->\n![Summoner spells decision diagram: PURPOSE — Choose what the spell must solve.. USE — Check conditions and destination.. ADAPT — Play around the option now spent.](../visuals/assets/mi26-desktop.svg)\n\n*Illustrative choices for summoner spells; not measured game timing or positions.*\n\n## Common mistake and correction\n\n**Mistake:** Treating a ready spell as a guarantee of escape, or assuming an enemy’s spell is down without seeing it used.\n\n**Correction:** Check the specific interaction; record observed enemy use separately from estimated return time.\n\n## Practice\n\nReview three uses or missed chances to use one selected spell. Before seeing the outcome, write its intended purpose, the needed conditions and the next action you must avoid while it is unavailable. Rehearse one unfamiliar use in practice mode using current client descriptions.\n\n## Limits\n\nSpell availability, upgrades, restrictions and cooldown modifiers vary by mode and setup. This is not a spell loadout recommendation or a complete interaction reference. Enemy timing estimates retain uncertainty.\n\n## Related lessons\n\n[Cooldown tracking](../micro/MI15-cooldown-tracking.md) · [Crowd control](../micro/MI21-crowd-control.md) · [Combos](../micro/MI25-combos.md)\n\n## Sources and limits\n\n[Riot: How to Play](https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/how-to-play/) identifies summoner spells as additional champion tools. Specific effects and restrictions must be checked in the current client; the use-and-reassess routine is editorial guidance.\n\nChecked 4 November 2026 (Pacific time). Examples and exercises are editorial teaching proposals, not validated rank-improvement methods.\n", "minutes": 2, "sha256": "6f6c0de8a534414fb9a8e380f055034897d3b8ce1346a0aa989723f604e1f01a"}];
